      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>BREAKFAST-SUPPRIL</head>
        <p>BREAKFAST-SUPPRIL</p>
        <p>BOILING WATER OR MIL</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>SHE: "And will you be true to me ? " He: no' My dear, am I a prophet?" "PAPA, what will you give me when I marry?" !' My consent, Clara. Soms men who start out to rise in the world sand in the attic.</p>
        <p>THERE may be rich fools, but poverty is no par- ticular sign of ability. PE is not always refoym, anymore than noise js music.</p>
        <p>"WHY, this is the third time we've met, Baron 1 " " Yes ; this must be your lucky day!" DEBT is the only thing known that enlarges the more it is contracted.</p>
        <p>CARRIE : "Are you sure that May and Algy are engaged?" Amy: "Why, certainly. I heard them quarrelling last night." Idtsrourrsc seldom gathers friends, and when it does they all stand around and say, "I told you io ?:'</p>
        <p>MRs. 399: "What is Mrs. Gayly so angry about?" Mrs. Hanton : "Some one circulated a report that she had become religions."</p>
        <p>AN ETYMOLOGY. —Professor : "The author of the Iliad, was home-less at first, until seven cities claimed him, and then they called him Homer."</p>
        <p>LADY (engaging a cook) : "And one more ques- tion. . .'' Cook: 'Have I got a young man? Yes, I have "</p>
        <p>MOTHER: "Almost everyone thinks baby looks Just like me." Miss Oldrival : "Never mind, dear; he may outgrow it." A COUNTER QuEsuoN.—Stranger : "How much is it worth to draw up a will?' Lawyer: "How ranch are you worth ?" "How can there be such a thing as a whole day, you know,' mused Fweddy, "when it bweaks every itrawning ?-"- - A GIRL always likes to find a man after her own keart; because what is the good of a fellow who is after some other girl's heart? THE eagle is a very noble bird, but the average hen contributes more to the comfort of the human race. _</p>
        <p>THE man who publishes anonymously has a chance to be tickled by the unconscious praise of his personal enemies. MISS BEAUTI : "How do you like my new photo- graph? ' Little Girl : "It's perfectly lovely. Did you really sit for it yourself?" HE went to his pew, his boots creaking all the way. "Some people have too much music in their soles," said the clerunian —to himself. A MYSTERY.—Ada: "I can't imagine how that secret leaked out?" Ida: "Nor I. I'm sure every one to whom I told it promised to say nothing about it."</p>
        <p>Cluzsx "Is it your brother who has such a lovely falsetto voice!" Little Son of the house : " I don't know; but mother has a lovely false set o' teeth !"</p>
        <p>- MAMMA: "I hope you behaved like a little lady while Mrs. Hightone was trying to entertain you? Small Daughter: " Yes'm. I put my hand over my mouth every time I yawned." " MR.s. SAILHI is taking a great interest in church work now." Mrs. Frostly : "I didn't know that she had got her new sealskin yet." "So Brown married the pretty little girl he was in love with?" "Yes, but there are often days that he doesn't speak a word to her." "It is jealousy; I suppose he—" "No ; she does all the talking." A GREAT CITY. —Bridget (eagerly) : "Now you're back, tell me phwat yez saw in Noo Yark." Pat (gravely): " Th' ferry. boats ! Bedad, they've got a bow at th' both inds av thim so they kin go both ways at wanst." WHY HE ANNOYED HER. —"No," declared the Sultan, firmly, "1 cinnot love another." The Dowager Sultana was deeply. vexed. "Dear me," she exclaimed, "what shall we do? You have still one thousand five hundred wives less than the con. ventional number."</p>
        <p>WHY is a gun in the hands of a beginner like a fighting collier? Because it kicks more than it hits.</p>
        <p>VISIONS Or GLORY. - Mr. Binks (reading):</p>
        <p>"'Not a few great men have been Partial to eats.'" Mrs. Binks : "Isn't that glorious ! Our little Johnny will be a great man some day." "Eh ?" "I've often caught him tying tin things to dogs' tails, and you know dogs worry cats." DESDEMONA DAVIS (bursting into tears) : "It is too bad of him—he knows that I love him, and yet —and yet " Emilia Emerson: "Yet what?" Desdemona Davis: "He lets me flirt with anyone I wish to and doesn't say a word." THE WAY THEY SING "I AM A PILGRIM."— ale soprano sang in a high key, "I am a pil—" and then stopped. The alto repeated "I am a pil—" the tenor acknowledged he was "a pil—" and when the bass came thundering in with the like declaration, "I am a pil—" it was too much for the gravity of the singers. " Grim " was not in it.</p>
        <p>A REASONABLE SUGGESTION.—Mrs. Spratt (vigor- ously shaking her sleeping spouse) : " John ! John! There's a burglar in the house !" Mr. Spratt (pro- testingly : "See here, Manda ! If, instead of shak- • ing the life out of me, you'd go and shake that burglar, you'd be doing some good." THE RED MAN'S GRIEF. —The aged Indian chief wept. "Oh, these degenerate times !" he cried, with streaming eyes. "Oh, that I should live to see the day that my tribe refused to go on the warpath unless it was paved with asphalte, or at least macadamized !" Through blinding tears he gazed with ever-increasing bitterness upon his braves trying to break the mile record for pneu- matics. _</p>
        <p>GOODMAN: "Why do you always leave church before the sermon?" Smith: "Well, the fact is, I daren't stay. lam so subject to walking in my sleep." EQUAL TO TUE OCCASION. —Domestic: "Oh, please, mum, what shall I do? Half the soup is spilt, and the boarders is at the table." Mrs. Slimdiet (firmly) : "Empty the box of red pepper into what's left. Tnere will be enough then.'</p>
        <p>A causry old bachelor, not liking- the way his landlady s daughter hail of appropriating his hair- oil, filled his bottle with liquid glue the day before the ball to which the girl was invited. She stayed at home.</p>
        <p>WAITER (looking for a " tip ") : "I hope you'll remember me, sir." Mr. Hardup Masher: " Wheally, my good fellah, you waitahs are so much alike in attire and—ah—personal appeaw• ance, that I cannot undertake to do so."</p>
        <p>TEACHER (wishing to drive home the moral): "Now, children, why did the boy stand on the burning deck?" Johnny Thickneck : "Because it was too hot to sit down on, I guess." LIVING EVIDENCE.—Wife: "The cook says she never was with a family that lived any better than we do." Husband: "Yes. The policeman at the corner has gained twenty pounds since she came." THOUGHT HE WAS AT Hom E.—" Markham is wretchedly absent-minded." "What's he been doing now ? " "Went out to dine yesterday, and apologised prcfusely at the end of the dinner for the poorness of the spread." BERTHA: "Fred always speaks in such a low tone !" Laura: "Doesn't he? . . . and he often seems to me so curious! . . . do you know, I'm always afraid that he once proposed to me, and I didn't hear it." Arran THE HONEYMOON.—She: "Edgar, what ruin is that over there ? " He: "Don't know." she "Au, Edgar! you don't love me any longer! On our wedding tour you always knew everything."</p>
        <p>SMALL Boy: "Say, Sammy, whatoare you buy- ing a bouquet fort" "Why, Ise going to take it to Mies Prim, and I tell her a young gentleman sent it. She'll give me enough to pay for the bouquet three times over 1"</p>
        <p>RECORDER (to prisoner) : "How do you live ? " —Prisoner "I ain't particular, as the oyster said when they asked whether he'd be roasted or fried." Recorder: "We don't want to hear what the oyster said. What do you follow ? " Prisoner: "Anything that comes in my way, as the locomotive said when it ran over a man." Recorder: "We care nothing about the locomotive. What is your business ? " Prisoner: "That's various, as the cat said when she stole the chicken." Recorder : "That comes nearer to the line, I suppose?" Prisoner: "Altogether in my line, as the rope said when choking the pirate." Recorder: "If I hear any more absurd comparisons I will give you twelve months." Prisoner: "I'm done, as the beefsteak said to the cook."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>TRIND AND PROVED!</head>
        <p>TRIND AND PROVED!</p>
        <p>Sufferers from Gr vel. Lumbago, Piles, Pains in the Rack Driwev Wind a,-.1 Water Gowipteint Diseas s of Kidneys BI ot.te; -tone, c aura Rheumatism end Gout will flnoi pi) It{ ell'e iii Holroyd's Gravel Pills. Try a Fmall box and If niit satisfied your money will be returned. Prices is. lid. of all Chemists or post free for 12 stamps from HOLROTD'S Medical lfall. Cheekheaten. Yorks. Don't be put off. If you cannot get them writ,' .o the Proprietor and a box will be sent next pat.—Advt.</p>
