      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>JOTTINGS.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS.</p>
        <p>THOSE who would enjoy good eating should keep good-natured; an angry man can't tell whether he is eating boiled cabbage or stewed umbrella. A mOITSZ lately showed great presence of mind on falling into a dish of cream. It swam round and round violently, until it was able to crawl out on the butter. This was in America.</p>
        <p>DYSPEPSIA SPECIALIST (irritably) : "But, madam, you must chew your food. What were your teeth given you for ? " Female Patient (calmly) : "They weren't given to me, I bought 'em."</p>
        <p>HE: "Have you heard the news? Yesterday morning Mary Hobson jumped into her father's carriage and eloped with the coachman." • She : "What's her father done about it?" He : "He has advertised, 'Send back the horses and all will be forgiven.'" In a little country theatre an amateur theatrical performance was given in which the moon was obliged to appear several times. During one of the moonlight scenes a strong draught of air extin- guishod the lamp which was responsible for the moonlight, whereupon the hero exclaimed, audibly: "What does this mean?" "The wind has blown the moon out," responded the manager. "You look so much like your brother," said Dennis to Phelim, "that I could tell yez was brothers if I'd never seen aither av yea." IT is more blessed to give than to receive, and the fact that so many people are willing that others should have the blessing shows that people are not so selfish as some folks would have us believe. • OBEDIENT. —Mother : "Where were you during that thunderstorm ? " Boy : " Over in that field with the big tree in it." "But I have told you distinctly many, many times never to stand under a tree during a thunderstorm." "I didn't. I sat down." BISKS : "I understand young Briefless is about to mary the daughter of old Bonds, the millicn- sire." Jinks : " Yes, so I am told." Binks :</p>
        <p>"Will he give up the law business," Jinkis : "Yes, he will give up the law business and go into he son-in-law business." A PRISONER'S GRATITUDE. Counsel for the defence had pleaded with such earnest and pathetic eloquence on behalf of his client, who stood charged with robbery, that the audience was moved to tears, and the prisoner himself was mop- ping his eyes with a "wipe" of flowered silk. At that moment the barrister, happening to glance in his direction, suddenly stopped in his speech and exclaimed: "Why, tl-iA rascal is using my pocket. handkerchief !" NOT WHAT SHE EXPECTED.—" Can I see your father for a few moments before I go, Miss Simp- kins?" he asked. "I want to speak to him upon a, matter of importance." "Certainly, Mr. Samp- son,'' replied the girl, with a blush. After Mr. Sampson had taken his departure, with a happy smile on his face, Miss Simpkins found her way to her father's shoulder, and, stealing her one arm about his neck, whispered :—"What did he want, papa?" "He is an agent for a patient gas burner," said the old man, "and I've agreed to give it a trial."</p>
        <p>SPLENDID RESULT. —Doctor : "Do you find your- self stronger after taking the medicine?" Patient (in a faint voice) : " Oh, yes, doctor ; I feel quite strong enough to undertake a journey to the next world." A PENITENT TIIIEF.—Scene : A Police-court.— Counsel for the Defence: "My client has a claim on the indulgence of the court, for he only took ten shillings, without touching the pocket-book, whichacontained five thi: (sand pounds.' (Here the prisoner burst into tears.) Magistrate (affected) : "Then you are sorry ?" Prisoner: "Yes, for not seeing the pocket-book." HER ADMIRER: "Ah, thanks ! I've been listen- ing—dreaming over your charming playing from the other room." She : " Really ? But it was my little sister playing her scales !" HE: "I wonder why it's said to be a man, and not a woman, we see in the moon." She (with emphasis) : " Because he stays out at night." A Pious WlSH.—Speculator (ill in bed) : " I do hope I shan't die now that Credit shares have got up to 3O!" A CURIOUS FRAUD.—Quite recently, a Berlin</p>
        <p>auctioneer was brought before the magistrates and convicted on the charge of forcing up the prices at a mock auction by biddiag as a ventrilo- quist along with the other persons in the room. LABOURER: "I see you're painting the auld tree." Artist (angrily): " Yes. But don't bother me just now. Go and get on with your work." Labourer : " Weel, my wark is jest to chop it down, so you must hurry up with your paintin'."</p>
        <p>A DEFENSIVE LAW—Little Johnny: "Just hear that baby squall! I don't wonder that mens hates babies." Little Ethel: "Do they?" "Do they? Eagles carry off babies, don't they?" "Yes." "Well, there's a law against shooting eagles."</p>
        <p>AN absent-minded professor was sitting at his desk writing one evening, when one of his children entered. "What do you want? I can't be dis- turbed now." "I only want to say good-night." "Never mind now; to morrow morning will do as well." AN INNOCENT MISTAKE.—As regards the con- sumption of champagne in England, which does not seem to have in any way diminished owing to the increase of duty, Mr. Baring-Gould tells a very significant story. A gentleman recently applied to a large apple-grower in the West Country for a hogshead or two of his sparkling cider. The reply was that he had none available as in previous years, because a certain London firm had taken the whole year's "pounding." As the apple-farmer added that the firm would no doubt supply his former customer with cider, the gentleman wrote to them, and in due course received the following reply: "Sir,—We are not cider merchants. You have made some mistake. We are a firm of champagne-importing merchants from the celebrated vineyards of MM. So and-so. at So-and-so."</p>
        <p>_ THE SPINSTER'S ROMANCE -"Aunt Edith," said the happy young maiden, radiant in the exultant affection of her first engagemew, "were you never in love? A far away look came into Aunt Edith's grey eyes as she answered in low and tender tones: "Yes. Nellie. Onoe I was as happy in a true man's love as you are now. He wan as dear to me as the apple of my eye. He loved me truly, and he was my beau ideal of manhoo 1," "Did he die, auntie?" asked the girl,, softly. The spinster shook her bead, as she prooesded "Charlie and I would have been married,, and we should have been happy all the rest of our day's had not a serpent in the form of a jealous WOMAD come between us with a cruel untruth which separated us forever. "You poor, dear auntie," cooed the maiden, soothingly; and she smooched the wronged one's tresses with her soft little hands, " She— Ude serpent—came to me in the guise of friendship and told me that my fiance was not the wealthy man everybody believed 'him to be, and I—of csurse I had to break the engagement. I ooald</p>
        <p>not tell him the real reason, ion know, and he was too proud to ask why he veal dismissed. He. went his way, and we have been strangers ever since. "Wasn't it true?" "No, it wasn't). The serpent married him herself, and then I discovered that he was worth a million. MOBS HASTE, LESS SPEED.—It was 9 p.m. and Herr August Glimmermann stood ready equipped In travelling costume. Ho put on his gloves and his hat, snatched up his plaid, turned the gas off, and was about to leave the room preparatory to starting on a long journey through Germany and Italy, when, in the dark, his sleove swept over the table in consequence of which something dropped en the floor, which, to judge from the sound, must have been a coin. Although during his absence no one could enter the room, as he was in the habit of locking it and taking the key with him, yet he thought it better not to leave the money lying about, but secure it in his puree. He therefore relighted the gam, and found that the coin was only a copper of the value Of one penny sterling. Putting it in hls purse' he hurried out of the room and quickly looked the door, for it was just striking nine and his train left at 9.18, so that he had only jest time to catch it. Eight months later Glimmermann,on his return from Italy, unlocked the door of hid room and went in. Hal what is that The gas was burning merrily just as he left it, after lighting it to seek the dropped coin, he having forgotten to turn it off in his hurry, and a couple of days later he was presented with • gas bill to the amount of £1 ss. 30.</p>
