      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAREST NELLIE, Although it is too early yet for the modes for the coining winter to be very definitely settled, yet many of the best shops are making a large display of between-season gowns, wraps, and millinery, and it is not difficult to gain from these an inkling, if nothing more, of what Dame Fashion has in store for future use. As to materials, knotted tweeds, canvasses, and checked and shot woollens are likely to prevail—crepon retiring into obscurity for a time at any rate—while bright navy-blue, all shades of brown, and warm tones of purple will be among the most popular of colours, although green is holding its own fairly well. Skirts are still full, Ivo: there is less stiffening apparent in the new Ids than in those of last. summer (a very clesir- .‘ 7e mmlification when the difficulty of managing a ;t Illy-lined skirt on a wet muddy day is taken into consideration), but if there is not much that is novel in thi part of the gown, there are considerabla alterations in the fashion of bodices.</p>
        <p>Jackets are to be short and full in the basque, but for one jacket a dozen capes will be worn, unless I am greatly mistaken. The - latter are fairly long, reaching well below the waist,•and are very full. Box-cloth capes, lined with plaid silk, are much in favour for present wear, and many have the seams outlined with narrow bands of brown leather, which look smart, but add to the weight considerably. The new sealskin jackets have double-breasted fronts, with big tortoiseshell buttons and bishop sleeves;, but Inverness capes of seal, or full circular ones with deep cape collars of astrachan or chinchilla, are likely to be, more fashionable than 2oats. The sable collarette has made its appearance again, with an additional number of meaningless little tails, some specimens ',ring "adorned" with as many as a dozen at each end, arranged in two rows, one above the other. .3lzunk ties are rather less hackneyed than sable, and one of good quality may be obtained for a com- paratively.small sum. There is quite a new coat selling now which looks very stylish. The novelty oonsists in the front of the coat being deeply braided down the front after the style of a military acket. The sleeves are very full, and the basque -bort and full.</p>
        <p>I ought to have told you just now when speaking of capes, of a very stylish one made at that world- famed Parisian house—that of M. Worth. It has double godet flounces in red velvet, veiled with accordion pleatings of black chiffon, and there are incroyable revers, enriched with white net and lace appliqué and thick ruffle in pleated chiffon. The hat is in coloured plaited straw, encircled with a brim of black tulle or net drawn over wire, over which droops a deep flounce of black pleated lace, overlaid with two smaller ones in white lace. A huge bow of yellow faille, brocaded with roses, ornaments the front.</p>
        <p>As to hats, these seem bigger and more laden with trimming than ever, but wings and feathers have almost entirely replaced flowers, early autumn although it still is. Boat-shaped white or very pale grey felt hats, with a plain band of black or brown ribbon round the crown, and an eagle's feather (so-called) at the left side are smart-looking for everyday wear, while for more dressy occasions there is an immens3 variety in felts and beavers, with huge bows of fancy velvet and long ostrich feathers or groups of wings, plain or spangled, to choose from. A pretty type df hat has a wide brim of soft grey felt, a puffed " beef-eater " crown of lizard green velvet, and a triple plume of grey ostrich feathers at the the side, a band of grey ribbon-velvet with cut steel buckles at intervals encircling the crown. Ruches of shaded ribbon are very popular as trimmings for all kinds of felt and straw hats, blue and green shading being that specially affected, and the ruching very closely done. Cocks'-feathet ruching is also considerably in vogue as a trimming. For bonnets plaited che- nille and silk is largely used as a foundation, and the trimming is invariably arranged to give a wide effect above the forehead, wings and stiffly-wired bows of satin ribon being chiefly depended upon to produce the appear- ance aimed at. Here is an elaborate hat suited for most occasions. It is of soft black French felt, and boasts a rich ostrich tuft and handsome osprey of black and white, while fan bows in ribbon striped with white fill the sides and back, with lovely paste buckles in the centre of each.</p>
        <p>It may not be generally known that common table salt is an excellent dentifrice. If a damp soft cloth is rubbed in the salt, and the teeth rubbed ir the downward direction with it, they will become beautifully white, the breath sweet- ened, and the gums red in colour, healthy and firm. This simple remedy should be impressed upon quite young children, as neglect in early childhood lays the foundation of the bad teeth of man or womanhood, and yet quite little ones are unable to manage a tooth-brush, though there would be no difficulty in applying a soft cloth. Your loving JZMNIZ.</p>
        <p>IMPURITIES IN THE BLOOD.—" We are seen hosts of letter from people who have received great benefit from the use of Clarke's Blood Mixture. It cannot be too highly estimated, for it cleanses and clears the blood from all impurities.* This is a good testimonial from the Family Doctor. which goes on further to say : • It is the finest Blood Purifier that science and skill have brooght to light, and we can with the utmost confidence recommend it to our subscribers and the public generally." For Scrofula, Scurvy, Eczema., Bad Legs, Skin and Blood Diseases, Pimples and Sores of all kinds. Its effects are marvellous. Thousands of Testimonials. , Clarke's Blood Mixture is sold everywhere, at 2s. 9d. per , bottle. Beware of worthless imitations and substitutes.— Ada</p>
