      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>Girl formerly signified any young person of either sex. The services of nd less than 43 gods and goddesses were needed to raise a Roman child.</p>
        <p>Men attending the pans in salt-works are never known to have cholera, small-pox, scarlet lever, or influ- enza.</p>
        <p>Over two hundred thousand pounds worth of dia- monds are stolen every year from the South African diamond mines.</p>
        <p>If the wife of a Turkoman asks his permission to go out, and he says " Go," without adding " come back again," they are divorced. It is said that moths will not attack green fabrics. Arsegic is used in dying green, and the moths are wise enough to shun that deadly drug.</p>
        <p>The combined wealth of the Vanderbilt family is £52,800,000.aud the estimated lucerne from it per annum is £2,772,800. No other family l 8 5Q rich. The common housefly is often literally devoured by parasites and it has been proved that these parasites are also infested with minute creatures thst threaten their destruction.</p>
        <p>" Familiarity breeds contempt," is a proverb found in one form or another in every Europr an and Asiatic lan- guage having a literature. Its earliest form is believed to be the Sanskrit.</p>
        <p>Paper, indestructible by fire, has been invented by M. Meyer, of Paris. A specimen of it, which was subjected to a severe test-148 hours in a potter's furaace—came out with its glaze almost perfect. A French method of cleaning kid gloves is to put ea the gloves and wash them in spirits of turpentine,exactly as if washing the hands. Rub well and then stretch them and let them dry, hangiag in a strong current of air.</p>
        <p>Scientists say that people who drink water containing a large percentage of lime, like the Kentuckians and Tennesseens, are famous for being tall. If this is so, Londoners should excel. The river Thames contains twenty-two per cent. of Hine ; Liverpool water ouly five per cent.</p>
        <p>One of he cleverest inventions over pasied by a patent office is the machine for sticking common pins in the papers in which they are sold. The contrivance brings the pins in rows, draws the paper in position, crimps it in two lines, then at a single push passes the pins through the paper and sets them in position. Court plaster should he used with care. If the injury be very slight the method of application is unimport- ant; but if at all deep, never use the plaster to cover the cut. Simply use long narrow strips and apply them across the cut to bring its edges together. Then the secretions of the wound can escape. Never apply court plaster to a bruised wound.</p>
        <p>Some Uses for Hot Water.</p>
        <p>Hot water is at more of a medical property than many believe or know. Because it is to be had for the making, thousands think it valueless, on the theory that what comes easiest is oftentimes least thought of. The uses of hot water are, however, many. For example, there is nothing that so pronoply cuts short congestion of the lungs, sore throat, or rheumatism, as hot water when applied promptly and thoroughly. Headache almost always yields to the simultaneous application of hot water to the feet and back of the neck. A towel folded several times and dipped in hot water, and quickly wrung out and applied over the toothache or neuralgia, will generally afford prompt relief. A strip of flannel, or napkin, folded lengthwise and dipped in hot water and wrung out, and then applied around tho neck of a child that has the croup, will sometimes bring relief in ten minutes. Hot water taken freely half an hour before bedtime is helpful in the case of constipa- tion, while it has a most soothing effect upon the stomach and bowels. A goblet of hot water taken just after rising, before breakfast, has cured thousands of indigert.on,and no simple remedy is more widely recom- mended by physicians to dyspeptics.</p>
        <p>Danger From Handkerchiefs. Paople are apt to ignore the most palpable and simple Precautions that should be takenjo hygienically cenduct life. If this were not so there would be shown more care in the use of handkerchiefs, and in their cleansing. Especially should this be the case in families of whom any member is troubled with a cold, or with influenza. Onn'pers in with a catarrhal affection may impart the , troubfelo an entire household. This fact should make it common practice to isolate the handkerchiefs of an individual who is affected by "influenza." The hand- kerchiets used by such a person, too, should be treated in the following manner :—They should be placed under water into.which a quantity of kerosene oil has been poured, and there renialn for say two or three days : theh the water is to be heated by pouring in boiling water,and when this is cool enough they may be washed, soap being used, of course. Another washing in oil and soap makes the disinfection sure, and completely re- moves all stain and effects of nasal appropriation. Then rinse the handkerchiefs carefully in warm water, and, if ! possible, hang uoon a line to dry in the open air. Let them remain out on the line over night. When hand- kerchiefs are treated in this manner, diseased matter is robbed of its danger, a fabric of delicate character spared the sacrifice occasioned by hard rubbing and washboard penalty, and the luxury of a soft, clean and white appliance may be had for the suffering nose, which is liable to be for a time very sensitive from effects of " blowing and excoriation." If the best quality of kerosene oil is used, the handkerchiefs are freely rinsed after oil and soapy water has cleansed and disinfected them, and there will be no odour of kerosene discover- able after in the neatly folded and ironed handker- chief.</p>
        <p>The Bicycle Revolutionises Trade. A humorous paper the other day painted the picture of a child of the next century being shown at the British /dug, urn the skeleton of an extinct animal, to wit the horse, which his grandmother rode in the nineteenth century, when bicycles were only just coming into vogue. It remains for time to prove how much truth there is in this jest. Certainly, as long as man remains civilised, the horse will never become extinct,but it does appear that many other things are being seriously aff,-cted by the encroachment of cycling. The list of industries whose degeneration is attributed to the bicycle craze is steadily growing. Pianoforte dealers say that their sales are greatly reduced since young people took to the popular outdoor exercise. Children who were formerly compelled to give a number of hours daily to piano practice are now allowed to spent more time in the open air, and this tendi-ncy has been greatly encouraged by the warnings given by the medical profession against the ill effects which are becoming too common, of too close application to study. Young married couples,who would at one time have devoted their savings to the purchase of a piano, now invest in bicycles, and go on summer trips instead oZ discoursing music. Jewellers complain that loicvele riding has greatly impaired the watch business. When 4 boy became 15 or 16 years old, it used to be the custom for his parents to present him with a watch—often a costly affair—which might last him the better part of his life. Now the boy clamours for a hi7ele, and he generally gets it, although it does not last as long, and generally costs more than a watch. The same thing happens with grown-up folks. When they get enough money in hand to buy a watch, the bicycle fever strikes them, and the money is spent on a " wheel."</p>
        <p>TUE TESTIMONIALS Publkhed on behalf of Hood's Sarsaparilla are as reliable and as worthy your confidence as if they came from your most trusted neighbour. They will stand the closest investigation. They are not purchased, nor are they from our employes, nor are they written up in our office. They are reliable statements from living people, end they prove be- yond a question the POSITIVE MERIT</p>
        <p>of this great medicine. Try it. "For some time my little boy, three years old, had been troubled with eczema. Since taking Hood's z'arsaparilla he has improved wonderfully. The complaint affected his eyes severely, but now they are entirely well. Be has also gained a splendid appetite. I trust this will encourage others suffer- ing similarly to give Hood's Sarsaparilla a trial." MRS. ANNIE Ezzrmarr, 14, Swahili-road, Beckenham, S.E. A BLOOD CLEANSER.</p>
        <p>"Messrs. C. I. Hood &amp; Co., "34, Snow Hill, London, B.C. : "I bought a bottle of Hood's Saraaparilla for my son. He had pimples on his neck and body, and lam pleased to inf4rm you that one bottle hag taken them all away. He seams all right now, and if at any time he or any of my family requires anything for cleansing the blood, I shall send for some more of Hood's Sarsaparilla at once. I can confidently recommend Hood's Sarsaparilla as a blood cleanser." PETER LOCKWOOD, Market-street, Paddock, Huddersfield.</p>
