      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>GLENALPINE'S OATH;</head>
        <p>GLENALPINE'S OATH;</p>
        <p>OR, THE MARK OF THE BLACK CROSS. BT SYLVANUE3 ()OBE, Ja.</p>
        <p>CHAPTER XVI.</p>
        <p>THE STORY OP GLRNALPINE'S OATH.</p>
        <p>Late in the day Peter Brun, with sad and down. cast look, stepped from the lower veranda of Blrchmear House, and strolled away towards the garden. He saw Harold Berwick approaching by the main walk, and he turned off to avoid the meeting. " Woe Is me !" he muttered, as he moved slowly on. "Why does fortune play such unseemly tricks? Only a few short hours ago I thought full sure that the Sun of Joy had risen indeed upon Birchmear ; and now it is darker than ever! Arthur is heart- broken; Sir John is miserable; and the hidden foe is surely making ready for some new deknon- stration. o!if I could only look into Mr. Harold's band I He and his evil-eyed mother are playing a desperate game. lam sure of it.—and yet I know not how to circumvent them. There's mischief in Harold's look. I marked it as he passed me but now. His face was as dark and—"</p>
        <p>Peter's soliloquy was cut short by an approach- ing footfall, and upon turning he beheld the weird, gaunt form and the spectral face of Sir Philip Glenalpine. The strange man wore the same wild garb be had worn in England—a vest and hunting-shirt of quaintly-trimmed buckskin, and stout leathern hose, and upon his head the Scotch bonnet with an eagle's feather at the side. " Peter Brun, be not afraid of me. Dost know me ?"</p>
        <p>Peter was awe-stricken, but he had no fear. "I have heard that thou art Sir Philip Glenal- pine." "Thou haat heard aright, Peter." The wanderer paused a moment, and then, in an easier tone, he added: _</p>
        <p>"I am but a poor waif, seeking to perform a sacred duty before this earthly race is for ever run. I have a debt to pay, Peter; and I am seeking my creditor. I think, in England, not many months since, the simple changing of a name threw me from the true track. But that cannot Interest you." Had Peter dared to speak the truth he would have confessed that the matter interested him deeply, working through the great curiosity he had to learn the strange secret of which he had heard BO much mysterious whispering. "By the way." pursued Glenalpine, after an- other pause, "I saw a man pass near here a few moments since. Did you observe him?" "I saw Mr. Harold, sir."</p>
        <p>"What is his other name ?" "Berwick,—Mr. Harold Berwick." "Is he Lord Birchmear's son?" "His step-son, sir." "Then is he not of Berwick's blood V"</p>
        <p>"Not a bit nor a drop of it ?" declared Peter, with energy. "He's not a born Berwick, thank God ! "</p>
        <p>Glenalpine seemed as deeply moved as did the old keeper; but he sought to speak calmly. "Can you tell me what was his father's name I"</p>
        <p>" His father," replied Peter, readily, " was a good-for-nothing, spendthrift baronet, named Theobald Travers, who died in this very India where we now are." "And in India Sir John Berwick married the widow ?" " Yes, sir." " And this Harold Travers, her son, was then with her ?" "I think so, sir." "Had he a commission in the army at the time ?" "Yes. He was a captain." "One question more, Peter: Do you know if he was ever stationed in Ireland 1"</p>
        <p>"Y, sir. I have heard him, when sitting over his wine, tellipg military officers of his wild scrapes while stationed with his regiment in Dublin."</p>
        <p>"Good, Peter, I thank you l" With this the str4nge man caught the old keeper's hand, and wrung it warmly, and then, without another turned and hurried away, leaving pour Peter Brun in a deeper maze than ever.</p>
        <p>Glenalpine strode rapidly from the garden, and not until he Ii d gained the street did he slacken his pace. At length his step faltered, and once or twice ho stagger.ai as thougo hi, limbs were weak. By and by he fairly ar.oppe", and leaned against the post of a gateway for support. "I must have food before I go further 1" he said to himself ; but he spoke aloud, and in evident pain. •‘ I must have fasted for more than four• and-twenty hours ! Yes—l have not tasted food since the noon of yesterday. That man's face has. driven all other— " Who talks of Wanting food here ?'• demanded a bluff, frank voice ; and at the same time Glenal- pine felt a hand upon his shoulder. lie looked up, and beheld one whom he had known in the other years, and whose face he has not forgotten. "Jasper Wallace I" "Gleualpine I Is it your own self? Is it Sir Phillip?" "You behold what is left of him, Sir Jasper." And something like a smile broke over the tkickly bearded face. "And is it you talk of wanting food? You—Sir Philli p—" Glenalpine put up his hand to interrupt the speaker. "I have fasted, Sir Jasper; but not from neces- sity. I have been so deeply occupied in another' direction that I forgot my bodily needs, and now my weakness admonishes me.of the neglect." ' Well ! I am glad you felt the admonition at. my._gate. Come .with . me. Sir Phillip, and .y9u, shall break your fast after your own desire. You and I will open a bottle of wine together." "And together we'll empty it 1' tried the wan- derer. And as he spoke he took Sir Jasper's prof- fered arm, but hardly had he done so when he moved back, as though something had startled him.</p>
        <p>'9ll</p>
        <p>"How now, Sir Philip ?" "I think my brain is turned," Glenalpine whis- pered. "Did I not speak a happy, social word—a word of companionable repartee ?' " Aye like your old self, you did." " Atiff I smiled f" " Plainly Sir Philip." "Alas I I have not smiled before since the lines of silver that crept into my hair." " Which shows plainly that you have needed friendship,7my good fellow. But cmfie,—my arm again. You shall say tbat my wine bath a healthy flavour and a heslthgiving power." Glenalpine once more took the old soldier's arm, and together they entered the house, and were ere long. seated ate table upon which were served re- frelhmeuts inviting and substantial. By and by the ice was fairly broken, and the wanderer was led to talk of old times and old places. Many a scene of the other ears was lived over again, and many a long-forgotten friend was brought back to mind. At length Sir Jasper ventured upon the topic he had nearest his heart. He was determined, if possible, to learn the secret of Philip Glenalpine's later life. He had heard many whispers and various surmises, chief among which had been the proposition of insanity, brought on by the loss of a beloved wife. But Wallace detected no signs of lunacy. There was evidence of a spirit unbalanced —of an intellect misdirected—but no token of a mind beclouded. By slow degree Sir Jasper approached the point, and finally he broached it outright. " My dear Sir Philip, it you can trust an old friend—if you can believe that the depository of my confidence may be as sacred as the gra.% e—if you would feel relieved to share your secret with a brother—if you would confide in one who will assist you if he can know the need—then I ask you tell me the story of the strange life you have led during these later years. Glenalpine reflected a little time, and then reached over the table and took his host's hand. "Sir Jasper," he said, "I will tell you. It may be that ere long there will arise a need that the truth should be known, and you, if you know it, can tell it. I shall be brief—very brief—for I can- not dwell upon the theme. Ask me no questions; Interrupt me not; but listen." He drank part of a glass of wine, and presently proceeded s "Fifteen years ago I lived in Dublin, or, I should say, upon the estate in the suburbs of that city which had become mine through my. wife. You saw my wife in London? Hush I Don't speak 1 I know you saw her; and you must have seen that she was beautiful. She had two sisters, younger than herself, who lived with us in Dublin. They were bright, joyous creatures. upon whom the warm sunshine of life lingered most lovingly. We were a happy family. - "At that time McGovran's reziment was stationed</p>
        <p>of I</p>
        <p>In Dublin, and the officers wire freely invited to my house. They came—they drank my wine— they ranged through my preserves—they used my coney—and they were made welcome. "Sir Jasper, to this day of festivity there came a dark, tempestuous night. I woke in the bleak ...rornitrg of sorrow to find my home of peace and j,ay forever laid in ruin. A wrong had been done to me such as the very demons of the nether world might have shrunk from. In one word, my household idols had been shattered and my sanctuary made desolate. The blow so far pros. trated me that for a month I lay very near to the valley of shadows. But I recovered my strength in time, and stood in the midst of life as a bleak and shattered tower in the midst of the ruined house. Without difficulty I learned the names of those who had wrought this worse than fiendish work.</p>
        <p>"There were five of them—all officers in McGowan's Regiment. I made sure there could be no mistake, for a terrible purpose had possessed me. I took from my cabinet this little book." At this point Glenalpine drew from his bosom a small leather bag or case, and took therefrom a thin book, the covers of which were of red parch. went.</p>
        <p>"And here I wrote the names of those five men. I wrote but one upon a single page, and I wrote them with an ink in which were drops of my own olood. And when I had written I kneeled down upon a newly-made grave and swore that I would not rest—that I would not swerve from my pur- pose until I had slain those five demons. It was a terrible oath, but I felt stronger and better when I Sad made it. Now there was a purpose in my life beneath the dread import of which the great agony Df my soul took a secondary place.</p>
        <p>"Within a week from that time I met one of those men in a public street of Dublin. I told him his doom and my dagger found his heart. And then I opened my book, and upon the page bearing his name I made a black cross—a cross so black and so broad that it drank up and obliterated the name forever. See, here !" And Glenalpine opened the book and showed the cross, extending its mil- night arms from side to side and from top to bottom of the page. "Two of those men I found and slew in Dublin. The others had taken the alarm and fled. Another I traced to Edinburgh. He acknowledged his crime before I inflicted vengence upon him. The fourth I followed into France and thence back into England. I found him at length in York. I knew him well, and I swept him from the world of the living. As I had been once before, so was I again arrested by the officers of the law. Friends claimed that I was insane, and the Lord Keeper of the Great Seal issued a commission in my behalf. But I did not suffer him to he deeeived. lA4 '49 the Chancellor myself and told him the whole truth, and he did not give me back to the criminal courts; though for a time I was restrained in friendly durance. "And now, Sir Jasper, there remained but one name upon my book—my oath required but one more life for its fulfillment. The owner of that name had gone to India with his regiment. Thither I went. 1 followed him to Delhi, but missed him. I returned to Calcutta, and learned that he had gone back to England. Back to England I dragged my weary body, and missed him again. I had, after all this time, almost relented from the final fulfillment of my dreadful oath. So far had I wavered that I had thought, if I found the man grown to be good and true, and turned from his evil life, that I might spare him. But—" "Have you found him, then?" asked Wallace eagerly. "1 hare found him !" "And who is he ? " "Still a villian ! still pointing the ruin of others, even as he brought ruin upon me and mine!" " Sir Philip I' "Hush 2 Ask me not a question. There is one more page in my book—and upon that page a name. The Marl of the Black Cross must be made there- on L Then—then, Sir Jasper, my work will have been done, and I can lie down to sleep. You shudder. Do you blame me?" "No, Sir Philip—l do not, I cannot blame you!" The old soldier spoke with deep emotion, and his face and his eyes, glowed with unwonted fire. "For such a crime the perpetrators deserve to die. Within the ken of law there is not a cause more just than the extermination of villains so base. And you, as the embodiment of the great suffering, in the absence of legal power to punish, have the sole right of vengeance." Gleualpine arose and grasped Sir Jasper's hand. Therta wes an abnormal fire in his deeply sunken eyes, and the expression upon his whole face, gleaming out in spas of the thick beard, was pre- ternatural and startling. "Sir Philip, you are not going?' "I am strong now, Wallace.' "But the shades of night are gathering, and the streets are dark. This roof may be your cover."</p>
        <p>"Not yet, not yet, Sir Jasper. A night lost in repose may be to me the loss of the purpose of years. In my eager searchings I have' unearthed a plot wnich I did not at first dream could lie so near to my own purpose. In fulfilling my oath I may secure blessing to those who deserve it. A double-dyed villain is abroad, and I must not lose his track. Farewell, my friend. You have heard my story. Should the tongue of denunciation and impeachment ever take up the name of Gleaalpine, you can tell the truth." And with this the avenging wanderer turned and strode from the apartment, and from the house. Sir Jasper had no will to stop him—no will but to stand in speechless wonder, and watch him as he departed. (To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>AN EASY INTERVIEW.</head>
        <p>AN EASY INTERVIEW.</p>
        <p>The way of the interviewer—like that of the trans- gressor—is oftentimes hard, but this was not the ex- perience of the writer, who interviewed Mr. S. Cross, of 44, Moor Crescent, Hunslet, Leeds. In fact, Mr. Cross seemed willing, even eager, to relate his experience, which we give below in his own words :—" I feel it my duty to let the public know what benefit I have received from taking Warner's Safe Cure and Pills. For years I suffered from a sluggish liver. I had a very severe attack of Jaundice some years ago and have never been free for long together since. In the Spring of 1892 I had a very severe attack of Jaundice and passing Gall- stones. Then again in September, 1893, had Jaundice, passing Gall-stones, which caused excruciating pain. After getting little or no relief I was advised to try a Physician. I consulted two of the best in Leeds, was some little better for a time, and then consulted an Herbalist, and took medicine till I was sick at the sight of it. I used to have an attack of Jaundice or passing Gall-stones every nine or ten days, till I was very much reduced in weight, and my friends began to •say ay end was near, and that I should do very little more work. I had another very severe attack in July, 1894. My wife then asked me to try Warner's Safe Cure, and I said ' I will try anything.' Now lam most happy to say that from that time I have been a different man. I commenced to take six doses a day and a pill to keep my bowels regular; and the first fortnight I gained albs., in 18 weeks I gained 151b5., and have never had the least sign of an attack since. I can eat any kind of food and enjoy it, and that is not what I had done for years; had always to be very careful ss to food. For reference as to the truth of my statement, apply to Mr. Cross,43, Raymouth-road, Rotherhithe, Loudon, S.E. ; to Mr. Cross, 118, St. Jame Street, Brighton ;or to any of my numerous friends in Leeds."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>TO SIBERIA.</head>
        <p>TO SIBERIA.</p>
        <p>On one occasion,during a parade of his Horse Guards, the Emperor Paul ot Russia was extremely dissatisfied with the manner in which the troops performed their evolutions. At length after a more than ordinarily stupid blunder on the part of the troops, the Czar could stand it no langer,and he determined to preside over the drill in person. The troops were well aware that the Czar's temper was on the verge of bubbling over ; and the knowledge so unnerved them that things went from bad to worse, until at last a blunder supervened (a blunder in which officers and men shzred alike) which proved the climax. Galloping up to the disorganised lines and reining up his charger at their head,livid with the fury which he 130 longer attempted to suppress,Paul gave veut to the following original and effective speech : ! —" Officers and troopers of the Imperial Horse Guards. ' Hight about face! Quick—march—to Siberia !" The I entire regiment, with unbroken composure, and dignity, wheeled to the right,and started off then and there upon their terrible march into exile. By the time they had arrived at a point lying some few days' march from the capital, the Czar's temper having cooled down, swift , courtiers were despatched after the exiled Guards with news of the Imperial clemency, and the troops were al- lowed to return.</p>
