        <p>Party divisions happily do not interfere with private friendships and social intercourse in Eng- land. Of this there is interesting proof in the announcement that Mr. and Mrs. Asquith are leaving The Glen, Peeblesshire, to visit Mr. A. J. Balfour, at Whittingbame. But then Mr. Asquith, his wife, and Mr. Balfour each knew in other days the sacred intimacy of the " Souls." After the stay with Mr. Balfour, Mr. and Mrs. Asquith will go on to Dalmeny and be the guests of Lord Rosebery. There is also another instance of the friendliness of political opponents in the statement that the Duke and Duchess of Devonshire are among the house party at Guisachan, the Highland home of Lord and Lady Tweedmouth. The Duke has had some excellent deer•stalking.</p>
