      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>STRANGE HISTORY OF A SCRAP OF PAPER.</head>
        <p>STRANGE HISTORY OF A SCRAP OF PAPER.</p>
        <p>How a scrap of paper saved a man's life and a woman's happiness is told in the following authentic report abridged from a much longer narrative of the facts which appeared in the "Sussex Advertiser" a week ago It must be understood that this is no fictitious romance. but a chain of undoubted facts, verified and confirmed by personal evidence of the accredited representative of a respectable and able newspaper. Mr. Barber, of Ringmer, near Lewes, is the hero, and his wife the heroine, of this affair, which had its orgin in a grave misadventure experienced by Mr. Barber in Sep- ber, 1894 , when he had a stroke of paralysis. A local doctor and consulting physicians were sent for, who pro- nounced it a hopeless case. "Mr. Barber," said the doctor, "to be frank with you, Idon't think you will ever be well again, even though you may be able to walk with sticks." Mr. Barber describes his sufferings as having been truly excruciating. "When," said he to the reporter, "they dressed my poor leg it was like being crucified. It was worse than death. At that time the id doctor that visited me said, 'There is no use denying it; you are really suffering from paralysis, and that in its worst form.' I was often in agony. The suffering has been truly unspeakable." Thus, in an agony that made life worthless, and with certain death as his only prospect, the patient lay in despair. Yet a scrap of paper saved him. In his own simple and intelligent words, here is the story. "My wife happened one day to glance at a small piece of newspaper in which something was wrapped, and there read an account of a miraculous cure brought about by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. We talked the matter over and she persuaded me to try them. Dr. Williams advised cold water baths, as described in his, directions for use, at the same time using the Pills. They did me a wonderful deal of good, though one of my doctors said that the treatment would kill me. After I had been taking the Pills for some days my strength began to return to me. Before, I was as weak as a little child and could not lift the lightest object. I then began to be more hopeful. The low spirits which I was in seemed to pass away, and I began to feel another man. It was not long before I was able to leave my bed. My doctor ceuld not help noticing my changed condition. " Why, Mr. Barber," he said, "you look decidedly thet- ter." " Yes," I replied, " I am thankful to say that I am." " What have you been doing 2" be asked. I told him. His retort was, " I wonder it didn't kill you?' He had previously told me mine was a hospital ease, and now, having examined me, he said," Whoever this Dr. Williams is, he is a wonderful man. I couldn't decide what the case was, and he has cured you?' I had, pre- viously to having the Z'ills taken medicine time after time for indigestion, but only Dr. Williams! Pink Pills ever moved it. I was soon able to walk across the room by the aid of two sticks. My strength came gradually, and as I gat stronger I was able to walk about without the aid of sticks, and the villagers were astounded when I took my first walk after my serious illness. They never thought that I would get about again. Now I can get up at four o'clock in the morning and attend Brighton Market, a distance of about eleven miles, three times a week. Mine is a wonderful case.' I have been examined since my complete cure by more than one medical man, and they each agree that my cure is little short of a mir- acle."</p>
        <p>secon,</p>
        <p>Dr.Williams' Pink Pills have cured many cases of pare lysis, loecmotor ataxy, rheusiatism,neuralgia, St. Vitus' dance, nervous headache, and prostration; diseases ,of the blood, such as scrofula, chronic erysipelas, and re- store pale and sallow complexions to the glow of They are a specific for all the troubles peculiar to the female sex, and in men curing all cases arising from worry, overwork, or excesses. Sold by all chemists,and by Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Flolborn-via- duct, London,at 2s. 9d. a box, or six for 13s. 9d. Genuine only with fril name, Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Ea People.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</head>
        <p>THE WARRINGTON VOLUNTEERS.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL PRIZE MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual shooting competitions in connection with the First Volunteer Battalion, Prince of Wales's Volun- teers (South Lancashire Regiment), took place at Alicar Range on Saturday, September 28th, and Saturday, October sth. The shooting committee consisted of Cap- tain Silverthorne (adjutant), Captain J. D. Fairclough, Lieutenant B. Fairclough, and a sub-committee consist- ing of all colour-sergeants, and one other representative of each company. The donors of prizes were—Lord Lii- ford, Lieutenant-Colonel Ridgway, Captain Withington, Major Pickmere, the Mayor, Surgeon-Major Lister, "Attention," Mesdames Beamont, Stanton, R. T. Fair- dough, and Sharpe, Miss Milner, Messrs. Peter Stubbs, T. Fletcher, J. Rhoades, Buckton, Edward Greenall.John Fairclough, J. M. Edelsten, F. W. Monks, John White, Henry Milling and Company, T. Stanley Carter, T. Mor- ris, and R. Davies. The results were as follows :</p>
      </div>
