      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895. NOTES ON OAKES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895. NOTES ON OAKES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>OPENING OF A NEW CATHOLIC CHURCH AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The new Roman Catholic Church of the Sacred Heart, Bank Quay, was cpened on Sunday, by the Right Rev. Bishop Whiteside, D.D., of Liverpool,and the Right Rev. Bishop Bilsborrow, D.D., of Salford, assisted by a large number of clergy. The church has been built by Mr. Wionard, of Wigan, from the designs of Messrs. Sinnot, Sinnot, and Powell, of Liverpoel. The old English gothic style of architecture has been followed, and the building is composed on the outside of red brick, the windows being edged round with sand stone to give re- lief. Other parts of the structure havOalso been re- lieved with sandstone. It provides sitting accommoda- tion for 650 worshippers, and is situated lathe middle of a piece of lan 4,250 square yards in extent. Ample space is, therefore, left for future extensions, as the church is but 120 feet in length and 70 feet in breadth. Facing the roadway is a massive towcr 114 feet high from the level of the road. Let into that side of the tower fronting the road is a hugh tracery window, aud beneath this stands a commodious porchway. Access to the church is gained by two doors leading from the porchway, the floor at this portion of the building being laid with artistic tiles. The space in the interior of the church has been well utilized, due regard having been paid to the convenience and comfort of worshippers. 'There are two side aisles, the width of each being twelve feet. Bordering each aisle are three massive stone pillars, which have been artistically carved at the tops by Mr. Essenhigh. From the aisles four arches spring, on either side, into the nave, which in .width measures 22 feet. The height from the nave to the inner ceiling is 33 feet. Coming to the sanctuary, the arch of which Is 40 feet high, there are three altars—one high altar, bounded on either side by one of smaller dimensions. To the left of the sanctuary is the organ, a chamber having been specially built for its reception. Near to this chamber are the choir stalls. All parts of the church are well provided with windows, so that in the day- time an abundance of light streams into the interior. On the north side are placed spacious sacristies and con- fessional boxes, the doors of which are composed of pitdapine, excellently varnished. The seats and also the roofs of the side aisles are likewise of pitchpine. An efficient system of lighting has been introduced into the church, and the heating apparatus is of the most im- proved kind, pipes running through the building. The cost of the land as well as that of building the church amounts to £6,000. This, however, it is understood has been acquired, but there is still a substantial Wm to be raised to defray the cost of furnishing, he. THE MORNING SERVICE. At the opening ceremony which took place on Sunday morning, there was a very large congregation, the edifice being crowded to its utmost capacity. The Right Rev. Bishop Whiteside, D.D.. of Liverpool (who sang pontifical high mass at eleven o'clock), the Right Rev. Bishop Bilsborrow, of Salford, who delivered the ser- mon ; the Hight Rev. Father Carr, of Formby, assistant priest; the Very Rev. Canon Beggan, of Bootle, and the Right Rev. Dean Finnegan, of Appleton, deacons of the throne; Father Cody, of St. Alban's, and Father Clarke- 110n, of St. Mary's, deacon and sub-deacon of the mass • the Rev. Father Hughes, secretary to the Bishop Of Liverpool, master of ceremonies, assisted by Father Duggan, isf St. Mary's, Warrington, Father Clarke, of Widnes, and Dean Lennon, of Chorley, who occupied a prominent place in the sanctury were all present at the opening ceremony. Mr. Richard Eccles was the leader of the choir, and Mr. F. Mather presided at the organ. The ceremony which was gone through was of a most elaborate character. After the usual cere- monies had been completed, the Right Rev. Bishop of Salford preached an interesting sermon from the words " And Jesus said to his disciples, I say to you, unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink His blood, ye shall have no life in you, he that eateth my flesh and drinketh My blood bath everlasting life, and I will raise him up on the last day." St. John vi., 5,6. He said that however poor in design, however small in dimensions, and however common in material was the House of God, Be made it His home and abided in it day and night to receive their homage, to hear their prayers, to grant their petitions and to bless them in a thous tnd ways. ,On that joyous occasion, the opening of a new church, their thoughts naturally turned to the blessed Sacra- ment, not only because it was the fountain head of the chusch's holiness, the inexhaustible treasure house of her ' graces,but also because it was the revered object around which were grouped all her ceremonies and worship. It was an elementary truth with them not only that the Catholic Church never taught any doctrines opposed to the scriptures,but that it was impossible fcr her to do so since the scriptures were written by the inspiration of the Holy Ghost, and the Catholic Church in her procla- mation of truth was ever the organ and under the guid- ancs of the divine spirit. There was, perhaps, amongst all the doctrines of the Catholic Church none that had a more solid scriptural authority than her doctrine of the real presence of their Lord in the holy eucharist. The Evangelists for the most part did not record the same events. They did not dwell upon the same mysteries., but all of them, as well as St Paul, had given the weight of their abundant and collective testimony to this great mystery,as if they bad foreseen that it would make such large demands upon their faith and tow down their understandings to the obedience of Christ, and as if it would in course of time be questioned by those who made the measure of the infallible word of God their own human and erring judgment. He proposed that morning to lay before them one or two out of many pas- sages of the Holy Scriptures which no unprejudiced mind would doubt proved the real presence of their Lord in the eucharist. Christ raised up Peter to be at the bead of His church. Their Lard gave him supremacy over the church, thus using His divine prerogative say- ing, '• Thou shalt be a rock, and upon this rock will build My Church." On a later occasion, when He was surrounded by His Apostles and appealing to Peter,as He generally did,He asked" Lovest thou Me more than these the other Apostles love me." Peter did not answer the question directly because he remembered his fault, but he said "Lord, Thou knowest that I love Thee." "Feed My sheep." And so Be asked him to make a triple pro- fession of his love and then He said " Be the shepherd of My lambs ;" in other words "I am the Good Shepherd. I give My life for My sheep. lam not going to leave them without a shepherd, and I appoint you; Peter, in My place to be the shepherd of the fleck of Christ. Feed the sheep, take care of the lambs." Then He said to His Apostles "Whatsoever ye shall bind on earth shall be bound in Heaven, and whatsoever ye shall loose upon earth shall be loosed in Heaven." These no doubt, were mysterious words to His apostles, and they would ponder greatly upon their meaning. He appeared to them on Easter Sunday after- noon and said to thew' "Peace be to you. Receive ye the Holy Ghost. Whose sins you shall forgive they shall be forgiven, and whose sins ye shall retain they are re- tained." Coming to the question of the real presence, the preacher said that if any one did not understand our Lord, Be always caused his meaning to be shown with- out there being any doubt. He said to the Jews: "Un- less ye eat My flesh, and drink My blood ye shall have no life in you." The Jews and His apostles certainly understood His words in their literal sense, for they asked how could they eat His flesh and drink His blood. He made no attempt to contradict them, but allowed them to go away with this impression on their mind. The language used was of a very positive character. The preacher also contended that if the eucharist was a piece of bread of merely earthly manufacture, as a late Ang- lican Bishop had told them, and as probably the present Anglican Bishop of Liverpool would assure them to-day, taken as a bare commemoration that Christ died for them, it would be inferior bread to the manna, for the manna was supernatural, a heavenly gift, a mysterious • gift. Now the Jews and all the disciples who listened to Him understood Him exactly in the way that the Catho- lic Church preached the doctrine to the present day. It was a hard saying to many as it was now, but they as Catholics believed the Scriptures, and though they did not, could not, penetrate these mysteries, yet on the liv- ing word of truth they believed them. Could reason fathom the mysteries of God ? Could it grasp God Himself? Could a finite mind grasp the infinite ? Did not the Scriptures record a nudtbs r of incomprehensible mysteries ? What about the Trinity ? That was a dogma which not only surpassed reason, but to many appeared to be contrary t) reason, and so with the incarnation. In conclusion, the preacher referred to the question of Union, or rather, he would say, of reunion. Did not the word mean that there had been union before? Union on what doctrine, and in what religien? No one could say anything else but in the Catholic relig- ion, and if the 270 different religious sects that were crying out and swarming at the present day wanted to, be re-united there must be some restrictions of private judgment. Let them pray that in God's own time many might return once more to be re-united in the profession of that faith far which their forefathers had made so many sacrifices. The word reunion •neant that at one time there was a breaking away, and that they should re-unite. He appealed to the other creeds to unite to the Catholic church which based its doctrine upon the words. of the scriptures. He hoped that that church which was just opened would extend its influence in the in- terests of true religion. Further ceremonies were also performed, and a collec- tion was taken for the church fund. EVENING SERVICE.</p>
        <p>There was again a crowded congregation at the even- ing service. The Bishop of Liverpool officiated at the Rosary and Solemn Benediction, and the Very Rev. Father Sykes! S.J.,rector of St. Francis Xavier's Church, Liverpool, preached. In his discourse the rev, gentle- man traced the history of the Roman Catholic Church</p>
        <p>in thi. country-0% hi ch liad,in a general sense,been mainly supported hy the p mnies of the poor. They had always had the po r with tti,m, hut nevertheless the poor had atwa% s given their lttle mites for .the support of their church. It was a beautiful thing to have a Catholic Church in their midst, to feel that they had a place in which there was always that sacred Real Presence. This church which they had just planted in their midst was the embodiment of God's graciousness towards them. He appealed to them to use it well, to prove themselves worthy of so great a gift. Speaking of the Reunion of the Churches,the Rev. Father Sykes observed that every true Catholic wou,d wish from the very bottom of his heart that such would come to pass, and would pray daily for its consummation. But though this was the case their Protestant friends were bugging to themselves nothing more nor less than a delusion if they thought that the Catholic Church was going to make any con- cession to bring it about; they were mistaken if they thought that Rome would yield some doctrinal point just to please them. Their Anglican friends would have to recognise that they were not on the same plane as themselves (the Roman Catholics) ; they would have to recognise that the Catholic Church had truth on its side, and that they (the Protestants) had not. Consequently no reunion could come about by compromise. Reunion, must come about by individual submission to God's dis- ciples until by degrees the whole multitude of the great English nation should again be gathered into the bosom of the Church of Rome. Catholics must put no obstacles in the way of good Protestants, but rather attract them to their church, in order that they may see for them- selves what &amp; true and enlightened worship they pur- sued. Let their " Light shine before men," and in the times of their children, or their children's children Eng- land might be seen again a Catholic country. He felt sure that this holy consummation so devoutly to be wished would come about—in God's own time though— and in the interval their duty was to daily offer up prayers for that glorious day. When that day did arrive an unbroken song of praise would arise from every church, chapel, and dwelling throughout the length and breadth of the land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>ale met on Saturday at Runcorn, and a most pleasant game as regarded the feelings of the players, but a most unpleasant game as regarded the climatic conditions was played. The day was an awful one and in striking contrast to the previous Saturday,when Leigh was met. The players were wet through at both matches, but at Leigh it was with sweat,w bile at Runcorn it was with rain, and uncomfortable as the former was the lat- ter was still more uncomfortable. Tne Runcorn execu- tive were anything but pleased with the vagaries of the Clerk of the weather, for unfortunately, they connot sue him for the loss he has caused them to sustain.</p>
        <p>If the play on Saturday were anything to go by, the result of the match ought to have been a draw, for War- rington had quite as much of the play as Runcorn, in fact, in the last half, they had the greater share. How- ever, points however easily obtained, count more than all the play. Runcorn thus won by eight points to nil. The first try from which the goal was secured was very cleverly obtained, and Runcorn folly deserved it,but the second try was an absolute gift to them. Had Evans simply "ave put his opponents off side as he could have done, instead of kicking, no try would have been ob- tained. However, the best of players make mistakes sometimes.</p>
        <p>As I have stated,the teams were very evenly matched. The Runcorn forwards had a little more combination from longer experience, but the Warrington forwards went with such dash and determination that they made up for want of combination. In the second half they fairly pushed Runcorn by main force,and this is by a set who are reckoned light I It would be hard to particu- larise the players, as they one and all played so well, but about the best were Burrows, Saunders, Fairhurst and Hill, though the remaining four, Thornton, Wilcocks, Andrews and Barrett were very little behind them. Gay- ter and Farmer were the most prominent Runcornian forwards, the latter being al ways in the thick of the fray.</p>
        <p>The half backs were fairly matched. Nevins worked like a Trojan, and not content with watching the ball in the scrimmage, he pushed along with the forwards. Houghton also used his feet to good advantage, and the two will make a capital pair of halves. The other side were not,backward either, and fed the three-quarters well.</p>
        <p>The bulk ot the work at threequarters fell upon Bar- ber and Heesom for Warrington, and Warder and Dun- bavin for Runcorn. Barber is so consistent in his play that he never appears out of condition and he played with his usual dash on Saturday. Heesom inade his first turn out for the season,and showed that he had lost none of his old sureness of tackling and fielding the ball. His tackling in fact was a feature in the game. Burton had not quite so much to do, but he got through what he did with credit, although suffering from a slight in- jury to the knee Broady. had scarcely anything to do. The game appeared to rage all in the opposite aide to him. He had hard lines on one occasion however when he was called back owing to a pass being slightly forward. But for this there was not a man on the field could have caught him. Warder and Duubavin showed up the best of the Runcorn men, but this was probably because they had much more to do than their confreres. Jolley and Evans were about evenly matched as full backs. Evans is rather slow in getting in his kick, and that rather spoils his play. Jolley is a great drop from Houghton who formerly occupied his position. He will have to improve greatly before he can obtain county honours, although of course there are not many to choose from in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>On Monday, Warrington enjoyed the luxury of a win. Broughton Rangers were the victims, and they had such a putting as they never expected. Although Warring- ton only scored two tries,they had a dozen minors which show that although they do not count, that Warring- ton were the masters at the game. The Warrington for- wards again played a grand game, and quite beat their heavier opponents. There were three Warrington half backs, but of course one of them was playing against his native town. Garritty, the player in question did not however shine very much, for he and Quigly were over- played by Nevins and Houghton. Burton and Foster had the bulk of the work to do in the threequarter line, Barber and Cross being left to themselves pretty much. Forster made a very promising debut in first class foot- ball, and scored a very clever try. Barrett among the forwards also put the finishing touch to a good bit of forward play. Heesom was at his uld place at full back and displayed all his old resource.</p>
        <p>The victory of Warrington on Monday has raised them a little in the table, and now instead of lnly having two clubs below them, they have five, atone them, two Yorkshire clubs. They have also relatively a better position than Stockport.</p>
        <p>There have been many surprises this week-end in the matches of the Northern League which have been played and as a result of them,there is now no undefeated club. Bradford and Halifax were each beaten on Saturday,the former actually on their own ground by Hunslet, and the latter by Hull. After Hunslet's performance at Brad- ford, it is surprising that they should be beaten at home by Leigh. Tke latter club are " coming" with a rush, and are actually the second in the League. Since their initial match when they were beaten at home by Leeds, they have carried all before them, and have as good a position relatively as any other club in the League. This is an nnlooked for circumstance, for although the team was not despised by any means, they were not thought to be so strong as they actually are.</p>
        <p>Oldham have had a very good tinae this week end, for they have met both Wakefield Trinity and Batley and scored 2 goals, 6 tries to nil against them. They are ter- rible fellows those Oldham men when they start. There were four games in which there was only the difference of a try between the winners and losers, the winners being Wigan, Leigh (twice), and Hull, and the losers, Widnes, Tyldesley, Hunslet and Halifax. St. Helens also beat Broughton Rangers by the goal kick. Rochdale Hornets have at length won a point on the statistical ladder,Stockport being the team who have obliged them with it. Huddersfield are doing badly, and are not up- holding their reputation at all. They suffered two de- feats, one of which was at home, during the week end.</p>
        <p>The strangest team in the League is Stockport. They commenced with four games at home,every one of which they lost. Since then they have played three away,and not one of these has been lost. They have beaten both St. Helens and Laeds, and drawn with Rochdale Hor- nets. Stockport air surely must not agree with them. Anyhow it will be interesting to see how they fare at their next home match.</p>
        <p>The following are the positions of the clubs in the Northern League up to the present time : Score. Score. Pte. PH. Won. Lost. Dm. For. AR'et. Bradford Leigh Beghouse Ran 5 ... 4 ... 1 ... 0 ... 38 ... 3 ... 8 Halifax Mannlngham 6 ... 4 ... 2 ... 0 ... 40 ... 32 ... 8 Leeds Wigan 5 3 1 1 .24 13 7 Runcorn 6 . 3 .. 2 ... 1 ... 64 ... 27 ... 7 St. Helens Hull Hunalet Oldham Tyldealet, Li versedge 6 2 2 1 .28 .23 b Batley 6 . 2 3 .. 1 ... 23 ... 31 ... 5 Stockport Warrington Widnes Beght'n Ran 6 ... 1 ... 4 ... 1 ... 21 ... 34 ... 3 Huddersfield 6 ... 1 ... 5 ... 0 ... 21 ... 47 ... 2 Wakefield T 6 ... 1 ... 5 ... 0 ... 16 .. 73 ... 2 Rochdale H 6 ... 0 ... 5 ... 1 ... 13 ... 71 ... 1</p>
        <p>The Lancashire and Cheshire clubs figure as follows : Score Score Pts Plci. Won. Lost. Dm. For. Ag'st. Leigh Runcorn St. Helens 4 . 3 1 0 .18 9 6 Wigan Tyldesley stoeu port Warrington Widnes Rochdale R 3 ... 0 ... 2 1 ... 6 ... 38 ... 1 Wg'ht'n Ran 4 0 ... 3 ... 1 3 ... 20 ... •1 Oldham The following are the positions of the Yorkshire 1 section :</p>
        <p>Score Score Pt,. PM. Won. Lost. Drn. For. Ag'st. Manningham 3 ... 3 ... 0 . 27 ... 6 ... 6 Leeds Hghouse Ran 3 ... 2 ... 1 20 ... 3 ... 4 Batley Hull 4 2 2 . 9 10 ... Halifax Hunelet Bradford 2 1 1......11 ... 10 ... 2 Liversefte 3 1 . 2 .11 ... 11 ... 2 Huddersfield 4 ... 1 ... 3 ... ... 13 ... 21 ... 2 Wakefi'd Trrty 4 ... 0 ... 4 ... ... 3 ... 47 ... 0</p>
        <p>Warrington must buckle to to-day and revenge one of the two defeats they suffered last year at the hands of St. Helene. The two St. He:ens teams played havoc with Warrington last year, and as the Recs. cannot now be played, Warrington should endeavour to take it for two with St. Helens. They have a tough job on hand, but with determination they can manage it. Should they do so, they will have a chance of rising from the 17th to the 13th position in the league, whereas if they lose,they may fall to the 20th.</p>
        <p>The following is the team selected to do duty to-day : Heesom, full back ; Barber, Cross, Burton and Broady, three-quarters; Nevins and Houghton, half backs; Burrows, Saurders, Barrett, Bill, Fairhurst, Wilcocks, Andrews, and Thornton. forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>"THE MANUFACTURE OF COSTLY STEEL INSTRUMENTS." PAPER BY MR. T. MORRIS.</head>
        <p>"THE MANUFACTURE OF COSTLY STEEL INSTRUMENTS." PAPER BY MR. T. MORRIS.</p>
        <p>At the first meeting of the winter session of the South Staffordshire Institute of Iron and Steel works Managers, which was largely attended, Mr. T. Morris, of Warring- ton, read an extremely interesting paper on "The Manufacture of Costly Steel Instruments," from which we take the following extracts. The paper included in its scope references to the production of steel wire, and other delicate work. Costly and interesting specimens were shown by the essayist, including a set of studs and lady's ear-rings, made from intricate and beautiful designs in cast-iron; dentist's instruments, minute pins and figures for watches. &amp;c., all of which were treated of in the paper. Mr. Morris said the labour needed to produce many of the costly steel instruments was so great that the cost of the Siemens-Martin ingot, or ped- dled bar, as the case might be, sank into insignificance when compared with the cost of the delicate operations through which the material must pass before it appeared as one of those perfect instruments. He pointed out that cast-iron was a most useful metal, and the cast-iron studs for a gentleman's shirt, and the lady's ear-ring, shown that evening, would, for fine workmanship and other good qualities closely compete with any- thing produced in gold and silver. Mr. Morris then gave a description of the process of con- version of the puddled bar item steel. Siemens Martin steel was generally so uniform and reliable in quality and there was so little difficulty in producing it to any given analysis, that in his opinion it would in time en- tirely supersede crucible cast steel ; of course, the chief reason for this being the great difference in price bet- ween the two products. Many of the articles shown were made from Siemens-Martin steel. The makers, from whom the specimens wer3 obtained,used the purest and most expensive brands of Swedish iron, and the ingots they produced were hammered under powerful hammers lso as to make them as dense as possible. Steel billets for wire were generally rolled down to about In. diame- ter. Of the antiquity of wire there could not be the slightest doubt. That used in the dress of Aaron,the High Priest,was stated Is have been cut from thin plates of gold and wire had been discovered as far back as 1700ec.It was not, however, until about the middle of the fourteenth century that wire drawing was first practised, and then in Germany. Wiredrawing was practised in France at the end of the fifteenth century. and it was nearly 100 years later before the industry was intro- duced into England., during the reign of Elizabeth. The Forest of Dean was long the seat of the wiredrawing trade. In 1.663 the first mechanical wire mill proper was erected at Sheen, near Richmond. Bir- mingham appeared to have taken up the manufacture of wire in the eighteenth century with characteristic energy, but early in the present century Lancashire began to take the lead both as regarded quantity and quality. In reducing the wire rod to wire, the steel was passed through a succession of conical gently tapering holes in a steel plate. The very fine steel wire from which the hair springs of watches were made was made principally in France, and the draw- plates through which the most delicate wire was passed were set, according to the quality of the work, with a ruby, a sapphire, or a diamond. These draw- plates required great skill in their preparation, in the proper piercing of the extremely hard precious stones through which the metal has to pass. The Warrington wire manufacturer who presented him with many of the specimens got Ms. per pound, or £2,000 per ton, for the specimen of drawn wire, which was largely used in the construction of pianos, and other musical and mechani- cal instruments. For the piano wire he got 90s. per lb., or £lO,OOO a ton. It took 754 hair springs to weigh an ounce of 437 i grains. It took 27,000,000 of them to weigh a ton, and taking one to be worth three farthings, the value of a ton of these apparently cheap little things ran up to over £84,000. The barbed instrument used by dentists for extracting nerves from teeth was even more expensive, representing a rate of £444,000 per ton. A small pinion wheel shown among the specimens, with the least pinion made, and to produce a ton of these, with present appliances, would cost nearly a million pounds sterling. Mr. Morris also showed a specimen of inadvertent piping in steel ingots, which had been drawn down to wire for needles, and the de. feet had only been discovered when it was found that the steel would neither take a point nor an eye, because of the hole running through the centre of it. A mile length of the size of wire shown only weighed 211b5., and many of the ingots were 12 to 44 cwt. each, so that after allowing for waste, fifty miles of such wire might be got from one ingot, and the loss in the case of inad- vertent piping of the ingot was therefore high, because of the labour expended.—The paper was followed by a discussion in which several of those present took part. BUCKLEY-STREET CHAPEL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The Buckley-street chapel (Warrington) anniversary was held cn Sunday, when the preacher morning and evening was Mr. of . In the after- noon, a sacred concert was held in the chapel, the chair- man being Mr. Percival B. Scott. There were also present, Councillor H. Roberts. Mr. T. Morris, Mr. W. Roberts, Mr. Simpson. Mr. Wibberley, etc. After the singing of a hymm and devotional exercises by Mr. Wibberley, Mr. T. Morris, in introducing Mr. Scott, said that he was very pleased when he was asked to get Mr. Scott there that afternoon to take the chair. When asked to come, Mr. Scott did not hesitate more than a moment or two before he promised that he would do so. He was very glad that there was such a large congrega- tion. He had been there many times but he had never seen such a congregation before. He dared say that this was owing to the presence of Mr. Scott, whom he had no doubt they had read very much about, and whom they desired to see. He wished Mr. Scott success in es ery- thing he undertook. (Applause.) A miscellaneous con- cert was then given in which solos were given by Mrs. Morres, Miss Morrell, Mr. Cotterall, Miss Fieldhouse and Mr. . A violin duet was given by Mr. Blake- more and Mr. Trow, and the .and under the leadership of Mr. Richardson played a selection, and the children and choir also rendered an anthem. During an interval, Mr. Scott, who was received with applause, said that when Mr. Morris asked him to come there that afternoon, he promised to do so with no ordinary degree of pleasure. Mr. Morris had told them that that was the largest congregation Le had seen in that chapel. If that was so because he had promised to come among them, he was only sorry that the congrega- tion was not as large every Sunday, and he hoped that before long that building would be scarcely large enough to hold the people. (Applause.) If they could come there that afternoon for some reason. or other, there was no reason why they should not come every Sunday. They had Mr. Wibberlev who was working devotedly in their behalf, and he asked them to help him all in their power. In presiding that afternoon he had only one object in view, and that was to appeal on behalf of the church fund. That building should be kept in as good repair as possible and he appealed to them to show their generosity in a substantial manner. He also asked them to accord the singers and all who had taken part in the atternoon's enjoyment, a hearty vote of thanks. It had been a real pleasure to him to listen to them and he was sure that they were longing to hear them again. (Applause.) He also was sure that they would join with him in a vote of deep condolence with Mr. Fansha w in the loss he had sustained during the past week. He was left a widower, and there were several children who remained to mourn the loss of their mother. (Rear, hear.) He would have been there that afternoon but for his sad loss. If at any time in the future any of his (Mr. Scott's) friends desired his assistance he would only be too willing to give it. (Applause.) He was perfectly willing to assist any denomination in any place. (Applause.) A similar programme to ,that before the interval was then rendered, and the concert was brought to a termination in the usual manner.</p>
        <p>A stylishly attired young man named William Jone Pugh, who has been attracting attention at Douelas b his high style of living, was on Monday evening hand e over by the Manx police to a Scotland-yard detective on a charge of fraud.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
