      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>W&amp;RRINGTON.</head>
        <p>W&amp;RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>THE LEAGUE OF THE CRO-S. There was a very crowded meeting in the League Hall, on Sunday evening, The chair was occupied by the president, Mr. John Mullins, who is always in evidence at the League Hall. The meeting was first addressed by Mr. J. C. Dalton, vice-president, who referred to the attitude taken up by the Con- gregationalist body on the subject of Education. He said it was high time, the, those outside the fold of the Catholic Church, made up their minds on one cardinal principle in the Catholic faith. That principle was that the Catholic body would ever strive for the religious education of their chil- dren, and also for equal treatment for all schools. Mr. Mullins also addressed the meeting, at the close of which,the pledge was administered to seven men by the Rev. Father Firth 0.5.8. HOUSEHOLD LINENS, hemmed and marked free of charge.- 3. ano W. DUTTON, Sankey-at.. Warrington.—Adre. POSTMEN'S FEDERATION: WARRINGTON BRANCH. The annual meeting of the above branch took place at the Baths Hotel, Sankey-st., on Monday evening, Mr. J. Skelton, president, in the chair. After the routine business of the year had been dis- posed of, the members sat down to a tripe and chip- potato supper, generously provided for them by Mr. C. Boardman, which was enjoyed by all. Supper concluded, the rest of the evening was devoted to harmony. To terminate the proceedings hearty votes of thanks were accorded to Mr. Boardman for the supper, and also to the host, Mr. Gittens, for the very excellent manner in which he had catered for the party SEWING MACHINES Hand £8 10a. Treadle £4 4s with hand.ome walnut corer.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 and 18, Sankey-at., Warrington.—Advt. MR. LESTER BARRATT AT WARRINGTON. On Monday evening, Mr. Lester Barrett, the popular comedian mimic, and author, appeared on the platform of the new Parr Hall, and greatly amused and pleased a large audience by his novel entertainment, entitled " Hilarity," or " Homely jokes for homely folks." He was ably assisted by Miss Marie Smith (soprano), and Mr. W. Pickard,</p>
        <p>pianist and composer. The programme was a lengthy and varied one, and consisted of pianoforte solos by Mr. Pickard, songs by Miss Smith, and sketches, humorous and character songs by Mr. Lester Barrett, who, when he appeared in his new seaside sketch, entitled " Says Mrs. Brown tome," or "Papa's left at home," almost convulsed the audience with laughter. Miss Smith gave an ex- cellent rendering of the well-known song " Kil- larney," and was greeted with loud applause. The entertainment, which was in aid of the Warrington Shop Assistants' Association, was successful in every way, and it is expected that when all ac- counts are paid off there will be a balance of about £3O.</p>
        <p>WRINGING MACHINES. GARLAND, ironniong er Warrington. W ORKINGMEN'S GOSPEL TEMPERANCE MISSION. On Sunday night a gospel temperance meeting was held in the above hall, Mr. J. Drury presiding. The meeting was opened with singing and prayer after which Mr. William Shockles addressed the meeting, and said he had been a total abstainer for nearly 30 years. He had known employers pay for drunken sprees for their hands, thus foolishly caus- ing intemperance and neglect of work. Mr. William Nightingale sang with effect" Dare to do right' and," Only a Sunbeam. He gave a stirring address on "We are jolly people without strong drink." and said that all men should live so as to be a blessing to others. If a man lived to be a curse to others by his life, or by his business, be had better never have lived at all. Retribution must overtake him.' Mr. John J. Caldecott, of Blackburn, formerly of Warrington, gave a thoughtful speech on the " Certain triumph of the temperance cause,</p>
        <p>SEWING MACHINES Hand £2 18s. 6d. Treadle £4 4s. with handsome walnut cover.—J, and W. Durron 16 and 18 San key-Bt.. Warrington.7Advt,</p>
        <p>CYCLBS.—GARLAND, Sole Agent, Bridge-at., Warring on.</p>
        <p>" LIFEBOAT SATURDAY "AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On this annual occasion it bad been arranged in Warrington that a massed band of 200 instrument- alists from the Warrington Association of Bands should give a performance in front of the Town Hall on Saturday afternoon, a collection to be made in aid of the Warrington Lifeboat Saturday Fund. The weather, however, was so very unpropitious, and rain fell so heavily that an adjournment was made to the Drill Hall, where the advertised pro- gramme of music was gone through by the united bands, under the conductorship of Mr. Thomas Arrrstrong. No collection. however, was made from the public who had assembled to witness the performance, as it is intended to postpone the appeal to them to a future date, when probably the bands will give the performance in the Parr Hall, assisted by one or two vocalists. SEWING MACHINES, Hand x-c tos. oa. Treadle £4 45. t.l' handsome walnut cover.—J. and W. DUTTON, 16 an 13 Sankey st., Warrington.-4 dot. WARRINGTON DISTRICT HAIRDRESSERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>At the quarterly meeting of this Association it was proposed by Mr. J. Hedgcock, seconded by T. Hedgcock, that J. Podmore, jun., should represent the association as delegate to the Northern Counties Hairdressers A•sociation, meeting to be held at Manchester on October 15th. After a long discus- sion it was proposed by Mr. J. Podmore, sen., and seconded by Mr. T. Hedgcock, that the time had arrived when a set of rules should be formed con- cerning the binding of apprentices and matters relating thereto, and that a special meeting be called for the discussion of this matter as early as possible after October 15th. when the delegate to the N.C.H.F. meeting could give the result of his , inquiries at that meeting. This proposal was agreed to. It was proposed by Mr. J. McClew (president). and seconded by Mr. J. Titchard (vice-president), that a testimonal conveying the thanks of the association be sent to Mr. Charles Wainwright who has lately left town,acknowledging its indebtedneis to him for his services in connection with the assoc- iation and wishing him every success in Lis new sphere of labour. This was carried unanimously. The following hairdressers received the Northern Counties Hairdressers Federation tablet from the hands of the President, the Warrington District Hairdressers Association having joined that assoc- iation :—Messrs. J. McClew, J. Podmore, J. Podmore, jun., W. Podmore, G. Titchard, C. Olufield, J. Stocks, A. Lamb, P. Lawless, E. Smith, W. Smith, T. Hedgcock, J Hedgcock, J. Titchard, A. D umbel), G. H. Aorrell, J. Harris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>NATIONAL FREE LABOUR ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>THE NATIONAL FREE LABOUR ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>On Monday the third annual Congress of the National Free Labour Association commenced at Newcastle-on- Tyne. About eighty delegates attended. The annual report, in a review of the causes which led to the forma- tion of the Association, said the advent of the new Socialistic trade unionism was marked by deeds of vio- lence, both in London and the great industrial centres. The old trade unions had much to answer for in regard to this "so-called new labour movement." Seeing that the "so-called new labour movement " had "drifted into the hands of needy journalist, Socialist, and anarchic agitators, and of a heterogeneous mob of political out- siders," it was the bounden duty of the old trade unions to "pot down and stop the thousand senseless strikes which these men fomented to advertise themselves, to obtain seats in Parliament, and to make money." The report also claimed that the Association came into exist- ence spontaneously, and that it had protected thousands of working men against trade union tyranny. After an address from the President (Mr. J. Chandler, London), the report was adopted, and resolutions were alio passed in which the Congress re- affirmed its determination to uphold the principles of free labour, expressed its consternation at the tactics employed by the delegates and pickets of the new trade unions during trades disputes, and dire3ted that a Par- liamentary Committee should be appointed "to represent to the members of the new Government the necessity of adopting measures to restrict picketing and intimida- tion as at present practised." It was stated that Mr. Ritchie, M.P., had agreed to receive a deputation from the Association with reference to the representation of free labour in the Labour Department of the Board of Trade. The deliberations of the Association were continued on Tuesday. A resolution was adopted in which the Association "rejoiced that a stable Government had been returned to power by the votes of the working men of Great Britain," and "respectfully but firmly" called upon Lord Salisbury to put his declarations that he would do justice to British labour into legislative form, so as to safeguard the interests of workmen who would not be "coerced into joining the tyrannical and Socialistic trade unions." In another resolution the delegates de- plored "the outrages now being perpetrated upon non- unionist workmen in the labour dispute at Stalybridge." and called upon the constitutional authorities to protect free workmen from the tyranny of trade-union pickets. Other resolutions expressin,r the opinion that the time had arrived when a national understanding should be arrived at between employers and employed, and grati- fication at the progress the Association bad made during the past three years, and urging the Government to take steps to regulate the importation of foreign alien work- men were passed. ; The Congress terminated on Wednesday. The meeting , passed a resolution in which it placed on record its "emphatic condemnation" of the action of the Trade Unions Congress in endeavouring to force the Govern.</p>
        <p>ment to reintroduce the Employers' Liability Bill con. mining a clause forbidding contracting-outand heartily thanked Lord Dudley and other members of the House of Lords and members of the House of Commons for "safeguarding the interests and freedom ir.f British laboJr in the past." A further resolution was adopted in which the delegates expressed the apprehension with which they regarded the breakdown of the Trade Union Act of 1875, which had " wholly failed to secure the protection of the funds of the members of the varioug organisations registered under the Act," and called upon the Home Secretary to institute a searching inquiryin- to the grave question so as to secure a general altars- ' tion of the rade union law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>HIS COUNTRY FIRST.</head>
        <p>HIS COUNTRY FIRST.</p>
        <p>• "That man's; object is to serve his country with a sword, if necessary," said Lord Brougham, speaking of the Duke of Wellington, " but he would do it with a pickaxe." The duke's sense of duty to his country not infrequently made him deal with George IV. in a blunt straightforward way,and even to evade obedience to the. king's orders. An interesting anecdote, told in "Gossip of the Century," exhibits the duke disobeying the king that he might serve the nathn. The command of a regiment having fallen vacant. King G.orge said to Wellington, then Prime Minister, " Arthur, there is a regiment vacant, biazette Lord X. to the vacancy." "It is impossible please your Majesty ; there are generals who have seen much service, more advanced in life; whose turn should first be served." " Never mind that, Arthur. Gazette Lord X." replied the king. The duke bowed, went straight from Winsclor to Lindon, and gazetted Sir Ronald Fergusson, whose services entitled him to the vacancy. The king had the discretion to wink at Wellington's disobedience. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>THE PORT OF MANCHESTER. LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>During the seven days ending Friday, October 4, 115 sea going vessels (exclusive of passenger steamers),with an aggregate net registered tonnage of 32,934 tons, arrived at and sailed from the port of Manchester,which includes all parts of the Ship Canal. The number of arrivals and departures during the preceding week was 150, and the net tonnage 40,016 tons, the decrease being chiefly in the coastwise traffic,which showed a falling off of 37 ships and 6,135 tons; the foreign traffic showed an increase of twc ships, but a slightly decreased tonnage. Amongst the full cargoes entered inwards were seven of stone, five of pig iron, two of iron pyrites, two of steel bars,three of timber and wood pulp,two of logwood,and several of china clay and flints. The first full cargo of colours arrived by a sailing vessel, the Cicerone, of Ghent. The cargoes exported included four of coal, one of pitch, and two of salt. On Friday the Clan liner Clan Gordon left Manchester for Bombay direct, having loaded her Birkenhead cargo before entering the canal. I This is the first occasion on which one of the Bombay Conference boats have cleared finally from Manchester instead of from Birkenhead, and by doing so the time occupied between the Manchester docks and Bombay is reduced by several days. More than a snore of steamers and sailing vessels of over 1,000 tons net register are at present bound and fixed for the port, in addition to a large fleet of smaller boats. These include the steamers Lassell (1,173 trim) and Parklands (1,627 tons), with general cargoes from New York ; the Barcelona (1,180 tons), Cynthiania (1,934 tone). Durham City (2,037 tens), and Stockholm City (1,759 tons), with timber and general cargoes from Canada; the Gulf of Siam (2,126 tons), with general cargo from Melbourne ; the steamers Isle of Caldy, Lamington, Orianda, Heimdal, Alert, Urda, Mancestria, and Naranja,from the Meditcrranean with fruit ; besides a number of cotton steamers from Egypt and America. The steamers Girasol and Premier have been fixed with wood pulp from Drammen and Ahus respectively, the barques Cubaua and Riisoer with logvrood from Jamaica, and the Julie with timber from Bay Verte.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SALT SHIPMENTS FROM RUNCORN AND WESTON</head>
        <p>SALT SHIPMENTS FROM RUNCORN AND WESTON</p>
        <p>POINT.</p>
        <p>During the month of September the shipments of salt from Runcorn and Weston Point amounted to 5,507 tons, against 8,176 tons in September, 1894, a decrease of 669 tons. The total shipments from the canal ports for the nine months ending September 30, 1895, were 125,558 tons, against 117,076 tons in the corresponding period of last year, an increase of 8,482 tons,which must be regarded as extremely satisfactory in view of the diminution which has taken place in the volume of the salt exports from Liverpool this year as compared with last. The United States, however, took an unusually large quantity of salt from the Mersey last month, and if the activity in this branch of the trade continues the figures of 1835 may equal, or possibly surpass those of 1894.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>CONTINUED SERVICES.. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with St. Paul's, St. Peter's, and St. Barnabas' Chutchas were continued on Sunday, and were well attended. The advertised preachers were—St. Paul's, the Rev. Albert Warren, M.A., vicar of St. Michael's. Appleby : St. Peter's, in the morning the Rev. S. Wilkinson, M.A., vicar of Timperley ; afternoon the Rev. W. It Hart- wright, curate-in-charge of Orfcrd ; and in the evening the Rev. G. A. Le Bert, M.A., vicar of St. Ambrose, Widnes ; and St. Barnabas', the Rev. Dr. Oliver, of Liverpool. The Rev. A. Warren was curate of St. Paul's 20 years ago, and in the course of his sermon he I Paid a high compliment to the efficiency of the choir. He referred with pleasure to his former connection with Warrington,and said he was very much pleased to find that, although he missed many old faces that were once familiar to him, the services were as well attended and as bright and as cheerful as ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ORFORD.</head>
        <p>ORFORD.</p>
        <p>The annual harvest thanksgiving services for this dis- trict were held in Orford Church on Sunday. The in- to.rior of the building had been beautifully decorated as follows :—The altar by Mrs.Hartwright and Miss Thom- son ; thc pulpit by Mrs. Pollitt and Mrs. Corbett ; the screen by Mrs. Charles Grounds and Mrs. Cann ; the lectern by Miss Woods ; the font by Mrs. Grounds, and the other parts of the building by Misses Fisher, , Woolmer, Melbourne, Monks,Mag,gie Richardson,Wilson, Morgan. and Cann. The pulnit was occupied at the morning service by the Rev. W. R. Hartwright, curate- in-charge. The evening service was conducted by Mr. Flartwright,and the sermon preachOd by the Rev. C T. Watson, LL.B., rector of Lower Braughton,Msnchester, who based his sermon on the words " Ye shall bring a sheaf of the first fruits of your harvest unto the priest," and remarked that one heard it said sometimes that People came to harvest thanksgiving services who never throughout the whole year were found at any place of worship. He hardly thought that could be a correct: statement. They did see at their harvest festivals peo- ple whose faces they had not seen since the preceding harvest thanksgiving services. It did not, however, follow that those persons might not have been to a place of worship. It was charitable to think they had been. Whatever truth there might be in the statement one ought to be thankful that they were there on that occa- sine. It meant that they believed that the gifts of the earth came from God. In the afternoon the Rev. C. T. Watson conducted a 'children's service and delivered an address. Special hymns were sung at each service and in the morning the choir rendered the anthem " All Thy works praise thee,o Lord " (T. Mee Pattison) efficiently,. Mr. William Richardson singing the tenor solos witts good taste. The anthem at the evening service wall " Let us thank the Lord our God " (Caleb Simper). Mr._ John Cann presided at the organ. Offertories were takes in aid of the organ and choir funds, and amounted to £4 12s. sd.</p>
      </div>
