      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>MOST COLOSSAL FURNISHING ESTABLISHMENT</head>
        <p>THE MOST COLOSSAL FURNISHING ESTABLISHMENT</p>
        <p>IN THH NORTH: SPEC lALITE: HIGH CLASS SECOND-HAND FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>FURNITURE by JAMES LAMB, HENRY OGDEN and SONS, GOODALLS, GILLOWS of Lanca•ter, GARNETTS of Warrington. CUTTE Ft of Liverpool, SHOOLBRED of London, STARKEY &amp; MEI D of Altrincham, CHAMBERLAIN of Birmingham. and other High-cl 'es Houses. TERMS CASH OR DEFERRED PAYMENTS. All goods are advertised at cutting prices, being in most cases 50 per cent, below ordinary city prices for cash. If by deferred payments, no diff,r nce is made in the invoice price. but a slight percentage is charged according to the length of credit desired As compared with the hire system this saves from 10) to 2 0 percent.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY CHAT.—SECOND-HAND FURNITURE.— Benjamin Franklin said a penny saved is a enny made. This is an obvious truism, and ought to be borne in mind by all, rich and p 0 r, But at " The Pantheon" It is not on), a question of saving pennies, but f pounds. House Furnis ng is one of the important e, ohs in every family, welld erring careful thought. If you can economise with- outd riment to qu .lity and stability, then by all means do so,a f you cannot practice this theory better than by paying a - to" The Pantheon," which will cost nothing, but profit yO*l ouch. One of the most interesting sights in the Fur- nishing World is • • The Panthe m." a busy hive of indastry, containing without fear of contradiction, th ee to four times the stock of any two of the larzest furnishing houses in Man- chester. The bulk of this stock is High-class Second-hand Furniture Sy high-class makers, at quarter the price of the poor miserable, veneered orange box furniture from the East End of London, now so largely retailed by drapers. Beware of purchasing London furniture ; it lacks stability and finish. A Good Second-hand Article is worth a hundred of spurious imitations of furniture made in East London. Worsley's can tupply you with all you require in furnishing, by sich makers as Lambs, Gillows, or Garnet, at prices simply pul- verising competition. For instance, as a simple barg.dn you can obtain a Fine Sideboard by lloveatons which cost 685, for £l6 ; what chance has the competition of new furniture against bargains like these? If you go to an auction room and buy you take the risk yourself as to whether your pur- chases are sound end durable, but at Worsley's you have no risk, Worsley's ab,olutely taking all risk, guarantee every article for ten years. and will take any article or articles back not giving satisfaction. It will cost you nothing to call at " The Pantheon,"and see for yourselves ; you can inspect without feeling compelled to purchase; they will be delighted to show ou round whetir-r you buy or hot. Under thee circumstances you will be guilty of criminal, wilful neglig- ence of your own interests if you neglect visiting Worsley's before deciding where to Furnish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>ONLY ONE WORSLEY'S IN THE WORLD. GREAT BARGAINS IN COMPLETE HOUSES OF FURNII</head>
        <p>ONLY ONE WORSLEY'S IN THE WORLD. GREAT BARGAINS IN COMPLETE HOUSES OF FURNII</p>
        <p>THE GRIIATRST BARGAINS IN THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>COMPLETB HOUSES OF FURNITURE.</p>
        <p>Lot 1. Splendid House of ,Furniture, complete, the most substantial lot in this city, and containing more for the money. The Dining-room has a heavy Dinlog-room Suite, complete, in hard grain, Levant leas her, the frames beauti- full, carved in has relief; has comfortable couch andluxuri- ousiy upholstered easy chairs. a heavy dining table ton etch, extends to dine 10, with incised early English legs on patent castors; a handsome Per ian boreered carpet and rug to match, solid brass bar fender,fire brasses and dogs complete; cornice, with rings, ends and brackets ; Notting am lace curtains ; and las: , widish is thrown in gratis. and is in addi- tion to what any competitor could do, simply for an adver- tisement, a handsome Americen walnut sideboard, in the Renaissance style. to match the dining-room suite. The , Bedroom Suite is solid Cypru, wood anti is a suite which can : be highly recommended. guaranteed for 10 years, being charged in some establishments as much a, we ask for the whose of this house of furniture; a handsome solid brass and eon., elled bedstead, with complete fittl, gs of mattress and bedding; there are the usual accessories for bedroom—carpets, , curtains, fender, chamber ware, c. Also Kitchen Furni- ture, consisting of the usual chairs, heavy white top table, fender, ailman, and top bar, tloorcloth for kitchen, landing, and hall. I will pack and deliver the lot for 21i guineas, 10 ' per cent. extra if by deferr,d payments. Call and inspect this lot, it pulverises all competition ; if you approve buy, if you don't there is no harm done,—WORSLEY'S.._</p>
        <p>Lot 2. A Splendid House of Furniture for £4O, solid oak and walnut, heavy massive furniture throughout, in capital condition, worth £BO. dining-room, two bedrooms, kitchen, hall stairs, and landing ; a great bargain. Lot 3. A workingman's House of Furniture for £l5 : splendid bargain. There is better stronger, and more furni- ture in this lot than many competitors offer for £3O. Lot 4. The best House of Furniture for £6O ever offered. Same quantity as Lot 2, but higher class, except that we are prepared, as an advertisement, to present a pianoforte with this lot. or its va'ue. This offer holds good for 14 days only. Lot 5. And last, but not least, we have a magnificent House o' Furniture, dep,isited by the executors of a deceased gentleman of Alderley, with us for immediate disposal. Price for the lot, £2OO. Contains superb furniture for draw- ing, dining, sitting, and three bedrooms ; kitchen. hall, stairs, landing; be•ildes sundries ; honestly worth £OO. Full and comple'e particulars on application.</p>
        <p>GREAT BARGAINS IN DINING-ROONI FIT ItNITDR LOT 113. A remarkably handsome carved oak Dining- room Suite. In morocco, hair stuffed, hod, massive, an ha idsorne couch, two easys, six chairs, six-feet bideboard. eight-feet dining table, fine bold overmantel. coal caldnet, the lot complete for £45; worth £2OO. —WO itSLETS. Lox 212. A capital solid American Walnut Dining-room Suite, in best English Morocco, hair stuffed throughout, the framework is h.avy, magnificently carved in has relief, consists of broad comfortab e couch, pair of luxuriously up- hoisteredeasy chairs, a set of six high-padded back standard single chairs, all upholstered in a rich wine-coloured turkey grain Morocco, beautifully banded and studded, cost £7O ; price £l5 155., guaranteed for 10 years. The sideboard is of aob'e proportions, of modern design, well equipped with every moiern convenience, early English design, fitted with broad deeply-cut bevelled edge mirrors of great lustre and brilliancy, cost £65, for 16 guineas. A massive handsome Din.ng Table, £3 10s. , cast £l2 10s., and Overmantel, which matches the sideboard, for £5 10s„ worth £25. All the above in splendid conditl, n.—WORSLRY'S.</p>
        <p>Lot 251. A heavy handsomely-carved Dining Room Suite in Oriental saddle bags, a full snite of nine pieces, t gether with magnificent couch and easy chairs ; honestly worth £45, for 10fr guineas.—W.}ltSLßY'S. Lot 261. Excellent Spanish Mahogany and Morocco Dining Room Suite, nine pieces complete; cost £5O, for 8 kuineas.— WORSLRY'S. Over 200 Dining-room Suites on view, from £2 to £.500 ; aver 200 Sideboards from 355. to 100 guineas ; over 300 Dining Tables, from 15s. to £2O ; over 400 Overmantels.alt shapes, styles, and sizes, from Bs. 6d. to £3O. —WO RSLBY'S. Inspection invited.</p>
        <p>GREAT BARGAINS IN BEDROOM SUITES, _.....- _</p>
        <p>Lot 7-59. High-class Second-hand Bedroom Sulte,by Henry Ogden and cons, Spanish Mahogany, consisting of 7ft, Spanish mahogany wardrobe, rich in colour, modern fittings, worth £75, for £3O. Pair of the handsomest stands to match in Manchester; dressing tAble sft., washstand 4ft. 61n. for 18i guineas, worth £45. Lot 791, A grind Bedroom Suite by Todd. In American walnut richly and protusely carved in bas relief ; a suite greatly admired. Wardrobe is a masterpiece of design and workmanship beautifully finished inside with mode rn con- veniences. Dressing tab'e is mounted with large swing bevel- led mirror end jewel drawers. Washstand fitted with Carrara marble slab and back, three chairs, towel rail, and table de nuit complete, a very charming and effective suite ; price only 18 guineas ; worth trebIe.—WORSLEY'S. Lot 782. A similarSoite to Lot 791, same number of pieces for 10 gu.neas ; cost £35. WORSLEY S. A wonderful bar- gain. Lot 793. A genuine Sheraton Bedroom Suite, 6ft. 81n.,00et £250, for £59 15s. ; a real gem, worth seeing, first come, first served.—WORSLBY'S, Let 891. American Ash Bedroom Suite, cost £75, by Hud- son, £22. Similar to lot 863. Lot 897. Spanish Mahogany Wardrobe, break front, project- ing centre door. w,th full length crystal bevelled mirror, wings med. bonnet drawers, four trays, three lung drawers in centre, two l&amp; ge hanging compartments in the wings,Yery handssme, for £l5 las. ; worth £5O.</p>
        <p>Lot 19.—FuII-compass Iron-trains Cottage, by Brinsmead, £5 15s. Lot 27.—10u11-com lass Iron-frame Cottage, by Collard, £lO 106. Lot 35.—Fu11-cornpass Iron-trans Cottage, by Hopkinson, RIT Lot 47.—Fa11-compass Iren-franie Cottage, by Burling, RS 10s. Lot .49.—Pu11-compa.s Iron-frame Cottage, by Brard,</p>
        <p>£lB 18s. Lot 55.—Fine Handsome Cottage Pianofo to, by Hogg and Sons; the speciality plar,o, 7 octaves, A. to A. full trictio,d ; advertised for 24 guineas; price 14 guineas ; a bargain. Lot 63.—Magnificent Iron-frame New Pianoforte, by the renowned maker Kirkman. London guaranteed for 20 years ; in rosewood ; all latest improvements ; worth 60 guineas, music sellers'.price ; our prioe, 29 guineas. Lot 71.—Pine Steinway Model Upright Grand Cottage Pianoforte, by Hilton and Miton ;quite new ; splendid burr walnut care; a wonderful piano; highly recommended; gained the golsi medal; price 281 guineas ; worth 60 guineas. LOT 75.—Klegant Burr Walnut Piano, by Collard and Col- lard, recond-hand, but equal to new cost 85 guineas ; a genuine high-class instrument for £3l ; money returned if no, satisfactory in every respects.—Morsley's. LOT 110.—A Powerful Full-tone Upright grand. by Collard and Collard, in rosewood ; cost £66 ; for £24 10s. 1 almost new. LOT 120 —Very Handsome Piano, by John Broaclwood and and Sons, in solid rosewood; magnificent tone ; full compass, A to A •, cost GO to 70 guineas • price £2l ; a bargain. LOT 130.—One of the Finest Instruments ever maite,by that noted maker Brard ; second-hand ; wonderful tone, beautiul touch,repetition action,in splendid condition; for 27k guineas; worth three times the money. LOT 137.—Handsome and Powerful-tone Pianoforte, up- right grand cottage. by Scluedmayer ; £35 ; worth £lOO ; new. . _ _</p>
        <p>Lox 141 —Msgnifioent Upright Grand Cottage, by J. and J. Hop.lnson, London ; cost £75 ; for 19 guineas. LOT M.—Full-compass Iron-frame Cottage. by Brinsrnead, ElB. Lot 145.—1r0n-frame Cottage Plano, by Jus,,,, Browne, £2O. _ _</p>
        <p>--Lola 146 to 178 —Splendid Collection of Thirty Second hand Plans, by first-rate makers, from £1 10a. to £l5. We quote a few samples cnly:—Collard and Collard, 30e.; Broad- wood, 505.: Ballingall, if:4 10s.; Cadby, £6 105. ; Burling and Burling, IE7 10s.; Locke and Sons, £10; Challeu, £l2; Chap- pell, £l4; &amp;rd. £l5. Worsley's. Fifty Mfignificent New instruments, from £l2 to £6O. On View.</p>
        <p>Lot 215.—Pine American Organ. by Green, full compass. in massive carved American walnut case; seven sets of reeds s full Bourdon reeds; a wonderful bargain; £l5 15s. ; worth £45.</p>
        <p>Lot 217.—Splendld Harmonium. full compass, splendid tone, in excellent condition, for £3 155.; cost 18 guineas. Lot 225.—Magnificent American Organ, oust £3O, by Mason and Hamlin, for 10 guineas.</p>
