        <head>READ WHAT THEY SAY-</head>
        <p>READ WHAT THEY SAY-</p>
        <p>- One of those mod-rn monstce establishments, wi ose re- sources are so boundless, and whose stocks are so comprehen- sive, that it can, with • qua! care and despatch, furnish throughout a cottags or a mansion, is to be found in the famous house of J. GiIiNHON, furnishing contractor of 44, Oldham-street, Menche•te,. This colos,al business was commenced in lls7o, and being conducted with remarkable ability, energy, and foresight, and on sound commercial principles, It rapidly grew in public favour and patronage until at the present time it is the largest and most repream- tative house of its kind in Manchester. Mr. Gershon at- tends to every customer p,rsocally. and d Ts not leave cus- tomers in the hands of salesmen. Operations are carried wa in a magnificent block of four-storey bui dings. occupying a prominent and attractive position, and h ,ving a splendid frontage, They comprise shops, offices, and capacious show- rooms on the ground floor, and further barites ot extensive showrooms on the first, second, and third fl xors, together with showrooms at the rear of vast capacity and filled to re- pletion with an immense collection of valuable goals. Mr. Gershon Is a thorough exponent of the general house furnishing, ana stocks held are unrivalled in Manchester, either in magnitude, variety, or worth ; if wide experience and tar-seeing policy are absolutely requisite in properly selecting and getting together such a stupendous conglomer- ation ot ini,cellane ,us goods, it is equally true that a great amount of ability of no common ord g• is necessary to arrange and group them with such admirable effect, and to maintain at the same time su..th regard for the comfort and convenience. of visitors. Where sverithiugthat can possibly be considered to belong to this branch of business is fully represented, it!. an endless task to attempt anything like an enumeration In detail, it will be sufficient to indioate the leading lines with which the house is occupied. Superb an I comprehen- sive stocks are held in Drawing. Dining and Br, akfast Room Suites, made of all the beet woods, artistically elegant in design, and magnificently and gorgeously upholstered in all materials of various colours, shades, and tints. Bedroom Suites made of the most dainty materials,. and of the choicest patterns. And sub,tantiall, solid-looking Library. Strrtly, and Hall Furniture, together with inex- haustible supplies of splendid couches, ottomans, and so as, duchesse stands. shirty° rds, book-cases,cheffoniers, cabinets, Davenports, easy, occasional, and other chairs dining tables, dressing, work, card too, a d occa..ional tables, whatnots, overmantels, chests of drawers, as well as a large assortment of bedsteads of every deserhtion, p ttern, size and price.; : bedding, mattresses, curtains, fenders, fireirons and carpets, rugs, and mats of every matt, rn and design, from all the most celebrated makers. Briefly, the stock embraces every article of whatever kind that can possibly be required in furnishing any or every kind of residence, from the cottage to the mansion of the noble ; and not only is each in- dividual article to be found in these marvellous stores, but each article is represented by a largecollection, often unique In its kind. spiel including a wide selection in style, quality, and prices This enterprising firm have also manufacturing premises of consider •ble extent, and thoroughly equipped with pant and machinery of the newest and best kind end a large number of skilled workmen are constantly employed in the ma ufacture of cabinet work of all r-escriptions. Repairs, too, are execnted on the premises, a little army of assistants is employed in the control of this stupendous establishment, and competent MPH are sent all over the kingdom to fit up private residenr es, hole's, offices, public buildings, and mansions, in conjunction wi h their business. The connection of this representative house is far-reaching and most valuable, its goods leave acqnired the highest repu- tation in Lancashire and the neighbouring counties, and the name of "Gershon "is an ample guarantee for everything tht.t is superor to his line of business. The proprietor is a man of extensive experienee and ot great and varied know- ledge in the various branches of the business in which he. engaged. His able and energetic supers/don is given to the whole of the concern, and the utmost vigilance Is used that notlang shall emanate from his estab ishment which shall tend to detract from the high and well-merited reputation the firm has achieved. Mr. Gershon is bed in great esteem In priv ate life for his abiLty, public spirit, and commercial integrity. We strongly advise those about rnishing to call at Mr. GfiRSHON'S Universal l•urnishing Arcade, 44, OLDHAM - STRIDIT, MANCMHSTiIk. (Next door to the Adelphi Bank).</p>
        <p>y ETTERS ARRIVE BY EVE 'IY POST expressing thanks .L. 4 at thelmanner orders are carried out, and the following are published with the consent of the writers. READ WHAT THEY SAY. L. Samson, Eq.. the Managing Director of the Civil Service Dental Company. Limited, writes : "150, Brompton-road, south Kensington, S.W., "August 30, 1895. " Mr. 3. Gershon, Manchester. "Dear sir.—l have returned your packing materials and consider it only just to say :hat everything arrived in perfect condition. I am very p'essed with the goods. Should I require more, you shall have my order, and I shall be pleased to recommend you.—Yours truly, L. SAMSON. READ ON.</p>
        <p>_ Fred Bentley, Esq., writes Victoria Hotel, Manchester, Septcm her 25, 1895. "Dear sir,—l expected to be able to mil on you to-day on passing threwh, but time prevented my doing so. Every- thing has arrived in a satisfactory manner, and my wife and self are perfectly satisfied with the various suites you furnished me with, and now the work is completed the house looks exceedingly nice. I enclose cht qua in settlement. Please acknowledge to Southport, and oblige yours faith- fully, FEED. BENI:LBY. Mr. J. Gershon. Oldham-street. Manchester.</p>
        <p>ItEAD ON.</p>
        <p>W. Broadbent, Esq,, writes :</p>
        <p>" Reynaid atiage. Pi nkridge. September 27, 1895. "Dear sir,—All the goods arrived rafeiy, and give the greatest satisfaction. If they turn out equal to the goods I had from you 14 years ago, when I lived at Blackpool, I shall have no need to e9mplain. Cases and aeking returned. —Yours truly, "W. BROADHURST. "J. Gershon, 44, Oldham-street, Manchester."</p>
        <p>MARVELLOIJS BARGAIN.</p>
        <p>fil-Comequence of Financial Difficulties and a Death in the Family. I have had placed in my hands for immediatsi dis- posal the contents of handsomely furnished Villa, late the property of a solicitor, who, through the death of his wife and unfortunate spedulation, has come to grief. In addition to the dining, drawing, bed rooms, and library furniture, and a quantity of miscellaneous articles. I have a number of Law Books, particulars of which will be given below. Only a brief description of the goods can be given. Intending purchasers are cordially invited, and upow examination will find toe goods high Cia3S aud in spotless condition, they having been made to special order lees than 18 months ago. The hall is furnished with a most uncom- mon combination I-all stand, whioh forms a seat, bench, bracket, fitted with receptacles for sticks, umbrellas, brushes, gloves, to., and the back is finlshed with brass pegs, and a massive bevelled-e.ge glass mirror measuring about 3ft. long; price £5 15s. A pair hall chairs to match, 21s. The Dining-room is well worthy the at'entlon of those aliout furnishing. -It consists of a noble sideboard, which is guaranteed solid wainutwood through ;it measures 61t. long land abou. 91t. high. The lower portion is fitted with every convenience, plate drawers, wine bin, and cupboards, with splendid cary.d panels, and a truly magnificent back, with a massive shield eh 'PS centre bevelled g'ass and four smaller bevelled glasses, between which are brackets, supported by massive carved pillars. Next comes the solid walnut extend- ing dining table, with two extra leaves o,?ening out to a large ! size. and resting on finely carved fluted legs of rare thickness. To match are the half-dozen stand ad upholstered chain, two large easy chairs and couch, all studded Vk ith brass studs, and upholsterrd in a rich claret-colour leather of undoubted quality. To complete this remarkably handsome let, I have the pair of real Japanese vases, standing about 2ft. high. Now for the price. Some houses would ask £9O for them. I am in a position to pack and deliver the lot for 38 guinea.. The sideboard alone is worth the money. Tie Drawing-ropm really baffles description, it is so unique, and cannot be described in the space of an advertisement. The tour small occasional chairs, two conversational chairs. pair of Eugene easy chairs, and the gondola double-ettded !shaped settee, are most profusely inlaid with choice and rare w ods ; the upholstery is most uncommon, being a real broc belle of chocolate and silver, which is handsomely brocaded with flowers and sprays, trimmed with suitable gimp and highly finished. Next comes the magnificent inlaid cabinet, measuring about 4tt.9ln. wide and nearly Bft. high, fitted in IL most artistic manner with bevelled glasses, small cupboards enclosed with inlaid doors, It is simply a work of art, being complete in construction, design, and finish. The overmantle to match is very unique, most beautifully inlaid. finished with bevelled glass and side brackets to match the cabinet. There are two of the handsomest inlaid tables ever seen, octagonal shape, on curious shaped legs, with wine tray fitted between the legs. Now for the price. If £72 was quoted it would not be anything like the cost. lam able to offer the complete fittings for a stylish drawing-room at the low price of 38 guineas. The Library is fitted with 10 solid oak chairs, one large easy °hair, and very fine lounge. All are Doveston's make. hey are covered in leather, which is somewhat WWII. Whu n new they cost £5O. Can now be had for £lO the lot. There Is a very handsome carved oak bookcase, enclosed with glass do.ir, with carved pillars running up the sides. The lower pordsn is carved with curious heads. faces, A. Price, £lO. Very fine carved oak corner cabinet. 5 guineas. Superior black oak carved dinner waggon or side table, and several other oak pieces. Useful mahogany bookcase, with .folding glass doors, movable shelves, lower portion enclosed with cupboards and drawers. A great bargain ; for £4 15s. A fine old Spanish mahogany dining table. No better table ever matte, on bold fluted legs. Jive extra leaves, opening out to 20ft , also the leaf case. A dead bargain; tor £l2. . .</p>
        <p>There are several Bedroom Suites. he best roorn is in solid American wainutwood, and coat £9O, price 50 guineas The second room is in solid rosewood, which cost £8.,, for 40 guineas. The third room, which is very handsome, consists of a solid American walnut suite ; the wurclrob. is 61t. wide, fitted with two bevelled-glass doors, reaching f.om plinth to cornice; between the doors are lock-up drawers and cup- boards ; pedestal dressing table, titted with drawers in the pedisstsls. large bevelled glees attached; the double wash- stands, with cupboards in the pedestals, dark marble slab,tile tack ; botelled shaving mirror, towel Hirer, beside cupboard, and three chairs, upholstered in plush ; a £5O suite for 25 guineas. Several smaller suites, complete With wardrobes ; 13 guineas. Solid ash suite, with wardrobe, dressing table, washstand, bedside cupboard, chairs and towel airer, for £llOB. Several odd wardrobes, dreksing tables, and wash- stands. . .</p>
        <p>--Oaten oddments. Dinner set complete, 54 pieces, 18a. Ed. two corner cupboards, nearly new ; wicker invand carriage, on spider wheels and rubber tyres; cost four months ago, £8; price 555. A number of law books, among which are the following: William's Law of Real Prokertr. Leading Convay- cubing and Equity Can,es, " Inderman's ,' Leading Common Law Cases, " Inderman's ;" Law of Companies T. Eustace Smith ; the Conveyancing Acts, 1881 and 1874, with notes, forms, and precedents. iiy If. P. Wolstenholme and R. 0. Turner ; Introduction to Conveyancing, H. W. Hip- blnstone ; 4 vols. Stephens's Commentaries on the Laws of England. Ac.; a fine registPred writing desk, pedestal writing desk, both nearly new ; number cf bordered carpet squares, velvets, Brussels, and others. Some pleasing ull paintings and engravings, musical box, two pianos, quantity of china and glass. Several very fine brass bedsteads. Solid o.k case of cutlery, nevet been used, a wedding present. The whole have been removed, and are oh view at J. GERSHON'S Showrooms, 44, Oldtarn-street (next to Adelphi Bank). Can be warehoused till requtred, cad delivered free. Note address; J. INCHSHON, next to Adelphi Bank, 44, Oldlassa-street, Alancbeater,</p>
