        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12th, 1895.</p>
        <p>bw TILE old-fashioned peopiil The Church and Social Problems, re' are disposed to think thst,Po-1 cally and industrially,everythieg is ordered for the best in this country at the Pre' sent time, and that everyone should rest and ,„be' thankful under a Tory Government, will, it jo be feared, have been rather shocked, not t° scandalised, by some of the opinions enunciated a' the Church Congress this week. The Congress hae . dealt with a great variety of subjects, its Pr!. l° gramme including papers on religious educstion elementary schools,recent discoveries andthel3ihil. faith and science, and the stipends of the clerghbVtlB strange to say the speakers who seem to attracted the most attention were those dealt with the social movement. The clerical all lay members of the Church of England who present at the Conference actually seemed more deeply interested in the question a 5004.,,1-. ism and trades unionism than, for instance, in ways and means for obtaining more public 1n°13410 for denominational schools without more Pn'o control. When it is considered that Churchniel: and particularly the ,clergy, are as a rule Inn' more closely identified with the party of P° for reaction than with that party which stands l°,o rtt progress this interest -in social movements, olie other words, social reforms is just a li%cl surprising. The surprise is not ings°,l3„,to when the character of the papers is taken Tho account. Archdeacon WILSON, for exaruPle' dealing with the subject of Socialism did rot gi.oft any encouragement to the notion with some of the older-fashioned Tories comfort the; selves, that the period of political and social rto form is at an end, at all events for a long 61°,4 come. If there be any truth in the Arelideae°'re indictment of the present industrial system sge 8 e.• or ought to he on the verge of changes in nolnParxi . son with which those of the last fifty years are c, • most trivial. He declares that under the "Pree°7o, legalised scramble" for wealth "- wen are 11°640 make ;,money with and then throws aside ' tools." "The blinding power of customu ;13 The Turkish barbarities in A nia, to tnlw - extreme case, did not he supposed, seem to the Turks. They were done in the 'ivnY business. Yet there were results of our presand . system which we acquiesced in every daY, el:1r still more remoter results to which we 51111"; eyes, which might be almost as ghastly and christian in the sight of the angels of Gad ne.a4i; cruelties committed by the Torks. Yet soele"-' was not awake to the shame of it"-c°l3:, science uttered no reproach. It Was doclina horril in the way of business." Archdeacon Was°l4 not stand alone in his denunciation of raode.': social evils, for he was more or less supported t %Y. three or four other speakers one of whom w esae . DEAN of ELY. But when it came to the cl of remedies the speakers were singularly digidtio and singularly vague. The Rev, F. XoYrritA.t. suggested that the Church should take uP -ot titude of "cautions reserve" and of " intellige that.:sympathy " with regard to Socialism, and should at the same time be a rigid critic of tn as "methods of Socialism." The DEAN of ELY g/ n.trood of opinion that there were but two solutions .0b: the problem. One was "the conversion a bos</p>
