        <p>Masters and men to Christianity," that is to say to`,Christianity of the type practised by Count Tolstoi ; and the second—which seems more prac- tical as it is certainly more prosaic—is the forma- tion of representative councils elected by employers /14d workmen to which trade disputes might be Submitted. Archdeacon WILSON advocates the Itial of" exnerimental Socialism," that is to say, the taking over by the State of all unhealthy business and of the retail liquor trade ; and aalOther speaker, Canon ARMOUR, thought the Isluedy was to be found in the extension of trade Itilionisra so as to include the whole of the labour- , ing classes and on tho other hand the universal ,le,cloration of the employers. It will be seen therefore, that the clergy of the Church Congress !re Much readier to diagnose the diseases of ,fteiety than to find remedies. klother Trade THE • dispute in the shipbuilding Dispute. trade at Belfast threatens to ddvelope into one of the most tieplorable strikes or lock-outs of recent years. :Ile real cause of the rupture between the work- their employers in this case, as in m any VTililar instances, is not easily understood by out- But the central fact seems to be that the employed in the Belfast shipyards increase of wages. They say that when depressed thE y consented to a reduc- wages, and they argue that now that trade .ng and that orders for new ships are to, s the old rate of pay should be reverted the other hand the employers appear ta table in resisting this demand, uprni the t:Glind that the contracts they have in hand have 'clo taken upon the reduced wages, and that a 'rm to the old scale would involve them in</p>
