        <p>3` losses. It appears, therefore,that there is a h:ar issue at stake, and as neither side seems to be disposed to yield it is likely that Belfast will the centre of a disast:ous strike. What this raft, US to the industrial capital of Ireland may be -Btinlated from the statement that tiirteen thou- men will be laid idle and that fifteeen thou- Pounds will be lost in wages to the city. But, Tortunately, the mischief does not stop here t 18 pow understood that there is a compact be- the shipbuilding firms of Belfast and those the Clyde according to which they are to sup- each other in case of a strike in either die- If the engineers of Belfast strike for higher the employers on the Clyde will lock their out as a mark of" sympathy " with the at Belfast. This is a course of procedure may be considered good tactics by the concerned but it will certainly meet a largo measure of public condemnation. It be the means of causing a large amount of ' misery on the banks of the Clyde, and Will have the effect of driving away, perhaps to shipbuilding centres, many large orders it is said, were about to be placed with shipbuilders.</p>
