      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN POLICE COURT,</p>
        <p>At this court on Tuesday morning, before Mr. James Handley and Mr. E. Imison, a charge of being drunk and disorderly was preferred against William Brown, of no settled abode. The case was proved by P.C.Gregory, and a fine of 53. was imposed. PARISH CHURCH HARVEST FESTIVAL. The festival was continued on Sunday when the preacher was the Rev. W. L. Paige Cox, vicar of Rock Ferry. An address to children was delivered in the afternoon by the Rev. S. A. Boyd, who also preached in the evening. The musical portion of the service was specially attractive. ThA solos were sung by Mr. H. Branch, and Master S. Lane. Mr. .4X.,4 Uttler con- ducted the choir, and Mr. Johnson was the organist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>POOR RATES FR RUNCORN AND WESTON.</head>
        <p>POOR RATES FR RUNCORN AND WESTON.</p>
        <p>Messrs. James Handley and F. J. Norman have affixed their signatures to a new poor rate for Runcorn of ls. I. in the £, which it is estimated witl produce £3,491 7s. Mi. This, it may be stated, lathe largest rate made be the overseers within the last 23 years, it not having exceeded is. in the £ since the 15th April, 1872, when it was is. 6d., the overseers then being Messrs. William Rigby and rhomas Brewer. The increase in the require- ments of the Rill-, corn .S:ihool Board resulting from the taking over of the Wesleyan day schools,iargely account for the increase. The new poor rate for the township of Weston, is is. 23. in the £, estimated to realise £661 153. id. The tateis for the same sum as the correspond- ing period ta 'lye months ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WRECK OF THE RENFREW.</head>
        <p>THE WRECK OF THE RENFREW.</p>
        <p>Nothing ha;;;:el been seen ef- the -b7;die-S.--cdthe Runcorn men, viz., John Burton, Cavendish-st., and William Lloyd, Shaw-st., who were drowned by the sinking of the steam flat Renfrew, in the gale of Wednesday week. An inquest was held on Saturday by Mr. Sampson, city coroner for Liverpool, on the body of William Carring- ton, 39 years of age, a flatman, who lived at thAddison- at., Bootle. Deceased was the mate of the Renfrew, owned by William Burton, 4, Preeson's Row, Liverpool, and also of Warrington, which was wrecked on the West Hoyle Bank. The body of Carrington was picked up by a flat on Thersday,and conveyed to the Carrington Half Tide Dock, from whence it was taken to Prince's Dock Mortuary. Deceased's wife,who had identified the body said her husband left home on Monday to go with the flat to Penmaeomawr. The inquest was then adjmrned till the 16th inst. RUNCORN RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the eighth and final competition for this year in connection with the above-mentioned club, took place in most unfavourable weather. The scores were :</p>
        <p>200 EOO 600 To-</p>
        <p>yds. yds. yds. tat. Sergeant W H Harvey 3l 33 ... 29 ... 93 Colour-Sergeant J Mort Private J Houghton 27 ... 27 ... 27 ... 8 Private J Mort Sergeant J A Rushton 27 ... 30 ... 19 ... 76 krivate Bradbu y 3l ... 20 ... 20 ... 71 Private S Joinson 29 26 ... 16 ... 71 Private S Stubbs</p>
        <p>The result of the above contests is that Sergeant Harvey retains possession of the gold medal for the en- suing twelve months.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>The foreign missionary anniversary was held at Elalton- road Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday, when tne Rev. J. D. Sutcliffe, late missionary on the South coast of Africa, preached. In the afternoon he gave a delightful address to the scholars of the Sunday school, adding considerable interest thereto by the exhibition of curios, idols, and articles of clothing, that the women spun and the men embroidered. Mr. Page, superintendent, in thanking the rev, gentleman for his address, said it was one of the beat he had ever heard. In the evening there was a large congregation. when Mr. Sutcliffe gave a description os the work of the Wesleyan Missionary Society on the West Coast of Africa. The address throughout was highly interesting.—The collections in aid of the Wes- leyan Missionary Society realised a very satisfactory amount. ANCIENT ORDEE OF SHEPHERDS. —RUNCORN UNITY.</p>
        <p>This branch uf the order held its 92nd quarterly meet- ing on Saturday at the White Barn Hotel, Cuddington, the abode of the newly-formed lodge known as " The Rose of Cuddington." The Secretary, Bro. Smethurst, kindly provided ego' for the various delegates. The officers present were P.C.S, VVildon, D.P.C.S. Eillidge, P.C.S. Rhodes and P.P.C.S. Santley. and the delegates in attendance were P.M. Woodland, Shepherd's Star ; Bro. Mann, Shepherd's 'Tent ; Bro. Antrobus, Pride of Acton ; and Bro.Smethurst (secretary),Rose of Cudding- ton. The accounts for the past quarter were audited by P.M. Woodland and Bro. Smethurst. They showed that the funds had greatly increased during the quarter, and that the membership had increased by twelve. After this the following officers were nominated for the ensuing twelve months : For P.C.S., J. Hillidge ; for D.P.C.S., I. Lightfoot ; corresponding secretary, W. Rhodes ; and treasurer, J. Santley. FUNERAL OF MR. J. KNOWLES. The funeral of Mr. J. Knowles, of the Royal Hotel, Lime-st., Liverpool, and formerly of Runcorn, took place on Saturday afternoon in Anlield Cemetery. The deceased died suddenly in his hotel the previous Wednes- day. The chief mourners were Messrs. J. E. Knowles, W. E. Knowles, and C. Knowles (sons), Mr. J. Stringer (nephew), Mr. J. Hampson (nephew), Mr. W. Roberts (brother-in-law), Mr. J. A. Roberts (brother-in-law),'Mr. J. Ackerley (nephew), Mr J. Bate (cousin), and Dr. Adams ; and amongst others present wereMr.Lightburn, Captain White, Messrs.C. Reynolds, J.Adams, J. WKin- nell, T. Lowton, J. EL Smethurst, J.Jackson, T.Hughes, J. Gerard,Fl.J.Marshall, E.Graham, C.A.Bell, J.R.Brom- , ley, R. N. Glover, T. F. Hughes, J. B. Pressure, E.Hud. son, M. Barker, J. G. Pagnall, and G. Morris (represent- ing the Personal Liberty League). The service was conducted by the Rev. J. Passmore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>CADETS OF TEMPERANCE.</head>
        <p>CADETS OF TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>In eonnection with the Runcorn Guide of Youth sec- tion ot this order, a meeting was held in Greenway-road schoolroom on Saturday evening. Mr. Thomas Whyte occupied the chair. A feature of the meeting was a pre- sentation of two prizes, the gift of Mr. Thomas Cooke,to the two who had secured the largest number of members during the last six months. The first prizs was a book,by the Rev. Silas K.Hocking "Chips,doe,and Mike,"which was presented to Mr. Thomas Wilred Whyte. The iiesond was a pictorial book entitled "From Adam to Moses ;" this was gained by Maria L. Tarleton. Mr. Thomas Cooke, in giving the prizes, spoke of the great import- ance of personal influence, that showed how boys and girls could work for temperance, and induce others to become abstainers. He gave a striking illustration of the good that might be effected in this direction. He also recited with effect a Lancashire recitation, entitled, " Bob and his wife, S.dly." Mr. W. Bisbrown moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Cooke for his kindness in giving the prizes. Tnis was seconded by Mr. Edward Key, the secretary, and carried. The Chairman, Mr. Whyte,then offered two more prizes, to be competed for during the next six months. ACCIDENT ON THE WEATIM—NAIIROW ESCAPE OF A</p>
        <p>BQATWOMAN. A collision between a narrow boat and a steamer oc- curred on the river Weaver, close to Rocksavage Bridge, Runcorn,on Tuesday morning, and a boatweman named Mrs. Gough, of Runcorn, narrowly escaped being drowned. It appears that two narrow boats, named respectively the Ada and Amy, containing cargoes of salt, and belonging to Messrs. Rayner and Company, of Runcorn and elsewhere were proceeding to Weston on the river Weaver, on Tuesday. The boats were in charge of Mr. Gough, and Mr. William Gough and his wife. When about one hundred yards beyond Rocksavage swing bridge they passed by another boat corning in the ' opposite direction. In order to let it pass,it was neces, sary for the Amy, which was behind, to steer more into the centre of the waterway, and as it did so it was caught in the swell " of the steamer Thistle, and slightly collided with it. The narrow boat was over- turned, and Mrs. Gough who was in charge of it at the time, jumped into the water. Her husband,who was on the first boat,swam to her rescue,and brought her to the side of the river. She was taken to a house near to, where she was provided with dry clothing. It is thought the steamer's propreller came in contact with the narrow host for its staves were afterwards found to be stripped. the steamer ran into the opposite hank and had to be pulled off by means ot Ncinches. It belonged to Messrs. Brunner. Mond and Co., and was proceeding to Liver- pool. The narrow boat which was sunk,in addition to a ' cargo ot over thirty tons of salt, contained all the fuel- lure and effects of the boat people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>VA A MINER SATURDAY, OCTOBBR 12, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE VA A MINER SATURDAY, OCTOBBR 12, 1895.</p>
        <p>THE LIGHTING OF RUNCORN BY ELECTRICITY. A meeting ot the Committee appointed to consider the advisability or otherwise of introducing the electric light in Runcorn was held in the Town Hall, on Thursday morning. The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi was appointed chair- man. After some discussion, the Secretary was instructed to write to various places using electricity, in order to obtain further information. HALTON-ROAD WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the ladies' sewing committee, connected with Halton-road Wesleyan Church, was held on Tuesday when the work for the coming year was arranged. Mrs. Frith was appointed treasurer, Mrs. George and Mrs. Graham, se-:retaries. The Chairman (Rev. W. George) informed those present that the trus- tees of the chapel were greatly obliged for the grant of £2O towards the trust fonds which thk ladies contributed as part of the result of their work of the previons year. It was decided that durin:: the next few weeks the room in which the ladies met should be re-furnished and re- decorated. ST. LUKE'S CONGREGATIONAL CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>At this church on Sunday the annual harvest thanks- giving services were held. The interior of the building had been most attractively decorated by the following : Mrs. LUWO, Mrs. Clarke, Mrs. Lacy, Mrs. Slade, Miss Stade. the Misses Millington, Miss Clough,Mrs.Robinson, and Messrs. Lowe, P. Cartwright, Wood, Slade, and Clarke. At the a ternoon service the preacher was Mr. Joseph Parker, and at night Mr. R. Peacock preached. —A fruit banquet was held on Monday evening.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>Trrasner.—Before his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. The business consisted of 8 derault summonses, 122 original plaints, and 50 judgment summonses. ACTION AGAINST A HAIIPERMAKEIL—Arthur Greenvrav, farmer, Alvanley, sued for the sum ot £4 6s. from R. Lloyd, harnpermaker, Mouldsworth, for breach of con- tract. The plaintiff was represented by Mr. Burton, solicitor, but the defendant did not appear. Mr. Bur- ton stated that on the 26th November last year, the plaintiff ordered 16 doz. hampers at 16s. per doz. to be delivered in March for the early potato trade. He paid £lO on account. The defendant, however, did not fulfil his contract, and only supplied 10 doz. of the hampers, tbe consequence being that in June, at the last moment, the plaintiff was obliged to go to Man- chester, and purchase six doz. for which he paid £1 per doz.—Mr. Greenway corroborated his solicitor's state- ment, and his Honour allowed damages £3 14s. A MONEYLENDER'S INTEREST.—JOhII Henry Harrison, Waterloo-road, Runcorn, claimed the sum of £3 17s. 61. for money lent and interest, from Zschariah Hughes,27, Blantyre-st.—Mrs. Hughes, who appeared on behalf of her husband, admitted that the sum of £2 17s. 61 was owing ; but objected to the Ll which had been charged for interest.—The Registrar said the amount was due according to the agreement entered into, and if people were foolish enough to sign such promissory notes, they must take the responsibility.-7 Verdict for the plaintiff.</p>
        <p>A WARRINGTON' TAILOR'S CLAIM.—A claim for £3 6s. balance of a tailor's bill, was made by Edgar Balmford, of Sankey-et., Warrington, against Thomas Fowler, GrPenall-st., Warrington, but formerly of Runcorn. The plaintiff was represented by Mr. G. W. Allen. He ex- plained that his client took the order from the defendant and had received some payments,both from the defend- ant and his mother.—The defendant stated that he WAS only 17 years of age when the debt was incurred, and that he paid 17s. per week for board, lodgings, and clothes. He denied that he had paid the defendant any- thing, but afterwards in cross-examination, admitted that he had done so once or twice.—Mr. Allen stated that the plea of infancy could not apply in that case as clothing was a necessary article.—His Honour gave judgment for the plaintiff, the sum to be paid at 3s. per month.</p>
        <p>AN INTRR2LEADER CAsx.—ln a Runcorn interpleader case, Messrs. Emmott and Co., Ltd., Manchester, were the execution creditors ; Jabez Horrocks, formerly a newsagent and tobacconist, High-st.,Runcorn, the de- fendant, and Amelia Horrocks, his wife, stationer and tobacconist, Lowlands-road, was the claimant.—Mr. Wragg,solicitor, Manchester, appeared on behalf of the plaintiffs, and Mrs. Horrocks stated her own case. In doing so, she stated that certain goods had been seized by the creditors, and these were her property. She was the wife of Jabez Horrocks. and had earned from 6s. to 10s. per week by doing plain sewing. She had been married 19 years. Her husband was a plumber by trade, but five years ago he stopped work on account . of au accident. When he was woi king he gave her about 283. per week. She had been the tenant of the houses in which they now lived since January last. Her husband was at that time in difficulties, and the effects at the shop in High-street, which was in her husband's name, were sold early in February. She had conducted the business in Low- land's-road herself for about 21 years, and cleared about 103. a week. A list of various articles of furniture was handed in,and she stated that she had bought these her- self, and had borrowed £l7 from her brother-in-law, in order to do so. These goods had been seized by the eaecutien creditors.—By Mr. Wragg : Her husband had had the" Umpire "newspapers from Mesers. Emmott. Ehe denied that she had sold more than one or ray° odd copies of the " Umpire " at the Lowland's-road shop.- Her husband had the entire control of those newspapers. Her brother-in-law from whom she had borrowed the money was William Horrocks,of Higher Broughton. She could not remember his address, but he was a plumber at Boddington's brewery. Hie could not say how much she paid the auctions,er when she bought the goods. In order to pay the bailiffs she pledged some of her cloth- ing. She paid the bailiffs something like £2.—Luke Holmes, auctioneer, gave evidence as to selling the goods at the High-street shop in February of this year, under a distraint for rent. He had not his books with him, but he remem- bered that Mrs. Horrocks purchased the articles stated on the list, with the exception of a wringing machine and dresser.—Mr. Wragg stated that his client's debt was contracted since July, 1894, duri-o, which time, Mrs. Horrocks had been trading with her husband. He contended that the story she had put forth was a very improbable one. He doubted the credibility of her hav- ing borrowed money from anyone, and contended that the money she stated she had placed in a drawer, was a joint stock affair. It was a doubtful matter. He also thought that the auctioneer should have submitted his books to the Court.—His Honour said it was a case which was by no means free from suspicion. The brother-in-law might have been called to speak as to the borrowed money and the auctioneer might have been better prepared in his evidence. She had produced a list of goods she stated that she had bought at the sale with money she had borrowed. If it was true that she borrowed £l7 to purchase them, then they would be her own. Her evidence that she bought the goods was borne out by the Auctioneer. He must say that she gave her evidence very well and answered the questions put to her straight forwardly. The matter came to this, that either he must come to the conclusion that she had committed deliberate and disgraceful perjury or that her statements were true. There was no evidence brought against her, and it was quite probable that the trans- actions referred to really took place. He would give judgment for the claimant and allow her costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</head>
        <p>MERSEY MISSION TO SEAMEN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the Runcorn branch of this mis- sion the annual harvest thanksgiving services will be held on Sunday. In addition to the usual display of fruit, flowers, and cereals, the harvest of the sea will be represented. The Rev.A. Jameson,M.A., will preach at the morning service, and the Rev. 11. E. Ellwell, M.A.,of Bootle, in the evening. A children's service will also be held in the afternoon.—On Monday a concert and social evening will take place. FIALTON•ROAD WESLEYAN CHOIR, RUNCORN,</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the members of this choir to the number of 35 had a social evening and fruit banquet in the Morning Chapel. The decorations with which the room was embellished had been lent for the occasion by Mr. Graham, of Church-st. A capital programme of songs, games, &amp;c., was gone through. Mr. H. Pope con. tributed a pianoforte solo, and songs were well rendered by Miss C. Taylor, and Messrs. R. Yates, 11. Johnson,T. Johnson, A. Clare, and J. Brown. Mr. and Mrs. Yates gave a vocal duet, and Miss M. Pope performed a piano- forte solo. A most enjoyable evening was spent, all expressing themselves delighted with the evening's pro- ceedings. "CONVENTIONALITY." This was the subj.,ct introduced to the members of the Brunswick Mutual Improvement Society on Wednesday evening by Mr. F. H. Gone. In the course of his paper he stated that conventionality largely retarded the moral and spiritual progress of the country. He dealt severely with conventionality in the religious bodies of to-day ; criticised the churches' action with regard to young people and amusements ; and advocated more charity. He said success had become the fetish of the world, and anything was sanctioned under the name of business. He instanced cases in which so-called Christians forgot honour and principle when they became business men, and conchaded by pleading for reality in. life, and for a stand against the formalism which was present in all classes ot society. An interesting discussion followed, and was taken part in by the Chairman (Mr. T. Simistor) Mr. Millington, Mr. J. D. Jones, and Mr. Millinger. The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>In the Brunner Guild Hall, Runcorn. on Wednesday evening, the first annual concert and distribution of prizss in connection with the Runcorn Cycle Club was held. The large room had been nicely decorated for the occasion with fairy lamps, bannerettes and suitable mottoes. The President of the club (sir. S. Taylor) pre- sided, and in the course of a few introductory remarks, he said he weuld like to mention the practical help given by the club to the Lifeboat Institution and Nurse Fund; two more worthy objects they had not in the country. He could speak of the great good being done in Runcorn by means of the Nurse Fund, by helping those who could not help themselves. It was really a great help to the Nurse Fund, when last year, the Cycling Club organised a carnival and handed over the sum of £l5 to the fund. this year they had still been more successful and had raised £25 towards that object. He hoped the club wo Id go on successfully.—An interesting and varied programme was then proceeded with. Those who con- tributed items were Messrs. Shiogler, Parry and Worrall A. Grounds, Miss Clara Taylor,Miss Pope, Miss Lily John- son,Miss Sirokin and Miss Alice Taylor. All sang in such good style as to receive hearty receptions. Mr. William Grounds was the accempanist. During the evening a number of prizes which had been competed for during the season were distributed. The following is a list of the prize winners :—Ten miles handicap, Ist prizo (gold medal) H. Stubbs ; 2nd prize (silver medal, gold centre) H. E. Smith ; 3rd prize (cycle stand) T. Grounds. 25 miles handicap, Ist prize (gold medal) G. Webster ; 2nd prize (gold centre) W. Rushton ; 3rd prize (silver medal) W. H. Hill ; extra prize for fastest time (gold medal) G. Whinyates. Attendance prizes (Wednesday section) G. Whinyates, W. Rushton, W. H. Hill ; (Saturday section) J. Crawford, E. Lawson, and G. Davies. The entertain- ment was brought to a close by a vote of thanks being tendered to the Chairman, and to the donors ,f the prizes. Dancing was afterwards engaged in until mid- night.</p>
        <p>.....-.--.....--</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>RUMOURED NEW INDUSTRIES FOR</head>
        <p>THE RUMOURED NEW INDUSTRIES FOR</p>
        <p>II UN CO RN. VISIT OF SALT UNION DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>For some time now, rumours have been extensively circulated in Runcorn and district to the effect that it was the intention of the Salt Union, Limited, to estab- lish chemical works in the vicinity of Weston Point. The rumour was regarded by some as but a type of many of its forerunners which have been so plentiful in Run- corn of late, but nevertheless, the rumour gained cred- ence in some quarters, and on Thursday there seemed to be a modicum of truth in it, or at least something on the tapis, when a number of influential directors and chief officials of the Salt Union visited the district. The party consisted of the Hon. Lionel Ashley (chairman), Mr. E. S. B-Gould, Mr. A. M. Turner, Mr. Hermon Falk, Mr. Thomas Ward, J.P., Mr. J. M. Fells (general manager), Mr. Pretty, Mr.Rigby,Mr. Forster and Mr. Grigg (manager, Middlesboro'). They arrived at the Old Quay, Runcorn, shortly after one o'clock, they having come tie tht Ship Canal from Liverpool, in the i.e. " Herald of Peace." They at once proceeded to Wilson's Hotel where lunch was psrtaken of. This over, they were conveyed in waggonettes to ; Weston Point where several hours were spelit in investi- gating various matters. Of the real intent of the visitors we can state on reliable authority that they were making a survey of their works there, in addition to viewing certain sites whereon it was quite possible that within a very short period they might determine to erect chemical works, a proposal which, it may be mentioned, has been receiv- ing the earnest attention of the directorate. The Runcorn lav-hy and the new tipping stage in course of erection for caal and salt were inspected. If an advantageous arrangement can be made with the Ship Canal Company for shipping large quantities of salt from this point,there is every probability that the present operations carried on by the Salt Union, at Weston Point will be consider- ably increased. Already the question for extending the warehouses at Weston Point for storing larger quantities of salt awaiting shipment for foreign markets is under consideration. Before any steps are taken, however, they are awaiting the decision of the Ship Canal Com- pany, and of several others whom the change would effect. The crux of the whole matter lies in the fact that the company are desirous of cheapening the cost in despatching their goods, and if this district proves to be the most favourably situated for doing so the Salt Union are prepared to avail them- selves of the advantages. Considering the extensive ex- port trade done by the company, if the alterations take place work will be constantly found for a large number of men. The definite decision of the company will be known in a few days, and the result will be anxiously aasaited in Runcorn and immediate district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WIFE BEATER -SENT TO PRISON.</head>
        <p>A WIFE BEATER -SENT TO PRISON.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn PAice Court on Wednesday morning be- fore Mr. F. J. Norman (chairman) and Mr. E. Itnison, Peter Knight, was charged under warrant with non-ap- pearance to a summons for assaulting his wife, Annie, I now of Regent-street, on June 20th. Complainant stated that she had been married 19 years, and had previously bad an occasion to bring her husband to the court for assaults, for which he had been sent to prison. Mainten- ance orders bad been made against him but he cared nothing for them, an.' he had not paid a single halfpenny towards her support. The orders had been annulled through her residing with him on his promises to reform On June 20th she had been washing at hersister's all day. On returning home she asked her daughter, where her father was and was told he was in bed, having had some drink. She sat down and prisoner came into the room dragged the chair from under her, and struck her whilst on the floor until she was stunned. He threatened to finish her, and later on knocked her head through the window, Her injury necessited her being under the doctor for a month. She had two children under 10 years of ageand one 11.---Prisoner said the account given was untrue. His wife by all means tried to get him home and then sent to gaul.—The Chairman said that several orders had been made against the prisoner to maint.tin his wife arid fatnily. Ile was a lazy fellow, a curse to his wife, family and town. He would have to go to gaol for two months and an orderwas made for 15s. per week for the maintenance of his wife and family.</p>
        <p>At the Assembly of the Baptist Union at Portsmouth on Thursday, resolutions were passed urging the exten- sion of the School Board system, protesting against the support of sectarian schools from public funds, and de- claring that all State-aided schools should be under public control ; in favour of disestablishment, and de- mnuding a Government scheme of licensing reform.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>FRODSHA3f.</head>
        <p>FRODSHA3f.</p>
        <p>DRUNIERNNE69.—Before Dr. Steele and Mr. Thomas at Frodsham Police Court on Tuesday, Lydia Feaney, of Ship-street, was fined ss. including costs for being drunk the previous night.</p>
        <p>FRODSIIAM MUSICAL SOCINTY.—This Society which was allowed to lapse last year, has now been re-formed, and the first rehearsal of the season was held on Tuesday evening. The Rev. H. B. Blow( has been appointed to the position of president of the Society; Mr. B. Rigby, treasury and secretary; and Mr. Birkett Musgrove, of Runcorn, the conductor. A number of glees were rehearsed on Tuesday, but it is intended to take at an early date Von Bree's " St. Cecilia's Day." A conversa- alone is to be held in December, and during the season several concerts will be given. Already about fifty members have joined the society. WISLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONS.—At Five Crosses Wes- leyan Chapel on Sunday, the foreign missionary anniver- sary was held. The Rev. A. Smith, of Didsbury. was the preacher. Special music was rendered, Mr. Joseph Berrington conducted the choir, and Mr. Basnett was the organist. The collections taken on behalf of the Society realised a satisfactory sum.—On Wednesday evening,the annual missionary meeting was held. A number. of addresses on the work of the Wesleyan Foreign Mission- ary Society, were given. THRI FATAL ACCIDENT TO AV OLD Maw.—Mr. H. ChUr- ton held an inquest at the Red Lion Hotel, Frodsham.na the body of Samuel Gilchrist, of Main-st., Frodsham, who died on the Wednesday previous.—Mary Elizabeth Nield said the deceased was her grandtather,and he was 87 years of age. He was deaf, but otherwise he was in possession of all his faculties. On the last Saturday in August he left home, and whilst rut met with an acci- dent. On being brought home he complained of having been injured on the left thigh. He did not blame any- one. Be died on Wednesday never having recovered from the shock.—Dr. Burton,gave it as his opiniot that death was due to general weakness., induced by the In- jury sustained bp the acciclent.—James Worsley, in the employ of Mr. John Smith,cab preprietor,Lowton,abated that on the 31st August he conveyed a party of pleasure seekers from B. dford Leigh. After spending the day on Overton Hills, they started back from the Ring o Bells shortly before seven o'clock. Oo reaching the top of the hill he found the brake would not act, and his horses trotted down the hill. Whilst descending the brow he saw an old man in the roadway, about a yard from the parapet. The cross-bar struck him and pushed him sut of the way. When he got to the bottom of the hill he returned to the eld man,and assisted to take him home He had been a driver for 10 yearsond never during that period had had an accident.—The jury retured a verdict of " Accidental death," and did not attribute blame to anyone.</p>
        <p>H A RVEST THANKSGIVING SERVICES. These services which were commenced on Thtirsday week were con- tinued on Sunday. The church had been beautifully decorated by the following ladies :—Mrs. Charles Rey- nelds, Miss Simpson, Miss Hitchens, Miss Elszelhurst, Miss Burton, Miss Ashtos,the Misses Linaker, the Misses Aston, Mrs. S. Davies, Mrs. Scott, Miss Edge, Mrs. Oek- leston, Mrs. Digr.:le, Mrs. Parkinson, Mrs. C. Linaker, Mrs. H.G.Linaker,Mrs. Holland, Miss Selby,Miss Lewis, Miss Roberts, Miss Smith, and Miss Hill, assisted by the Rev. A. E. Simms, Messrs. C. W. Linaker, R. Jones, and others. The decorative material was kindly supplied by lhe Rev. H. B. Blogg, Mr. Lewis (Biadley), Mr. Bate (Netherton), Mr. Frankish, Dr. Burton, Mrs. Hill, Mrs. Linaker,Mrs. C. Linaker, Mrs. Higginson, Dr.Selby,Mrs. Darlington, Mrs.Parkinson,Colooel Ashton,Mrs.Thomas, Miss Hayes, Mrs. Roberts, Miss Hitchens, Mr.Hern, Mrs. James Illidge, Mr.B. A. Righy,Mr. Hayes, Miss Simpson, Mrs. Timmins, Mr. Kydi, Miss Watson,and Mrs. Diggle. The Rev. H. B.Blogg was the preacher. The choir gave an excellent rendering of the anthem " Praise the Lord, 0 Jesusalem." Mr. R. Birkett Musgrove was the or- ganist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SHIP CANAL</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL</p>
        <p>ANO CHER MISHAP AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The steamer Comera,gb, bound from Waterford to Masichester,when passing Runcorn on Monday morning, failed to steer, and collided with several flats on the wall side. The Maggie was sunk,the Fanny and others being also damaged. To all appearances the steamer escaped without serious injury. The sunken flat did not inter- lere with the navigation of the canal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LAND NATIONALLSATION AND AG RI- C ULTU RAL DEPRESSION.</head>
        <p>LAND NATIONALLSATION AND AG RI- C ULTU RAL DEPRESSION.</p>
        <p>The Land Nationalisation Society has forwarded to the members of the Government a manifesto in which it expresses the conviction that the root of the present agricultural depression lies neither in foreign competl- tien, ignorance of new methods, nor the heavy charges of th.. railway companies, but rather in the landlord system itself. The memoralists therefore earnestly seek to impress upon the Government tha fact that there is only one way to get rid of the depression,and that is by the steady diminution and ultimate abolition of the pre- sent private monopoly of " the nation's land," with a view to establishing in its place a system of State owner- ship.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>W ESTON.</head>
        <p>W ESTON.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING FESTIVAL.—This annual fes- tival was held in Weston Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday and Monday. The chapel had been tastefully decorated with fruit and flowers, and great credit is due to the fol- lowing ladies and gentlemen for the excellent way they had performed their work Bunbabin, Mrs. J. Griffith, Miss Ford, Mrs. Norcott, Mrs. Dodd, Mrs. Somerville, Mr. Kirkham and others. Messrs. William Dnnbabin and John Griffiths contributed gifts of flowers and fruit. The preacher at the morning service on Sun- day was the Rev. T. Brackenbury, and in the evening, Rev. W. George. The choir rendered a special anthem at each service. A service was also held on Monday when the preacher was the Rev. G. D. Putron Gliddon. On Tuesday a fruit banquet and coffee supper were held in the schoolroom when a large number of friends were present. The school band rendered several selections, and the choir sang suitable harvest pieces and anthems.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>THREATENED STRIKE OF ENGINEERS.</head>
        <p>THE THREATENED STRIKE OF ENGINEERS.</p>
        <p>Several meetings of employers in the shipbuilding trade were held at Glasgow on Thursday in connection with the disputes at Belfast and on the Clyde. To a meeting of master shipbuilders representing both ports a proposal in the nature of a compromise was submitted by Mr. Knight, general secretary of the Boilermakers' Society. This was for an advance of 5 per cent on piece-work and a shilling advance to time workers, the advance to take place on January 1. The employers decided to concede the advance, postponing, however, I • the date of its taking effect until March 2, and stipulat- ing that the concession should be conditional on the con- tinuance of good trade. The employers' reply has now to be submitted to the men. A meeting of masters of engineering establishments at Belfast and on the Clyde was held, at which it was decided to request the Execu- tive Council of the Amalgamated Society of Engineers to delay the occurrence of the strike at BAfast for a week, in (fader that another conference b.tween theparties may take place. A third meeting of employers was held for the purpose of considering a proposal to federate the various shipbuilders' associations of the United Kingdom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST FURVEST THLTHISGIVING.-011 Sunday evening, a harvest thanksgiving service was held in the Primitive Methodist Chapel, which was deco- rated for the occasion. The preacher was Mr. C. Hill, of Runcorn. The anthems, "Consider the lilies," and "I will lift up mine eyes," were rendered by several members of the choir from Greenway-road Chapel, Run- corn. Mr. W. Gittins played the American organ. There was a good congregation, and a satisfactory col- lection was taken. On Monday night Mr. Hill preached again ; and after the service, he held a sale of the fruit and vegetables.</p>
        <p>HARVEST THANKSGIVING SERVICES AT THE PARISH CHURCH—The annual harvest thanksgiving services were commenced on Thursday week. The church was very beautifully decorated. The Preacher was the Rev. Canon Hull, vicar of All Saints' Church, Norhampton ; and his eloquent and impressive sermon was much ap- preciated by the large congregation, which had assem- bled in spite of the inclement weather. The services were continued on Sunday. The chief morning service was a choral celebration of the Holy Communion, when an interesting sermon was preached by the Rev. J. W. Gilbertson, of Daresbury. There was a very large num- ber of communicants. The children's service at 2 30 was attended by the scholars of the Hill school and Mission school, and the members of the adult bible classes. In the evening the church was crowded. The solos in the anthems were admirably rendered by Miss Wright.</p>
        <p>MISSIONARY MEETING AT TUB WESLEYAN CHURCH.— The annual meeting on behalf of Foreign Missions was held on Tuesday night, In spite of the inclement weather there was a good gathering. Mr. A. R. Nor- man, of Runcorn, was to have presided, but he sent :a letter enclosing a donation, and expressing his regret that owing to ill-health he could not be present. In his absence the chair was occupied by the Rev. W. Gorge. The report showed that last year the Runcorn circuit contributed £279 4s. 2d. to the funds of the society. Of this sum £36 15 was from Halton, the juvenile branch collecting £l9 lie. 6d. The Rev. J. D. Sutcliffe, of West 'Africa, who appeared in the picturesque costume of a Yoruba chief, gave an extremely interesting account of Ithe Yoruba country in which he had laboured. The Rev. C. de P. Gliddon thanked Mr.Sutediffe, in the name of the meeting, for his address, and wished him success 'in his work. The meeting expressed a wish that Mr. Gliddon should writa to Mr. Norman, conveying its aympatnies for him in his illneas,and thanking him for his donation. The choir sang the anthem "Sing unto God with a voice of thanksgiving." The tenor solo was rendered by Mr. A. Saunders. Mr. Sutton presided at 1 the organ. A gaod collection was taken. The meeting I was,throughout, full of interest and instruction,and the congregation went away feeling that the cause of Foreign Missions deserved their fullest sympathy and support.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PROPOSED ENLARGEMENT 9F RUNCORN BOARD SCHOOLS.'</head>
        <p>THE PROPOSED ENLARGEMENT 9F RUNCORN BOARD SCHOOLS.'</p>
        <p>A meeting of the School Management Committee of the Runcorn School Board was held on Tuesday after- noon, when the plans for the proposed enlargement of Greenway-road Board Schools were considered. Mr. Holme, architect, Liverpoel, was present. He has pre- pared in the plans submitted,for a junior boys' and girls' school, with accomm .dation for 164 boys and 164 girls ; 327 in all, the girls to be housad on the ground floor and the boys on the second floor. In the basement part of the building a swimming bath is propesed to be pro- vided, 42fc. by 18ft., 21t. Hin, deep at one end, and sft. 6in. deep at the other end ; a spring board, a bench, and 14 dressing boxes. At the west end of the basement it is proposed to provide a boiler house, space for a hot air pump,two w.cds, a small office,and a coal shoot. At the east end a clerk's office 18ft. by 12ft., by loft., a waiting-room 20ft. by 18ft. by 10Ft, is proposed to be provided. In order to provide light for the basement portion of the proposed new building, it is suggested that the first floor should be raised two feet above the ground level,and to use what are called borrowed lights. Accommodation is also provided for a caretaker and for a board room over the caretaker's rooms, which will provide accommodation for ten persons. A School Board entrance leading off Victoria-road is also shown on the plan. The Clerk's office is planned to be used as a committee room. The bath as at present planned will hold 19,000 gallons of water, and the deeper portion of it will probibly be below the level of the adjoining sewers ; but the greater portion of the discarded water can be used with advan- tage in flushing the sewers of the town' owing to the position which the baths will occupy. Dr. AlcDougall,the chairman of the School Management Committee, who has taken a prominent and praiseworthy part in pushing the project forward, contends that the remaining portion can with perfect ease, and at the small cost of a penny per day, be pumped into galvanized iron cisterns, lilted over the latrines, and used with marked benefit in flush- ing. The architects estimate el the cost of board room, waiting room, clerk's office. An., is £5OO, and surmming bath, pumps, Ac., £750 or £1,250 in all.—lt was deciaed that the plans should be submitted to the Board for con- sideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>on RY'S GRAND CIRCUS, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Great Success of the re-opening of this popular place of amusement. Everyone i • Warrington and District should see this great show OREA.T CHANGE o CONIPANY, MONDAY OCTOBER 7vit and every performance. First appearance of Miss SCOTT, the gr,atest Lady Horsewoman and bar,-luck Equestrienne In the world. First appearance of Mons. P. A. RANGE. First appearance of Mr. E. FIANNAPORD, with his stud of trained Horses and Ponies. First appearance of DOUGLAS AND SON, the wonders. Twenty other great Stars. All the Clowns headel by TONEY FELIX. PRICES of ADMISSION :—Front Malls (plush self-rai.ing chairs. numbered), 2s. . Back Chairs, Is. 6d. Side Seats, Is. Pit and Side Circle. 6d. Gallery, 3d. NOTICE.—To avoid the Crush Perions can enter by the Side Doors-2d. Extra to Gallery, 3d. Extra to all Seats in Front of the House.</p>
      </div>
