      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WIGAN MURDER..</head>
        <p>THE WIGAN MURDER..</p>
        <p>PRISONERS BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES. SENSAIIONAI, SCENE IN coma. CONFESSION BY WINSPANLEY.</p>
        <p>At the Wigan Borough Police Court on Thu.rsday three colliers, named Kearsley (alias Winstanley), Elijah Winstanley, and William Halliwell, who at the time of their arrest lived in Kay's-houses, Lower Ince, were charged on remand with the wilful murder of Detective sergeant Kidd, of the London and North-Western Rail- way police,at Wiganon the 29'h of last month.Halliwell was also charged with inflicting grievous bodily harm upon Detective William Henry Osborne on the same date. Mr. Kershaw, of London, instructed by Mr. Mason, Euston Station, prosecuted on behalf of the Treasury, and Halliwell was defended by Mr. Lees, of Wigan. Mr. Dix, the Mayor, presided over a full bench of justices. Mr. Kershaw, addressing the magistrates, said the prosecution had come to the conclusirm that they would not prefer a charge of murder against the prisoner . Halliwelf, and would ask that he be discharged on that count. Be would as, however, that Hailiwell should be committed, together with the other two prisoners, on the charge of doing grievous bodily harm to Detective Osborne. It was the intention of the prosecution to call Halliwell as a witness in the charge of murder:</p>
        <p>- Halliwell was then formerly discharged fr)m the dock.</p>
        <p>Upon the application of Mr. Kershaw, Halliwell was placed out of hearing. Just as the case was proceeding, Winstanley appPared to faint by the dock rail, and then fell into the arms of a constable. The painful scene continued, and in. the man's violent efforts to break away hysterically he said, "It's me; I didn't intend to kill him, but I did kill him." Dr. Roncroft fellowed into the dock and said he would te_better shortly. .</p>
        <p>Mr. Kershaw, resuming his opening statement, said that Robert Kidd entered the service of the London and North-Western Rsilway Company in 1885, was removed to Liverpool and Warrington, and at tne time of his death was stationed at Manchester. On this particdlar day he came to assist William Osborne' another officer stationed at Wigan. Depredations had been carried on for a period of two months. It was for the purpose of finding the persons concerned in these depredations that both officers journeyed to the gonds siding on the night in question. He then proceeded to review the evidence that would be given by Osborne, who was the first to meet Halliwell on his hands and knees. Osborne imme- diately caught hold of this man and wrestled with him whilst on the ground. He was kicked by a man who passed him, and a third man pushed him with something ' glittering like a knife iR the moenlight. Osborne was able to strike this third man on the head with his staff. There appeared to be a misapprehensiou as to the eight men. There only appeared to be three concerned. Hal- liwell stated that lie met the two men, Winstanley and Kearsley, at about six o'clock, in the New Inn public- , house. He had known both prisoners for some years. They left the New Ina for the Fox Tavern, and after that they went to the railway siding, and he was told by Kearsley to watch and see that nobody came, and it was whilst watching at this corner that Osborne came upou him and attempted to take him into custody. There was anether witness who was originally arrested, named Pritchard, who stated that they were together up to a quarter past seven. The two prisoners and Halliwell left together. Kearsley asked the landlady of the house for fourpencei with the object of getting Pritchard a drink. Kearsley said "Go and get a drink while we get a bag, of coal." He would also call anther wit- ness of an important character in the person of Kears- ley's daughter, who would state that Kearsley left the house between six and seven o'clock, and when he re- tured home that night she overheard something being said to his wife. This evidence would not be given against the prisoner Winstanlev, but against Kearsley,l and by that statement it would be proved that he knew a goose] deal about the murder at that time. Prit- chara would further tell them that he was one of the prisoners wearing other clothes than those he was wear- ing when he went to the New Inn. The men were ap- prehended, and had been identified by the witness Os- borne. When charged by Inspector Mackintosh prisoners replied that they were net there. William Henry Osborne was placed intim witness-box. A limp as he walked was the only outward sign of his recent terrible encounter. Detective-sergeant Kidd, he said, joined him at the Wigan Railway Station about eight o'clock on the 29'11, and they proceeded to the goods siding. They had not been there long when he saw Halliwell on his hands and knees. In reply to his question. "What do you want?" Halliwell shouted out, " Heigh up, lads. Have you got it 2" or" Have you got them ? "and ran away. He caught him, and a struggle commenced. They were both up and down several times, but ultimately he got his knee on to Halli well's chest, and his throat in his grip. Kearsley came up while they were in that position and kicked him on the knee, at the same time falling over. Kears- ley and he got up at the same moment, and then another man, something like the prisoner Win- stanley, came from around the waggons standing there. 'Whoever it was he had in his hand some- thing that shone in the moonlight like a piece of glass. Taking his staff out of his pocket he (Osborne) struck the man a blow on the wrist or fingers of his right hand. He shook his hand,and put it up to his mouth, as though it hurt him. Kearsley and the man like Win- stanley then ran away, and he foil iwed them for a few yards. Returning,he found Halliwell still on the ground. He tried to get on top of him, but Halliwell kicked him on the knee, and after further struggling got away with , his staff. He (witness) then went in the direction of where he had left Sergeant Kidd. He found that c.fficer between the wall and the siding and the waggons, on his hands and knees,and with blood running down his face. He said, " Osborne, is that you ? Get me a drink of water." Osborne tried to carry him to the signal-box, but when he lifted him up he could hear what he thought was blood running away from him. He succeeded in carrying the wounded man for some yards, when he fell from weakness with his burden to the ground. The next thing he remembered was being on an engine, and when he awoke to consciousness be was in the In- firmary. On the Monday morning following about six or seven men were brought to where he was lying, and from them he picked out Kearsley as one who was at the siding the previous night. The next day eight other men wereebrought in. From these he picked out Halliwell. On the Wednesday he was confronted with several other men, and after looking down the row he said, pointing to Winstaniey, "He's as near like the third man as any- thing." Be was of that opinion now. Kearsley said the witness was mistaken in saying he was there, and then asked, "What had I on? Clogs or shoes ?" Osborne said he could not tell. Winstanley said Osborne was a liar in stating that he struck him on the hand with his staff. _ _</p>
        <p>William Haiti well, who had turned Qneen's evidence, was then called. His entrance to the witness box caused quite a flutter of excitement in the court. He stated that on the 29th he was living at Kay's-houses. Lower (rice. He had known Winstanley about three years,and had been acquainted with Kearsley ever since he could recollect. On Sunday evening, the 29'h September, just before six o'clock, he went to the New Inn beer- house, Ince, in company with the two prisoners. They remained there ~bout an hour and three-quarters. Then they crossed the fields to go to the Fox Tavern. The London and North-Western Railway comes between the New lon and Fox Tavern. Trey all got over the fence on to the line, and went towards some waggons in a siding. Kearsley told him to stay at the wall side of the waggons and watch. After he had heen watching for about ten minutes he noticed two men— Osborne Bnd another. Osborne, saying" I've caught you now ; you little devil," sprang at him, and at the same moment the other man, whom he now knew to be , Kidd, rushed towards Kearsley add Winstanley. They were at that time on the waggon. He heard a bit of a struggle coming from the ' direction of the opposite side of the waggon, and that was all. While he (Haiti well) was struggling with Osborne nobody came up at all, arid he did not see the two prisoners again. As he ran away home, taking Osborne's staff with him, he heard the sound of another scuffi, but he could not say who it was between, as he did not look round. Later the same evening he went to the Fox Tavern, and saw both the prisoners. Winstanley was then wearing a hat instead of the cap he had seen upon him on the railway. The cap (produced) belonged to Winstanley. In the course of conversation Winstanley asked him how he had got on, and after relating his struggle with Osborne, told him what had occurred between Kearsley and him- , self and Sergeant Kidd. Winstanley said, "I don't think , the man I was agate of will live." The witness asked why, and Winstanley replied, "Because I stabbed him many a time." Halliwell said he bad done wrong, and left the beer-house. During their conversation Kearsley was about two or three yards away, and could not, he thought, hear what was said. Neither of the prisoners Raked Fialliwell any questions in cross-examination. The court then adj named for lunch. Amongst other evidence called was that of Elizabeth Kearsley, daughter of the prisoner. She appeared dis- tressed whilst under examination. Her father went out on the 29th ult., and returned later in the evening. Witness heard her father say to her mother there had been a fight. The exact words were "Our Elijah has been stabbing a bobby in the face and neck with a knife." Witness then came downstairs, and did not hear anything else. Her father afterwards went out. He sat on the step for a short time, and went round Westwood. Dr. Graham said he was called to the scene of the murder. He foetid Kidd already dead upon his arrival. A post-mortem later revealed nine incised punctured wounds on the head, face, and right side of the neck. There were also numerous abrasions and contusions on the head, face, hands, both elbows, left arm, and right knee. Three of tbe wounds on the right side of the neck injured large blood vessels in the neck. The in- juries were ouch that wculd be caused by a knife or sharp instrument. The outer side of the left index finger was cut off. There was no evidence of organic disease. The cause of death was due to syncope from hemorrhage, due to the wounds in the neck, the large blood vesaelsbeing injured. He also found a biolod trill at the scene of the murder. Kearsley and Winstanley werecommitted tc the Liver- pool Asszes on a charge of wilful murder. In reply to he usual challenge they had nothing to say.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>VISIT OF MR. W. H. JUDE TO</head>
        <p>VISIT OF MR. W. H. JUDE TO</p>
        <p>RUNCO RN.</p>
        <p>Mr.W. H. Jude,the well-known musician of Liverpool, provided the large audience which filled the Runcorn Public Hall on 'lhursday evening, with a rich musical treat. His programme was entitled " The Music of Humanity," and it was fulfilled in a manner which left nothing to he desired. It will be remembered that Mr. Jude'a visit was fixed for an earlier date, but at the last moment he was compelled to withdraw from the engage- ment owing to illness. In introducing himself to a Runcorn audience on Thursday night he apologised for • the occurrence. Mr. Jude was still somewhat indis- posed, but,nevertheless,his performance throughout was an unequivocal success. His well-chosen illustrations of the subjects he dealt with were executed with musicianly taste and feeling, and were listened to with unalloyed pleasure. At the outset he made some appreciable remarks on those matchless words which Handel has made immortal in his Messiah " Comfort ye, comfort ye my people." Proceeding,he touched upon his work amongst the poor of Liverpool. In dealing with national music, Mr. Jude referred to the effect it pro- duced on various peoples, and gave several instances which had come under his own observation in his travels abroad. He illustrated his subject by selections on the pianoforte from the works of itlcharwenka, and also by the" Marseillaise" of the French, and " Home sweet Home." Elia interpretation was a complete success in point of execution and serve, and rec:ived the plaudits of the audience. Fireside music bad a capital representation in Eliza Cook's beautiful piece " The Old Arm Chair the piquant points of the story being made the most of by Mr. Jude. In dilating upon modern pianoforte music as exemplified by Cornelius Gurlitt, the musician amus- ingly contrasted the old and new fashioned methods of teaching the pianoforte, and referred to the fine and ex- emplary music of Gurlitt. Speaking with regard to Christianity and music, he said no amount of technique would make up for must.: which Cearlle. from the heart. The lecturer here gave a capital rendering of a fine piece entitled " Man of Sorrows," of his own com- position. By request he followed with " The village blacksmith," in which his fins baritone voice was heard to advantage. The next item entitled "A Rustic scene in Brittany," was the gem of the evening. It consisted of a number of excerpts from Alex Guilmant played on the harmonium, which under the masterly handling of the musician, carried the audience in imagination to an old Brittany village where various instruments were to he heard piping to the morris and o her dances of the , good ,dd times. In response to vociferous apphuse, he complied with an encore, and again seating himself at the instrument, he further delighted the audience with an inimitable rendering of the "Campbells are coming," After referrin to Shakespeare on man's ingratitude to man,he followed with the ainging,wish fine effect, of the descriptive song " Blow, blow, thou winto- wind." An appreciative sketch of Acton Rubinstein, musician artist, and poet " was next given, the lecturer playing ieveral selections from his works. A particularly fine rendering of R rbinstein's introductory part to the " Deluge" was played. The child's birthday was the last item on the programme, and this was illustrated by the singing of Behrend's pathetic song " Daddy." The proceeds were in aid of the' St John's Presbyterian Church Building Fund.</p>
        <p>THR DEATH OP A SUPPOSED RUNCORN ENGINEES. In our last issue, we published a letter from Mr. Thomas Armstrong, of the steamer Colonna, San Francisco, California, in which he gave an account of an engineer named Mr. William Thomason who died from the severe injuries he received by the Ibureting of.a pipe on board the Cotonna on Noverri- ber 13,1894. Owing to the fact that a portion of a 1, Runcorn " Examiner" was found in a coat belong- ing to the unfortunate engineer Mr. Armstrong wrote to us in the hope of finding some of his rela• tires. It is interesting to state that early on Sat- urday morning, Mr. Thomason, Norman's-row-I.BAm- corn called at the " Examiner " office, and said that the deceased man was his son, of whom he had not heard for a long period. He had given him up for lost, thinking probably that he had been 111r6tirtted. The ticket mentioned in the letter was the ticket of inspection which had been given to his son to go on board toe " Lord Googn," and was handed over to Mr. Thomason, in accordancb with the request of the writer of the letter.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL—RI7NCORN V. LIVERSIIDGR. This match was played at Liversedge o,i Wednesday. In the first half, passing by Wood and Barker ended in Thornton making a fine run, which enabled Jenkinson to score for Liversedge, Sharpe improving thf try. The Runcorn backs then got well through, and F. Guyter scored, Dunbavin kicking a goal. Nothing was scored In the second half, the game ending in a draw-5 points each.</p>
