      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>UNREGISTERED LODGING-HOUSES.</p>
        <p>Mary Cartledge, 27,Turner-st „ was charged with keep- ing lodgers in a house which was unregistered, on sept. 29. Inspector Powell said that on the above date he went to the house and found it occupied by eight persons, two of a family and six lodgers. The house was only adapted for six persons.—lnspector Norman said that he could mot register the house as it was too small.—Defendant said that she had lived in that house 13 years.—The magistrates decided to adjourn the case for a fortnight to give defendant an opportunity of getting another house. Timothy Monaghan, 22, Ship-yard, was charged with committing the same offence on Sep. 29. Inspector Powell said that there were 12 persons in the house which was only adapted for five.—lnspector Norman I said that six weeks ago he told Mrs. Monaghan that she could not keep lodgers in that house as it was not , adapted for a lodging-house. She then promised that she would not keep any.—Defendant said that the peo- ple complained of in the house, were relatives.—The defendant was fined ss. and costs. John Murray, 14, Ship-yard, was fined 59. and costs, for committing a similar offence on Sept. 29. Inspector Powell said that there were eight persons in the house which was only adapted for four. A FREZ FIGHT BETWERN A LANDLADY AND HER LODGERS. At the Borough Court, on Friday, Ellen Pook sum- moned Harriet Barrett, with whom she lodged at 3, Leonard-street, for assaulting her on October Ist. Mrs. Pook, who appeared in court with a black eye, said that she lodged with Mrs. Barrett who kept her on the under- standing that she (Mrs. Pook) paid the rent. On Tues- day, the defendant demanded the money for the rent, but witness refused to give it her, saying that she would give it to Mr. Bostock, the landlord. Mrs. Barratt thereupon struck her and took her pirse off her. She then said " twill spoil your pretty face,"and commenced to strike her all over. She got her purse back later on. Cross-examined : She never struck Mrs. Barratt with a knife—Mrs. Barratt admitted striking Mrs. rook, but said that it was in self defence. It was a fight between them. Mrs. Pook took up a knife at the commence- ment of the affray and wounded her in the breast and fingers. Witness told her not to use knives. She could use fists as much as she lieed.—Witnesses were called 00. both fsides,and the Bench deckled to bind defendant over for six months in one surety,and ordered him to pay ss. costs, although they considered that Mrs. Pook was blameless in the matter.</p>
        <p>not</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>MINOR CASES. – WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES. - WARRINGTON BOROUGH COURT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (chairman), Alderman Harrison, Mr. F. Monks. DRUNIENNESS.—For this offence, James Morgan, 10 Friars' Green, was fined ss.including costs : Ann Cottoe, Scotland-road, discharged ; Catherine Richardson, 38 Haydock-street, one month ; Annie Atherton, Pinners- Brow, fined in the costs ; Julia Coleman. Bridge-street, buildings 23. 6d. ; Elizabeth Kearns, 16 Silver-square. ss. and costs ; Mary Preston, 2. 6d. and Annie Hart, Silver-square, 51. ; Thomas Mitchell, 16 Hall-street, ss. and costs. - - -</p>
        <p>AN ABSENTEE FROM TRAINING.—John Ahern Was re• mended on the charge of being an absentee from the training of the Third Liverpool militia. TRANSFERS.—The following transfers were granted : Henry Hughes, to the Star and Garter from Thomas Mullins;Thomas Mullins to the Old House at Home,from George Crawford ; George Vernon to off license, 14 and 18 Powys-street, from James Potter ; Mrs. Green, to the Shakespeare Inn, from William Green ; John Shaw, to Little Horse Shoe, from George Edward Shaw ; Joseph Percival to Strangers Arms, from John Shaw. A DANGERODS Doo.—Richard Tranter, 2 Warhurter- street, was charged with being the owner of a dangerous dog, not under proper control. P.C. Meacack said that the dog had bitten five persons dur- ing the last twelve months. On the above date, the dog WM so insecurely muzzled, that witness could put his finger in the dogs mouth. Mrs. Hudson was called and said that her arm had horn torn some time ago by the dog. The defendant denied that it had done any more that snap at anyone with the exception of Mrs. Hudson. He had destroyed the dog since the issuing nf,the sum- mons. Mrs. *Hudson sal i that she knew that the dog was destroyed, and the bench then dismissed the case on payment of the costs, 134. 6d. OVERDRIVING A HORSR.—Frank Harrison, 8 Ells- tock-street, Joseph Whitlow, 10 Baxter-street, George Whitlow, 10 Baxter-street, and William Smith, 14, Baxter-st., were all charged with illtreating a horse on Sept. 29, by beating and over- driving it. Harrison was also charged with be. ing drunk in charge of a horse and cart in Berfoot-st.,on the same day.—Jonathan Broadhurst, Brastock-st.,green- grocer, said that he lent the horse and cart to the four defendants on the above date, and at about a quarter to twelve they returned, and he then saw that it was very fatigued and over-driven. It was seven years of age and was a large horse not used to running. —Walter Pickering, MB, Winwick-road, said that he saw the de- fendants coming along the road, and the horse was in such a state that it could hardly walk,and seemed ready to drop down. Joseph Whitlow and Wiliam Smith were basting "it with sticks. They were the worse for drink.—Another witness said that Harrison was so drunk that he could do nothing to the hor e, hut he cor- roborated the last witness; with regard to the other de- fendants.—P.S. Rowhottom and P.C. Lewis also gave evidence and said that when they examined the horse it Was in a very fatigued state. There was a large num- ber of weals on the horse's body, and it took 35 minutes for it to walk from Winwick-st. to Bostock-st., a dis- tance Ma mile, with the empty cart. Harrison was fined 105. and costs for illtreating the horse, and ss. and costs for being drunk, a total of £1 125., and the remainder 10s. and costs. a guinea in all. Battacs OF TIIH PEACIL—James Bibby, 64,Newsroad, and James Taylor,227,Knutsford-road,were charged with fighting in St. James-st , on Sept. 21.—P.S. Rowbotharn proved the case. It was shown that Taylor was the aggressor, and he was bound over for six months. Bibby was dismissed. the Ma7or saving that Taylor ought to pay his day's wages for bringing him there. The bother was over a eame of football. Yorrryreur. GAMOLEas.—inhn Baguliav (18),Bakehouse- , yard, George Dickenson (15), 1 Chapel-et., John Fogg I (13), 79, Birchall-5t., Thomas Higham (14). 66, Turner- Rt.. and William Savage (13). 34, 0 iver-5t., were all charged with gaming with cards in BPWROV St., on Sept. 28. All but Savage pleaded guilty.—P.C.Beecham proved the case and with reference to Savage, he said he was holding the cards. It was ~ventually decided to dismiss the case against Savage and Fogg, who had not been previously brought before the magiqrates. Higharn and Dickenson were fined as. 6d., as they had been pre- viously convicted, and BaguleY who seemed to be in- corrigible, waa fined 40s. and costs or a month.—Wil- liam Jones (17). 1, Gilbert-st John Morris (17), 11, Mason-sr., and James Parry (16). 41, Greenall-st., were i fined 21. 61. f•d• committing the same offence in Elles- mere-st., r n Sept. 21.—P.C. 13,nry proved the case.</p>
        <p>WEDNISDLY.—Before Mr. John Fairclough (chairman), and Mr. J. Geddes. TIRED or THE ARMY.—John Fawcett was charged with being a deserter from the Ist Battalion Yorkshire Regi- ment, stationed at Curragh Camp, Ireland, since the 4th October.—P.C. Baxendale proved apprehending the pri- oner who was cv.aring a military shirt and socks.—The magistrates remanded prisoner to await a military escort and granted the usual reward to the officer for his cap- ture. _ .</p>
        <p>WIFE ASSAULT.—John Flenry Kelly, Crossley-st., was summoned for assaulting and beating his wife, Martha, on the 24th September.—Mrs. Kelly, whose left eye was badly bruised,stated that on the morning of the day in question she roused her husband and asked him to go to work,whereupon he jumped up and thrashed her, black- ing her eye.—Defendant said that his wife had seized the poker and ordered him out of the house and in his attempting to take the poker from her she had hurt her eye herself.—Mr. Taylor, police court missionary, said, in answer to the bench, that he had visited the defend- ant's house on Monday and found that he had turned his wife out of the house. Complainant was, he said, a hard working woman, but he could say nothing for the defendant.—The Chief Constable also gave the woman a good character.—Defendant after being severely repri- manded by the bench, was bound over to keep the peace for six months in two sureties of £5 and himself in £lO or go to gaol for one month. THUBSDAY.—Before Alderman Sutton (chairman), an Alderman Wright. HUSBAND AND W IFE AT VARIANCE. —Henry Jewess was locked up on a warrant to show cause why he should not be committed to prison for the non-payment of £3 55., arrears for the maintenance of his wife, Sarah Ann Jewess.—Mr. Browne represented complainant and mentioned that not long ago she obtained from the magistiates an order for defendant to pay her 10i. per week. Defendant could make Is. per day, but since the order he had not paid her a penny and he had said that he would not do so,as she had a legacy to fall hack upon —Mr. Forshaw (Davies and Forshaw) appeared for the , defence and said that defendant only earned 30s. per I week and only worked six months in the year. More- over he had to keep three children, and complain- . ar.II none. The magistrates had full power to vary the I order and under the circumstances Mr. Forshaw applied I for it to he reduced to ss.—Complainant said that one of the childrel wa% 19 and be kept himself, while Mr. Browne mentioned that complainant was in the care of ! a Mrs. Kirkham and had left off her intemperate habits. —Mr. Forshaw then intimated that defendant was will- ing to have his wife back again. The complainant, however, had no faith in her husband behaving pro- perly towards her, so the magistrates adjourned the case for a fortnight, defendant to pay the costs, 15a., in the meantime and do what he could to support his wife. If they came together of course the order was nullified.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN PETTY SESSIONS</p>
        <p>MONDAY.-4WO-1:;ill:. E. Imison (chairman), and Mr. S. Taylor. LICENSES TRANSFERRED.—The magistrates approved of the following transfers. No objections were raised. Mersey Vaults from Samuel Houghton to Alfred Wid- ders ; Swan Inn, Shaw-st., from John Lyon to James Bourne; Holyhead Flarbour,from Luke Holmes to Henry John De Korte ; New Inn, Church-Rt., from George Mason to William Anderton ; Anchor Inn, Brunswick-st. from William Anderton to James Mutch. DRONICENNES —James Robinson, labourer, Nelson-st., was fined ss.including costs for being drunk on the 28th inst. in Church-st. FURIOLIS DRIVING.-rJaMeS Price, fisherman, Weston, was charged with furiously driving a horse and cart at Runcorn on the 24th September.—Defendant pleaded guilty.—P.C. Booth testified that the defendant was driving at a great rate.—The Chairman said it was a most dangerous practice, and a fine of 53. and costs would b., imposed. A SLEEPY DRIVER.—John Starkey, of Kingsley, was summoned for being asleep whilst in charge of a horse and cart at Balton on the 18th September.—P.C. Juett stated that on the night of the date named he net the defendant in charge of a horse and cart at Halton vil- lage. The horse was travelling from one side of the road to the other. He saw that the defendant was asleep and woke him up. On asking him where he was going to,he replied Kingsley. He was at the time travelling in toe direction of Runcorn.—A fine of ss. and costs was im- posed. A WARRINGTON DRIVER IN TROUBLE.—TOtuI Efickey,of 58, Golborne-street, Warrington, a carter, was summoned for driving without reins in Balton-road, Runcorn, on the 26th ult —P.C. Richardson stated that on the date mentioned he saw the defendant riding on a lorry drawn by two horses. There were no reins attached to the horses. On witness stopping him, he stated that he had had to use them to fasten the load on the lurry.—The defendant denied that he was riding on the lurry. He had only just jumped on to get his dinner. Be was fined ss. and costs.</p>
        <p>—„..— WALKING INTO THE LION'S DEN —TAMPS Williams, a tramp, who said he was on his way to Liverpool, was ' charged with begging alms in Bold-street on the sPli inst.—P.C. Richardson informed the bench that the prisoner came to his house, and asluzid for assistance. He noticed that he was far gone in drink. Witness brought him to the Police Station, and an searching him found 21. in his possession. In his bag,he had quite a tramp's outfit. He was committed to gaol for seven days. Anonms Dois OWNER FINED. —William Peacock,boat- man, was summoned for allowing his dog to be at large unmuzzled in High-street on the 18Ph September. P.C. Booth proved the case, and a fine of ss. including costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MINING AND FINANCIAL NEWS. --- EAST WEALTH OF NATIONS GOLD MINES, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>MINING AND FINANCIAL NEWS. --- EAST WEALTH OF NATIONS GOLD MINES, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>The East Wealth of Nations Company is very little be- hind its neighbour in rn-lintaininp; and increasing the reputation of that part of the Coolgardie Gold Field. Cable news from the local management just received. states that the property is developing well, both in the 100 feet level, where the reef has gone on increasing in size, until it is now eft. of crushing stuff, all carrying fine gold, and at the surface, where a large stratum of good alluvial cement is Apening up, of great value, and equal to the high expectvion formed or it by Captain Thomas. The East Wealth of Nations can no longer be deemed a " prospect," as the developments of the last three months have turned not so fortunately, in dis- closing a vast body of highly payable stone, that the Mine is now able to keen a large battery going,were such available, and which we understand will soon be the the case. (Financier, October 3rd, 1895.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>HANNAN'S NORM MINE.</head>
        <p>HANNAN'S NORM MINE.</p>
        <p>..... This is a new and promisins; West Australian gold mining venture. It has been issued by the International Trust and Finance Company Limited and so well are its p. ospects thought of that it was at once snapped up by some of the best people in the Stock Exchange. Con- sequently no public pmspectus will be issued but as the shares will beyond all question, have shortly an active market, some particulars which we are in a Position to furnish will be urged to our readers. This property is situated in the Flannan's district between the blacks known as the "Eureka and the Golden Z.me," and con- sists of about 24 acres. It is an important point that a very rich reef has been found on the immediately con- tiguous Eureka property ; there, at a depth of 60 feet, a trial crushing of two tons has returned 18 ,z. Toe shaft on Hannan's North has ben sunk to a depth of 50 feet. and the indications there found are described as very favourable, and as justifying the assumption that' with further development the property will prove very valu- able." The property has been reported upon by several experts of character and position, including Captain Oates and Mr. W. O. Brookman.</p>
        <p>That the Flannan's North property 14 auriferous cannot be doubted after such testirnany as this ; and we are not surprised, therefore, that in view of the great interest which is being felt in the Flannim's distric', and of the hir,h premium at which most of the mines in that loca- lity now stand, the Stock Erchange has promptly Ab- sorbed the liannan's North mine. The paren'age of the undertaking is well accredited, the property itself emirv•ntly good, and the capital reasonable, namely, £BO.OOO. Of this amount £20,000 has been set aside to provide working capital. It is said that the shares are likely within a few days to he (voted ol the Stock Ex- change in the neighbourhood of to premium, and that there is a good prospect of them quickly reaching a much higher price.—From "Money Market Review," October sth, 1895.</p>
        <p>The report of Lieut.-Colonel Yorke to th, Board of Trade on the colliAinn at Driehlington Station of the Great Northern 'Railway attributes the accident to the carelessness of the driver of the empty train. At the Liverpool County Court on Wednesday, a dock labourer named James Ryan was awarded £l5O and costs under the Employers' Liability Act against John Marreion, stevedore, as compensation for injuries caused by the falling of a ship's beam. Mr. Gladstone, replying to a correspondent as to shorter hours for public-houses, remarks that he com- monly. if not always, voted for measures of restriction when in Parliament, He is now desirous to avoid pro- nouncing opimions, lest he should interfere with the proceedings of those responsible, and because his means of information are scanty.</p>
        <p>' " DISFIGURED FOR LIFE" is the despairing cry of thousands afflicted with unsightly skin diseases. Do • you realise what this disfiguration means to sensitive Souls ? It means isolation, seclusion. It is a bar to social and business success. Do you wonder that despair seiv4i upon these sufferers when doctors fail, standard remedies fail, and nostrums prove worse than use:ess ? Skin diseases are most obstinate to core or even relieve. It is an easy matter to claim to cure them, but quite another thing to do so. COTICIIRA remedies have earned the right to be called Skin Specifics, because for years they have met with most remarkable success. There are cases they cannot cure, but they are few indeed. It is no long-drawn-out. expensive Experiment. One ' shilling invested in Curia/se SOAP will prove more cons ' vincing than a page of advertisement. In short, CUTICURA WORKS WONDERS, and its cures of tor- turing, disfiuring, humiliating skin diseases are simply marvellous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895.</p>
        <p>get into the open and the visiting three-quarters passed well until the pass to the outside lett width went for- • ward. The advantage gained was neutralized by a fine judicious bit of play on the part of Heesom. Then came : the home quartette's turn. A lot of ground was covered but the ruu finished up with a fast low pass from Burton which Foster failed to hold. The pressure was not relaxed and to ease off Smethurst had to kick dead. Barber cleverly made his mark from the exchange which followed and once mere scrimmages on the Rangers line. were the order. These brought out the thoroughness with whicn the home forwards went for the scrimmages and also brought out the tine play of Nevins and Hough- ton at half back who completely overplayed Garrity and Quigiy. This was particularly gratitying to the specta- tors who were loud in explanations (apparently intended chiefly for Gatti cy'e ears) of" We'd show, we'll show." \Viten scrimmages were becoming too frequent Burrows starred a passing, movement which was taken up by Burton and Foster, the latter being rushed into touch when within a yard or two of scoring point. Nevins, who was playing in splendid form, was at once in evi- dence in the ensuing seri mimeo au i i forced his way to the line with the ball in his possession. Unfortunately for him no one was at han Ito accept his pass when col- lared and the ball was iii%ercepted by a Broughton man and sent clear. Foster prevented it from travelling far and from his kick Brouel-.ton Rangers hail to concede another minor. Wa,r ngtoo therea frer experienced their first danger. It. *tame 5h ‘e• through fine passing which came off succesatal. end teeir enmed a fine race between Burton and fl ckni. the Former cutting sharp across the fiold to irvereept, 1)IA p ts.a. lir ought off a mart tackle and the glengee was saved. Exchange kick- ine followed this spurt but Heeglizell. Nevins, Barton, and Foster soon hal the ball b .elt and Barrett pet the finishing touch by planting :t try in what seemed an easy pled.. Burton failed at the kick. He made a clever drop kick lot goal a mi • utts or so later but failed to get tinder it. sat i+ lick was charged down. N elan daunted lie was directly again in pos• en on and almost reached the coverted line. Foster was more successful for he scored from a press by Houghton. erns made a poor attemnt it. gaal from a very easy place. Ts was eln-e- bslf- time, Warrington 2 tries. Broughton Rangers nil. Ii the opening of the second half the visitors appeared to better advantage. Barber,who had hitherto had tittle to do was set to work through passes from Foster and Burton. He was how- ever hedged in before he got speed up and heexperienced hard lines. Things settled down comfortably in the Rangers territory and it looked at this stage as if War- rington would pile on the agony. Burrows was in evid- ence with a strong individual effort which was met by short pasees.by the visitors who thereby regained ground. When things were at the hottest, Burton intercepted a , nags and get clean away from the ruck. He passed all except Smethurst but in Moiling him he dodged into Clemenson's arms and was made fast. Houghton and Barber supnlemented Burtnn's,.ffnrt and the scrimmagers were again at work Remind Smethurst whose downfall seemed certain. A minor was all the meagre satisfaction obtained, but nevertheless the spectators heartily reenenisel the indieidual and combined exhibi- tion which had hen shown. The Ringers from this point onwards were a beaten teem playing entirely on the defensive. Before the finish Barber had twice hard luck in not scoring and Hill made a good bid for goal from a penalty. One or two fine passing displays were also witnessed, while the effective work of the home for- wards was even more pronounced at theclose than it was at the opening of the game. The result which was the means of raising Warringten considerably in the League table was :—Warrington, 2 tries (plus a baker's dozen of Minors) ; Broughton Rangers. nil.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" V. RUNCORN " A ."—The above teams placed on the Wilderspool ground on Saturday. during a continuous shower. The ball was kicked off by Runcorn but Warrington soon returned it to the visitors 25. The viaitors, however, did not let it re- main there long, hut rushed it up the field, and got the ball in a dangerous position, but Lawless relieved and took the ball into safe quarters. Carey got close to the visitors' goal when the hall was taken from him and returned, falling near 'Laotians who kinked back, West saving by a splendid run up the fill. A scrimmage soon afterwards took place in the Runcorn 25. and Foden getting pqaaeasion of the ball kicked anc snored a drop goal, being loudly cheered for it. Pendlebury made a dash for a try but Mannion arrested his progrembut the ball was still in a dangerous place. I-. was worked back to the centre line where West got it and ran up the field with it and scored a try, which Lightfoot converted, Several other attempts were made by both sides to score but the full backs prevrntel anything more being dose during the first hall. On resuming play the Runcorn team exhibited some fine passing and West got away with the ball but he ran too far over the goal line, and tie try was nr,t allowed. The visitors made some bold rushes which resulted in nothing, until Gayter scored second try for the visitera. This try waa nicely con- verted by Lightfoot. The home team tried hard to change the vic'ory but neither side gained anything further at the call of time. The following men played : Runcorn : Fill back, Hankinson ; threccriarters,Gayter. Lightfoot. West and Richardson ; half backs, Pendle• bury and Rrereton ; forwards. Tsvlor, Urmson, Clarke, Fatlock, Shaw. boughton, Jrnes and F. Richardson. Warrington : Fu'l bark. Mtnnion ; threequarters, Cross, Isherwood, La wleaa, and Carey ; half backs, Foden and Price; forwards, Taylor, Thorniley, Savage, Hughes, Higham, Freeth, Scatt,aad Randalla.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION. A meeting of representatives of Lancashire and Cheshireclubs in the Northern Rugby Union was held at the Spread Eagle Hotel, Manchester, on Tuesday even- ing. Mr. J. E. Warren (Warrington) presided, and amongst thcse present were Messrs. E. Gresty (Brough- ton Rangers). J. Quirk (Leigh), J. H. Smith (Widnes), Brierley (Rochdale Hornets), Carroll (Runcorn), Mills (Stockport), Taylor (Tyldesley). Wardle (Wigan), Den- nett (S,. Helens). and J. Platt (Oldham). The meeting was chiefly occupied in considering the competition laws as suggested by the special Sub-committee appointed to prepare the alma. A sugges,ion was adopted that clubs should nominate representatives to serve on the Compe- tition Committee, and it was also decided that every club in the Union should furnish to the next meeting a list of playing members, with a view to registration. Messrs. Grestv, Brierlev. Mills.and Platt were appointed a sub-committee to make all necessary arrangements in connection with the Lancashire v. Cheshire match on the 21st inst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
