      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY. THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Runcorn on Saturday, and it is a thousand pities that such miserable weather should have come to spoil the fixture. The teams met in home and borne matches last year for the first time for a great number of year., the dispute over the old West Lanca- shire Cup being then settled. Judging from the charac- ter of the contest on Saturday it is very unlikely that any further discord will rage between the two clubs, for the game was of the most friendly character from beginning to end. The rain which came dawn in torrents during the whole course of the game was most unfortunate and the loss to Runcorn was no doubt of a very serious character. The Runcorn officials were looking to this match to helping their exchequer considerably, arid a loss of £3O is estimated to have been sustained. Instead of a tremendous crowd at the time of kicking off, only about 1,500 wet and miserable looking individuals had braved the elements,but these at times made their voices heard. Mr. Slevin of Wigan, was the referee, and the following players were under his control : Warrington : Evans, full back ; Barber, Burton, Heesom and Broady, three quarters ; Nevins and Hough- ton, half backs ; Burrows, Saunders, Barrett, Andrews, Thornton, Fairhurst,.Wilcocks,and Hill, forwards.</p>
        <p>Runcorn : Jolley, 'full back; Warder, Dunbavin,Myers and Robinson, three-quarters ; Abrams and J. Faulkner, hali-backs ; W. Faulkner, Farmer, Langley, Williams, Gayter, Johnson, Walker, and Lightfoot, forwards. Barber lost the toss, but this was of very little come. quence as there was very little wind. Nevins kicked off and Warder failing to return, a scrimmage took place at tha quarter flag. From some loose play, Runcorn drib- bled to half way. Passing between Farmer and Robinson was spoiled by the latter knocking forward and a good chance was lost. Play new settled in midfield, and upon Runcoin attempting to get away, Rill proved a stumbling block, and Nevins got the ball away only to see it well returned by Jolley. Abrams broke away, and passing to Farmer, the latter was tackled by Fairhurst just as he was getting dangerous. From the ensuing scrimmage, W. Faulkner attempted dribble on his own account, but was upset by Heesom, and floug,hton securing and passing to Barber the latter kicked well, and Jolley returning poorly, a great amount of ground was gained. From the lino out Saunders took the ball well and Barrett receiving, he put his side further on the aggressive by kicking well into touch. After further loose play.. Nevins made a grand dribble, and caused play to settle down well in Runcorn quarters. Rushes by Runcorn forwards were well stopped succes- sively by Barber and Thornton,and Burton also got in a good kick when surrounded by his opponents. Runcorn now recovered themselves and rushed along the field, ' and just as a try appeared certain, Heesom pluckily dropped on the ball, and thus frustrated their efforts. A somewhat prolonged attack succeeded, and Warring- ton had their work cut out to keep the invaders out. Heesom was especially prominent by his splendid tack- ling. Relief came bye kick to midfield by Hill, but immediately afterwards, the referee penalised Barber on the ground that he did not play the ball. A place kick in an easy position was taken by Dunbavin, but the ball went wide of the posts, and Burton easily staved off the danger by a long kick into touch. From tha line out and scrimmage, Andrews dribbled well down the field, and Houghton's subsequent kick was so well followed up by Nevins, that Jolley failed to return it, and he was tackled by Fairhurst, Wileocks, and Hill within easy distance of bis own stronghold Runcorn reg lined their lost ground, and a bad pass from Heesom to Burton almost let Run- corn in, Evans kicking over the dead ball line, and a five yards scrimmage was ordered. Houghton gsve his side relief by a good kick in touch, but passing between Dunbavin, Myers, and Robinson put Runcorn on the offensive again, and the latter was only just tackled by Broady in time. A grand dribble headed by Thornton, Wilcocks, and Fsirhurst gave Runcorn much anxiety, arid Jolley had great difficuly in putting an end to it. W. Faulkner soon afterwards unaccountably mulled the ball , and missed an almost certain score. After several ex- changes, another dribble took nlace, headed by Hough- ton, Wilcocks, Saunders, and Thornton, and the ball was taken right to the other end of the field. Runcorn al- most immediately returned the compliment, and Dun- bavin getting the ball passed just at the right moment to Robinson who gut over just outside the goal posts. Dunbavin's kick just went inside the posts, and Run- corn were five points ahead. Burrows' kick off was more of the association character, and Runcorn were soon attacking again. Evans at length received the ball just on the line, and unaccountably attempted to hick the ball although there were three Runcorn players al- most on him. The kick was charged down, and Abrams had nothing to do but drop en the ball, and another try was scored. Dunhavin's kick went wide, and immedi- ately afterwards the whistle blew for halftime, Runcorn lead.ing by. eight points to nil.</p>
        <p>- W. Faulkner re-started the game after the interval, and Andrew returned and play settled down in the centre. Houghton at length passed to Barber, who made a magnificent run to within a yard of the line where he had the ill-luck to find Jolley in his way, and Runcorn escaped by a very narrow shave, as they did many times this half. Runcorn were kept defeneliag, and attempts to break away were frustrated by Evans and Saunders. At length Warder broke away and supplementing a run with a kick, Evans made his mark under difficulties. His kink was returned by Jolley, and alternate kicks between these two ended in favour of the former. The scrimmage which followed the line out was carried in a surprising fashion by Warrington, who literally fora d their way right through Runcorn, and by main force they carried the game from near quarter to mid field. Another scrimmage here took place, and a pass from Houghton was smartly taken by Broady who was off like shot with practically a clear field. Great was the chagrin of Warrington when the referee called him back on the ground that the pass was a shade for- ward. But for this there was no one to stop Broady. Warrington still Parried the scrimmages in fine style, and Houghton was t icl:led by Jolley in a very danger- otiq position. Warrington were at this point of the game pressing hotly, and a fine passing and dribbling move- ment between Nevins, Houghton, Wilcocks, Thornton, and Fairhurst NOM just prevented from having a tangible result by Jolley kicking !n touch just on the line. A determined attack was made and Houghton was brought to mother earth on the line. A fine kick by Abrams at length ended the attack and Evans stayed a dangerous rush in fine style. Runcorn were now determined to , get the upper hand, and Barber saved just under the , goal posts. Evans and Burton were the means of raising this attack,and Warrington again were on the offensive. A free kick across the field, taken by Broady, could have been turned to good account had Barber been up in time. Warden only just secured in time, and he gave Runcorn an opportunity which they took of again attacking. A dribble of Runcorn forwards headed by Farmer and Gayter was stopped well by Nevins other- wise disaster to Warrington would have occurred. The closing stages of the game were slightly in favour of Runcorn. and an amusine circumstance occurred very near the close. W. Faulkner's jersey had been the ob- ject of so much attention that there was -very little of it left.and he procured a new one. Just as he was putting it or., and had both arms enfolded in the jersey.prepara- tory to putting it over his head, the ball came in his direction. Encumbered as he was, he managed to hold the hill, and by a tremendous punt sent it back, and then had the opportunity of putting on his jersey in peace. The whistle bew soon afterwards, and Runcorn were leading by a goal and try to nil just as they were at th,. interyai. They had undoubtedly the best of the °am' before the interval, albeit, the last try was very lucky, bur after the interval, Warrington held the upper hand for by far the greater portion of it.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON V. BROUGHTON RANGERS.—PIayed At Warrington on Monday under lavourahle weather' con- ditions. Teams : Warrington:—Full back,ffeesom ; three- I qdarters,Barher (captain),Cross,Burton,and Foster; half- hacks, Nevins and .toughton ; forwards, Hurmws, S +un- ders,Andrews, Barrett,Thnrnton. Fairhurst, Wilet..sck,and 11111. Brooghton Rangers : Full back, Smethurst ; three-quarters, Berry, Chapman, Barran, and Hackin ; half-hack, Garrity' and Quiglv ; fo,wards. Clemenson, Deakin, thompson, Fitton, Clapham, Worthing- ton, and Rix. Referee, Mr. .1. Smith, Widnes. Sides. men, Messrs. Jones (Iltincorn),and Underwood (Wigan). The Rangers played three " A" team men in Smethurst, Flackin and Rix, and thus neither team could claim to he representative, It was the first home appearance of Warrington si,ce its re.organisation, and much specula. tion was entertained AS to how they would shapedts the resolt shows th-v shaped very avelr,indeed the game had not been very long in Vogue when every confidence in them was felt hy the onlookers. Half an hour after advertised time the visitors set the hag in motion, Cross was the first to handle and to foot the ball, his kick travelling fast and far. The forwards followed well op and Cross, Burton and Foster. had a round of passing which further improved Warrington's initial move. The question uppermost in the minds of the spectators was bow would the forwaids compete with their heavier and more experienced opponents ? Two , scrimmages in quick succession showed up our home eight in a favourahle light for they worked them welt. Garrity for palpahle rd-side in his tackling of Nevins give a penalty agAinAt Ii aid... This sass entrusted to Burton whose kick was cistropd down. The same player, however, caught the rebound and set off, top speed, only to be collared when nearing the line. A furtive breakaway by the visitors was rather easily checkmated by the home defence and play returned to the vi,dtors 25. After a series of serimmages, in all of which the home forwards proved the better lot, the ba'l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>LIVERPOOL LEEK V. WARRINGTON.—The first team journeyed to LivetTool on Saturday to meet the Leek club. The weather, unfortunately, Was altogether against good football, and the ground, owing to the heavy rains, was in a very slippery condition. Matters were not improved by the absence of Webster and Lina- ker ; the latter having met with a slight accident, could not turn out, Leek kicked off and had slightly the best of the owning, but Shnond atopned the rushed, and Sel- by getting possession passed to T. Matter at the right moment, the ball eventually reaching Connor, who at once made tracks, but was beaten by F. T. Williams, who quickly put Foster in possession. A successful appeal for a foul throw against Smithson brought relief to the Leek defence. Marshall rEspnnded, and from his kirk Connor raced away and a score seemed certain, but the ball went over the line S.ill attacking, successive corners were forced until Aitken gave his side relief, and a brief visit was paid to Warrington territory, Owen'a shot going wide. A corns bitted movement by the Warrington forwards Inoked had for Leek until an appeal for hands Was answered in the affirmative. Jones send the leather well down the field, and Wilson aeain had an anxious time, Walls just miss- ing with a daisy cutter. Leek again got possession from the kick out, but Smithson pulled them up and passed to Connor, who slipped at a critical moment and F. Williams nipping in put Foster in p.ssession, who raced away and finally scored with a good shot. Warrington started strong from the centre and Leek Mt wing had a 1 turn of attack, hut were nulled tip in time, then a com- bined movement by the Warrineton forwards ended by I Jewkes scorin,: a beauty. Even play followed, T. Mather I being in possession when half time was called with the 1 score one goal 'mob. Owing to the unfavourable climatic conditions the interval was curtailed. Warrington went down with great dash evidently bent on a major point, hut Jones and Aicken defended well ; and incursion in Warrington territory was then made, and Wilson had to throw away a rather tame shot. Simond then had an opening, and the hall eventually reaching T. Mather, that player scored number twrs with a beauty. The pressure was still maintained, Norman passing well to Selby, the sloppy state of the ground telling against the final effort. Leek right wing then got off, but Mather sent them to the right about, and the Warrington for- wards were quickly in front of Jowett once more, Nor- man suffering a palpable trip when abont to shoot. Then Smithson put in a dropping shot, which was cleared. Jewkes meeting the hall next had a try, but without avail. Leek left wing now broke away, Marshall send- in. them hack with a big kick ; hnwever,they were soon - hack a.ain,this time with better luch,for Marshall miss- ing his kick, lot in Owen, Wilson having no chance with his shot. Each side now worked hard for a winning point and the play alternated between the two g^als, until the right wing pair overcoming all opposition, scored what proved to tie the winning point, time being called soon afterwards with the score : Leek, three ; Warring- ton, twn. WARRINGTON OLYMPIC V. STOCKTON HEATH RESERVE. This match was played at Mill-lane on Saturday. Wray started for the Heathens, who immediately attacked, Grifli,hs putting the ball through from a pass by Risley. Give and take play followed, the visitors haying slightly the hest of the play. The linmesters now came away.but Fi'zgerald was safe. Nothing else Wan scored tip to half time,when the visitors led by one goal to nil. On resuming Stockton Reath came away. Risley put the finishing touch to a smart passing movement. This reverse .e,.med to rouse the Olympics iin,i3olton scoring. This concluded the scoring, Stockton Heath running *Ut winners by two goals one. STOCKTON HEATH V. OCORTEINGTON PARK.—Played at Stockton Heath. The .7i.sitors winning the toss, started with the wind at their backs. Oughtrin.ton were early on the attack, a smart piece of passing ending in a goal being scored after five minutes' play. Oughtrington broke away and again scored, an appeal for off-side not</p>
        <p>being upheld. Each side in turn attacked, but the de- fence was sound, and half-time arrived with the score, two goals to nil in favour of Oughtrington. In the second half Stockton Heath were soon attacking the visitors being compelled to concede a corner, which was easily cleared. Not to be denied, the howesters again came away, Hallows scoring. Towards the finish the homesters made strenuous efforts to draw level, but could not break through the defence of the Park backs. The game ended in a win for Oughtrington by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>PENKISTE SCHOOL V. OLD SCHOLAR.9.--Chis match was played on Saturday. Winning the tess, the past started downhill and things looked bad for the school at first. the goalkeeper being called upon to save twice in quick succession. However the ball was at last cleared, and play transferred to the other end, a scrimmage between the posts resulting in a goal from the foot of R. Lowe. This seemed to put vigour into the Old Boys, and a long kick from Coates was followed down by Hills, who, passing the school backs, got a smart shot through the school posts, thus equalizing the score. A place kick for the school was well headed into goal by Lloyd. From the right wing Reynolds scored the second point for the school, who thus led by one goal at half-time. After the interval the old scholars seemed to be played out, and the school pressed all the time, Reynolds scoring three goals in quick succession, being ably assisted by the wing men, who kept the ball well in front of the posts. Harlow from the right rushed the ball through, scoring the sixth and last point for the sehool, who were thus left victors by six goals to one.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The ine.ting to make the draw for the district cup of the above association was held at the Roebuck Hotel, Bridge-street, Warrington, on Monday evening, Mr. John Lswis, in the chair. Before the ordinary business was begun Mr. W. R. Hannah, hon. treasurer, presented to Mr. Jlim Lewis (on behalf of the joint executives of 1894-5-6) a Aver mounted silk umbrella as a token cf their respect and good wishes on the occasion of his I marriage. Mr. Lewis briefly replied. The Secretary read correspondence re Witton Albion Football Club,who claim-.d to be within the ten miles radius. After hearing the evid-nce produced it was unanimously deciled that they te• admitted as members of the association. The following. clubs were affiliated—Lowton Rovers,Havdock Wesleyans, Warrington White Star, and Warrington Oly- mpic. The draw for the first round of the cup competi- tion resulted as follows :—_</p>
        <p>Warrington Crescent v. Haydock Wesleyans ; referee, Mr'. T. Worra 11, Sutton Combination.</p>
        <p>Firftworth v. Warrington ; Mr. T. Mair, Anderton Rovvrs.</p>
        <p>Sutton V. Newton-le-Willows ; Mr. T. Chidlaw, War rington. Orford Barracks v. Great Budworth ; Mr. H. Mercer, Farnworth. St. Helenq Landon and North Western Recreation v. The!wall ; Mr. F. Scarisbrick, Warrington St Elphin's. Stockt rn Reath v. Bank-street Mission ; Mr. E. Mills, Baroton Albion. . _ _</p>
        <p>Lowton Rovers V. Sutton Combination ; Mr. J. E. Robinson, Rainhill. Runcorn v. Penketh ; Mr. R. Catterall, Barnton Rover 3.</p>
        <p>Wittr n Albion V. Barnton Albion; Mr. J. Lewis, Pen- keit</p>
        <p>Vulcan Foundry v. Parr Victoria; Mr. James Mather, Warrington. Warrington St. Mary's v. Anderton Rovers; Sergeant W. Healey, Orford Barracks. and Burtonwood have byes. and Barntou Rnvera, Parr.and Warrington St. Elphin's (semi-fin/mists, 1894-5) are exempted from the first round of tne com- petition. A vote of thanks to the chairman concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT SITEP. HERDS, WARRINGTON DISTRICT.</head>
        <p>LOYAL ORDER OF ANCIENT SITEP. HERDS, WARRINGTON DISTRICT.</p>
        <p>The (via rterly meeting of this district was held at the Shepherds-by-Night Lodge, Coffe, Tavern,Lymm on Sat- urday. Owing to the illness of Bro. T. C. Duckworth, the Prov. C.S, of the district, the chair was )ccupied by the deputy G.C.S., Bro. W. Eckersley, who was sup- ported tor Bro. L. Weir, P.P.O S.ein the vice-chair, Prov. C. S. Bro. P. Edgar, and 20 delegates from the various lodges forming such district. After the quarterly ac- counts had been read and passed,and the other ordinary; business such as election of auditor, passing new menu. hers, &amp;c , had been disposed of satisfactorily, a short discussion took place upon various matters referred to in the A.M.C. report of the Order. In opening the dis- cussian, the officers thought under the circumstances that it would be advisable not to fully discuss the ques- tions, as the time at their disposal that afternoon ' through meeting so late was but limited, but that there was one important item whioh was mentioned at the last me( ting that should be contr tdicted, viz , that the Order was doclining. Bro. L. Weir, in touching upon this matter, said that in looking through the repot., re- port referred to he was pleased to say that the numerical strength had inc,eased by 1,685, thus making a total membership of 136.711. He also found that the funds of the Order had likewise increased by £33,000.and that the pre ent capital of the Order was nearly £444,000. Fle only mentioned this because he considered it desir- able that the statement previously made 'Should be re- futed. and hoped the delegates assembled would take the opportunity of contradicting such report when hearing it made outside.</p>
        <p>After remarks from Bro. HALEY and Bro. KIRKHAM, the matter was adjourned. At the conclusion of the meeting Bro. Hralty Wall called upon by the Chairman ta perform a pleasing duty. Bro. JAMES GRESTY said he could hardly find words to express the high opinion he had of Bro. William Gleaye's character. His conduct while acting as chief had been most praiseworthy. On behalf of the district he had gvat pleasure in presenting to him a handsome time- piece, and hoped that he and his wife would long live to enjoy the pleasure of looking upon the same. (Ap- plßuse.) Bro. WEIR said he had not had the pleasure of know- ing Bro. Oleave very long, but since he had known him he had been a most zealous and painstaking member, always ready to beln both monetarily and otherwise any cause for the benefit of his lodge and district. He trusted that their actions that afternoon would not weaken the friendship that had existed between them for so long a time, hut would cement it with a stronger bond of union. (Applause.) The timepiece would remind him.if at any time his mind was driftine, from the cause of Shepherd. dry, that if all others frowned upon him, he could &amp;wave find trite fellowship in the society.</p>
        <p>After Brno. Box, Kirkham, Whitlow, Dunn, Haley, Lowe, Aatall. Millar, Harris and Taylor had spoken.flro. Gave, on risine.torespincl, was very warmly received. He said in the first place he thanked them one and all for the kind remarks that had her.Pri made about him and aiso for the handsome, very handsome timepiece that had been nre.enterl to him. He could assure them that it would be highly prized and valued both by himself, his wife, and family. The time that he had spent amongst them and the various lodges had been one of the happiest of his Pro. (Applause.) The meeting terminated with the usual votes of thanks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>BAPTIST UNION.</head>
        <p>THE BAPTIST UNION.</p>
        <p>AUTUMNAL ASSEMBLY.</p>
        <p>The Autumnal Assembly of the Baptist Union of Great Britain and Ireland is being held this rear atPortsmouth. The inaugural meeting. in suppnrt of home mbsinns and the church extension movement, took place on Monday night. Close upon a thousand ministers sod delegates accepted invitations to take part in the proceedings of the Assembly. The second day of the Assembly was devoted to the subject of foreign missions. In the morning a meeting was Irdel at Sniithsea, when a statement Was presented setting forth the urgent need for increased subscriptions. It showed that the income was less than the expenditure by nearly ten thousand n °undo. and that while there were nearly 350.000 church members the present appro- ximate number of snlasoribers of ten shillings and upwards was less than eight thousand. For every pound subscribed the proportion spent in support of the mis- sionaries and their work was 17.. 911., and the expenses incurred in the collection of funds and administration amounted to only 24. 21-1. Addresses wero delivered by two young mission ries who are going out to India for the first time, and by eieht missionaries who are shout to return to their respective fields of Moor after fur- bmghs. The Rey. C. Williams (Accrington) delivered the valedictory address. A public missionary meeting was hold in the evening. On Wednesday. the President (the Rev. J. G Green- nu 7h, of Leicester), who took the subject of his address " A Free Churchman's Thoughts about the Church,' dealt with the recent discussions regarding the reunion of Chri.tendrun, and said that it was not by the deface- ment of denominational lines that Christian union could hest be attained, hut by the brotherhood of faithful hearts amid diversity of forms and administrations. At the spring Assembly of the Union it had been resolved to prepare an address to the British people in reply to the Pope's letter respecting Christian reunion. The ad- dress, which was now brought up by the Rev. TA'. Tymtns,vico-nresident of the Union, dealt, among other things, with the Papal assertion of authority, and said that until such pretensions had been abandoned near- tures from Rome could only betray a pitiable feature to understand the minds of moat Englishmen to-day. After prolonged debate and several division' s it was decided that the address should be referred to the officers of the Union. with instructions to them to recast it so that it might be issued in the form of an open letter Cr the rope. At a public meeting in the evening the educa- tion question was discussed.</p>
        <p>las Jamea Prom], an ex-member of the Cheshire County Council, pleaded guilty on Wednesday at the County Magistrates' Court, Liverpool to a charge of breaking and entering a dwelling house at Waterloo and stealing goods valued at £l3O. Although medical and other evid- ence was given to Phow that his mental condition was such to to render him irresponsible for him antinna. the magis•raten sentenced him te three months' imprison- ment with hard labour.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>RACE QUESTION IN AMERICA,</head>
        <p>THE RACE QUESTION IN AMERICA,</p>
        <p>The South Carolina Constitutional Convention has for the moment turned aside from the discussion of negro suffrage to consider a question equally vital and sensa- tional. At Friday's sitting, Section 34 of a proposed legislative ordinance was considered. It ran thus : "The marriage of a white person with a negro or mu- latto,or a person with one-eighth or more of negro blood, shall be unlawful and void." Robert Smalls,a coloured delegate, ironically proposed as an amendment that any white living or cohabiting with a negro or mulatto should be disqualified from holding public office, and that the offspring of such cohabitation should bear the name of the father and be entitled to inherit and acquire pro- perty. The uproarious debate which followed an amend- ment evidently framed as a rebuke to the notorious Laxity of morals in the State soon emptied the ladies' gallery. Mr. Smalls provoked an outburst of approving laughter by declaring that if lynching were applied as freely to whites who insulted and outraged negresses as as to negroes who assaulted white women, he feared the Convention would have to adjourn sine die for lack of a quorum. The original motion, slightly modified, pro- hibiting intermarriages between the rival races was then passed, and the convention adjourned for ten days to allow the farming delegates to gather in their crops.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>ANNIVERSARY OF RR. C. S. PARNELL'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY OF RR. C. S. PARNELL'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>Sunday being the fourth anniversary of the death of Charles S. Parnell, a great popular demonstration was held in Dublin, this tarring the form of a procession to Glasnevin Cemetery. Sympathizers arrived from all parts of the country, and ivytree leaves were generally noticeable, a large supply being brought from Avondale, Wicklow, by Mr. John Howard Parnell, M.P., for distri- bution. The proportions of this annual demonstration have not been greater since the funeral of the Irish leader, as it extended for two miles in a compact mass, there being delegates, from a vary large number of political and industrial bodies both in Dublin and in the country. The Lord Mayor, the Mayor elect, the High Sheriff, and most of tho members of the Corporation were present in state, while the members cf Parliament who attended included Messrs. John and William Red- mond, T. Harrington, W. Field, James, O'Kelly, J. H. Parnell, L. Hayden, J. Clancy, P. O'Brien and Dr. Kenny. The Irish National League of Great Britain and the Belfast Amnestry Association were also represented, and ameng the many wreaths was one from Mrs. Parnell.</p>
        <p>At Glasnevin Cemetery wreaths were deposited on and near the tomb,which was guarde by specially appointed stewards, in order to prevent too great a crush as the processionists passed slowly round the grave. In the course of the day the cemetery was visited by thousands of citizens and people who had come from the country but did not take an active part in the regular demonstra- tion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LEGACIES TO LIVERPOOL INSTITUTIONS.</head>
        <p>LEGACIES TO LIVERPOOL INSTITU- TIONS.</p>
        <p>The will has been proved of William Wolfall, Carp merchant, of 20, Fen wick-street, Liverpool, and of New Brighton, Cheshire, who died on the 18th August, 1895. Me gross personality is sworn at £19,066. The deceased gentleman bequeaths the, following legacies :—Church Missionary Soc.ety, London, £l,OOO ; Royal Infirmary, Liverpool, £2,000 ; Liverpool Scripture Readers' Society, £2,000 ; Blue Coat Hospital, £500; Seamen's Orphan Institution, £5OO ; Northern Hospital, Liverpool, £5OO ; Royal Southern Hospital, Liverpool, £5OO ; Mersey Mission to Seamen, £5OO ; Liverpool Town Missions to Seamen, £5OO ; Church Pastoral Aid Society, £2,000, to be devoted to the aid of churchs, or to assist any build- ing of such in needy parishes in Liverpool ; Liverpool Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, £500; Liverpool Stanley Hospital, £200; Liverpool Sheltering Home for Destitute Children, £300; Liverpool merchants' Guild, £200; Royal Society for the Prevention of Cruelty t) Animals, £2OO ; Liverpool Old Church School, £2OO ; Wallasey Cottage Hospital : £lOO ; Wallasey Dispensary, £00; Liverpool Orphan Boys' Asylum, £l5O ; Liverpool Female Orphan Asylum, £150; Liverpool Hahnemann Hospital and Flomeeopathic Die- pensary,£2oo; Liverpool Children's Friend Society,£2oo; Liverpool Hospital for Consumption and Diseases of the Chest,£loo ; Liverpool Medical Missionary Society,£loo; Liverpool Dispensaries, £2OO ; Liverpool Female Peni- tentiary, £lOO ; Liverpool Midnight Mission, £lOO ; Liverpool Dental Hospital, £lOO ; Liverpool Hospital for Cancer and Skin Diseases.£loo ; Eye and Ear Infirmary, Mrytle-street,Liverpool,£loo; Liverpool Deaf and Dumb Benevolent Society, £lOO ; Liverpool Infirmary for Children, £lOO ; Liverpool Police Court Poor-box, £25; and Liverpool Corn Trade and Guild, the deceased's share in the Liverpool Corn Exchange.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WILL OF BISHOP FRASER'S</head>
        <p>THE WILL OF BISHOP FRASER'S</p>
        <p>WIDOW.</p>
        <p>Estate duty has just been paid on £221,883 Os. sd. as the net value of thepersonal estate of Mrs. Fraser,widow of Dr. James Fraser, second Bishop of Manchester. The executors to the will, which has been proved within the last two or three days in London, are her cousin, Mr. EL T. Welch, of Leek Hall, Kirby Lonsdale ; Mr. R. C. Christie, of Ribsden, Bagshot ; and Mr. E. P. Charle- wood, of 51, South King-street, Manchester, to the two last-named of whom, for the executorship the testatrix bequeaths £l.OOO each, and to Mr. Christie a Bible printed in 1537. After bequests to members of her own and her husband's family, the testatrix bequeaths the portrait of her late husband by Millais to the Corpora- tion of the city of Manchester, and fifteen volumes of sermon noes and newspaper cuttings relating to the diocese to the Cheethatn Library. Two silver claret jugs presented to the Bishop and herself and eight silver trowels are bequeathed to the present Bishop of Man- chester. Mrs. Fraser bequeaths to Archdeacon Anson, Archdeacon Rawstorne, Archdeacon Hornbv and Arch- deacon Norris, £lOO each for the purchase of a souvenir of the late Bishop and herself ; to the Bev. W. Pedder and the Rev. E. Ptgot £2.00 each; to Dean Maclure, Canons Woodhouse, Powell and Kelly, the Rev. H. B. Hawkins, the Rev. J. Rawdon, the Rev. R. Tonge, the Rev. R. Birley, and the Rev J. Lloyd £lOO each, and 22 ladias, wives or widows of clergy of the diocese, £lOO each. Besides liberal bequests to servants Mrs. Fraser left a large number of legacies to charitable philanthropic, and other institutions, amounting in all, to about £39,000 She bequeaths to the Provost and Fellows of Oriel College,oxford44,ooo to endows Fraser Scholarship in memory of her late hushand,and to Owens Cellege, Manchester, £4,000, to endow a Professor or Lecturer upon Ecclesiastical History to be called "Bishop Fraser's Professot Lecturer." All the residue of her pure 1 personalty which can legally be applied for charitable 1 purposes the testatrix bequeaths to Bishop Moorhouse, of Manchester, Dean Maclure, of Manchester, Arch- deacon Wilson, Sir William Henry Houldsworth, Canon Tonge. and Richard Copley Christie in trust to pay and apply the annual income.—ln increasing the endowment of poor benefices ; in augmenting the stipends of poor incumbents or curates ; in providing a superannuation fund for,and pensions or gratuities to, aged or otherwise incapacitated incumbents or curates ; in giving tempor- ary assistance to deserving and necessitous clergy dis- abled by sickness or overwork ; and in aiding poor and necessitous parishes or districts le the diocese of Man- chester. The residue of her estate, not otherwise dis- posed of, she leaves to her cousin, Mr. Henry Thomas Welch.</p>
        <p>Chin Sac Mon, one of the wealthiest Chinese mer- chants in Chicago, is going back to China. Elegantly equipped special cars, steamer apartments richly fitted up, and ovations from his countrymen all along the line are to be the features of his victorious return to China.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON ST. MARY'S V. LONDON AND NORTH WESTERN Locos.—The above teams met on the ground Of the latter, St. Mary's won the toss, and Mr. Marshall, of the Loco department, kicked off for the home teams. The visitors pressed at the start, but could not score,the barks failinz to hold the greasy ball. Play in the second half was of a scrambling nature. The game ended in a draw, nothing being scored.</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN DISTRICT NURSE.</p>
