      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT ASSOCIA- TION OF CHRISTIAN ENDEAVOUR SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>As will be seen from our advestising columns the autumnal convention of the above will be held in Emmanuel Church, Bold-st., Warrington, on Saturday October 12th. We have been favoured with a , copy of the programme,which is an excellent one. There will be a conference in the afternoon preceded by a devotional meeting,conducted by the Rev. C. Andrew,of Latchford. The chairman of the conference is the Rev. Edward T. Reed, President of the Warrington and District Association. The subjects to be brought forward are,"Christian Endeavour and the Children," "Christian Endeavour and Evangelistic Work," "Christian Endea- vour and Foreign Missions." The speakers will be Mr. Henry Hutchinson.organisine Secretary of the Children's Special Service Mission ; Mr. John Crumblehulme, of Bolton ; Mrs.Higgs, President of the Oldhan and District Association ; and the Rev. James Mursell, of Derby. In the evening, the chair will be taken by the Rev. W. H. Towers,the newly appointed President of the Manchester and District Union. The Rev. James Mursell, who was at the recent great International Convention ofChristian Endeavour Societies in Boston, U.S.A., has kindly con- sented to give an address on " American Christian Endeavnur as seen at Boston, 1895." Mr.W.H. Hope, of Liverpool, will deal with the subject of " Christian Endeavour and good citizenship," and Mr. Towers will give a concluding address on "The Holy Spirit in our work," and will conduct the consecration meeting. During the past year there has been a wonderful growth all over the world and in this wave of progress the Warrington and District Association has shared Delegates are expected from many places surrounding Warrington, and it is hoped that the Convention will give a great impetus to the work at the beginning of the winter activities. The Secretary of the district is Mr. Arthur Mounfield. The Young People's Society of Chris- tian Endeavour is, it may be stated, quite inter-denom- national and may readily be adopted by any evangeli- cal church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>RE OPENING OF OHMY'S CIRCUS.</head>
        <p>RE OPENING OF OHMY'S CIRCUS.</p>
        <p>Since the re-opening of this circus on Saturday week, large audiences have been present at each per- ormance. During Mr. Oh my's absence in the Isle of Man, the interior of the building has been re-decor- ated, and it now presents a most attractive appearance. The initial perform anceson Saturday were a great success. Addressing the crowded audience in the evening, Mr. Ohmy expressed the pleasure it afforded him to come again amongst his old friends in Warrington, and to see that they in- tended to rally round him. He had made many friends in the Isle of Man, by whom his departure was much regretted, and in proof of that he informed the audience that he had no fewer than 30 telegrams wishing him success at his opening performances at Warrington. This was, of course, very gratifying to him. He was perfectly satisfied with the patronage of the people of Warrington on his first visit, and he hoped that during his second connection with the town he would be equally satisfied. He had been at the Isle of Man for six or seven seasons, but the last one had been the most successful of all. A capital programme has been provided nightly during the week. A great attraction has been the appearance of Mons. Chard and his troupe of dogs, who give a really clever performance. Erno on the wire rope, and Aguzzi as a jockey, contribute two very agreeable item s, whilst Miss Ada (Amy and Claude Ohmy, bid fair to become riders of repute. There are a host of other attrac- tions including, Alfrano and Alton on the flying rings; Miss Lizzie and her performing horse, Galatea; Mons Lefrong, Mr. Helding, the Clifton Brothers, Miss Nora, the Bros. Louie and Elba and the two Chinamen. Toney Felix is at the head of a troupe of good clowns. An excellent band is in at- tendance under the conductorship of Captain Met- calfe.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 28th September, 1895. During the week, which was the 39th of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths Warrington 2B 47 Latchford 4 0</p>
        <p>Total 32</p>
        <p>The excess of deaths over births was 15. The death rate from all causes was equal to 428 per 1,000, against 33-6 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 13.9 the corresponding week of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 24.5 per 1,000, against 17•5 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and against 1.8 the corresponding week of 1894. The ages of mortality were- - 1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 20 16 1 6 4 47</p>
        <p>The deaths included-13 from diarrlicea, 12 from measles, 2 from enteric fever, 1 from syphilis, 2 from cancer, 1 from pkthisis, 1 from diabetes, 5 from lung disease, 1 from brain: disease, 1 from gunshot wounds. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Szarlet fever, 2 cases. enteric 2 cases.</p>
        <p>Total number of patients in hospital on Sept. 28-41 METEOROLOGY.—The mean temperature was 62.7 Fah., and therefore 6 2 degrees higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 82 F., was recorded on the 25th inst., and the raimirnum temperature 40 F., on the 22nd inst.</p>
        <p>The Rainfall measured .02 inch. The general direc- tion was S.E., the reading ot the Anemometer denoting 625 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH FREEDOM.</p>
        <p>Max O'Rell has written a lively article in the " North American Review " on the tyrant y to which the people of the United States of America are subjected to at the hands of the so-called public servants—the negroes in the railway trains,and the managers of the hotel. "The Americans," he says, "like the French, have no initia- 1 tive and lack public spirit. The English are the only people who are served by their servants,let their servants be the Ministers of the Crown, the directors of public ' companies, or mere railway porters. To everyone to whom John Bull pays a salary he says, 'Please to re- member that you are the servant of the public.' When the English appoint a new official, high or low, it is a new servant th4t they add to their household. When the French and the Americans appoint a new official, it is a new master that they give to themselves to snub them and to bully them. For example,when the English rail- way companies started running sleeping cars the public said to them, • We don't wish to be herded up together like hop-pickers. You will please have the cars divided at night into two parts by a curtain, so that our ladies may be spared the annoyance of having to share a sec- tion with a man.' I do not know a single American lady who has not told me of that grievance,and how on that account she dreaded travelling alone. . . . I once heard a travelled and educated American say—' We talk a great deal about the freedom -four country, but it is England that has got the genuine article.'"</p>
        <p>The strike of Staffordshire chainmakers terminated on Monday. alter a month's duration, the masters conced- ing, an all round adv a nce of 10 per cent.</p>
        <p>The Boaed of Trade returns issued on Monday show that the imports hr September amounted to £30,618,854, against £30,240,461 for the corresponding •nonth last year, being an increase of £378,393. The esti-mt.. for September were £19,461,940,..0mpired With £17,599,320, showing an increase of £1,862,620. The imports for the nine months ended September 30 amounted to £303,975,839, against £304,638,140 in the corresponding period of last year, being a decrease of £662.301. The exports for the nine months were £188,820,437, com- pared with £181,482,571, being an increase of £3,157,868.</p>
      </div>
