      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>kettero to the Obitor.</head>
        <p>kettero to the Obitor.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE ChEESE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—! should be glad if you would give publicity to the fact that a Co-operative Society is in courss of formation for the exclusive sale of cheese and other pro- ducts of Cheshire farms. The low prices now ottainable for cheese make it imperative that the farmers should co-operate and thus secure to themselves the profits made by the middleman. Promise of support from large landowners has been given and it is to b hoped that the farmers will come forward and make the scheme a success. Co-operation is the only solution of the pr-sent situation. Stores will be opened in London where " Cheshire" is almost unknown and hardly ob- tainable at even the best shops, Manchester, Liverpool and other large towns. I should be pleased to hear from farmers and give them every information.—l am sir, your faithfully, 64, Stanley-street, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>C. C. BOWES.</p>
        <p>"MAKE-BELL I..VE AND SURFACE-WORKING." Sir,—One of the penalties of publicity is the liability to scathing criticism from anonymous correspondents. My note on Humbugs has not escaped this Storm of nameless animadversion. Some fair anonymous one— not of the new type I presume—asks, among other per- tinent questions,whether I do not consider myself as big a humbug as the rest in pretending to disbelieve the story of Adam and Eve, up )n which, she very cogently remarks the whole fabric of Christianity rests. And do I think, she adds, that " our dear curate" would so ' strenuously insist upon the entire truth of scripture nar- rative, if Eve were a myth and Adam a mistake ? Now this home-thrust brought me to my knees. Against the critical acumen and power of logical inference weilded by" our dear curate,"l have only the opinion and judg- ment of those better able than myself to decide 'so im- portant a question. Backed by these I should indeed be a" humbug " did I " pretend" to regard Eve as a myth and Adam as a mistake. These authorities aver that the account of this interesting couple, and the con- comitant talking serpent, was a pleasant fiction of the Chaldsens, the worshippers of Bel-Merolach and other fishy gods, long ages before the concoction of "our dear curate's " sacred scriptures. Again the acceptance of this fable as a fact is utterly inconsistent with the as certained facts of present day knowledge. But these facts are as nothing to prevail against the honeyed words of "our dear curate." When the wind sits in that *quarter .he zephyr of fiction will overcome a cyclone ot fact. Whether a person or event ever had an existence or no, matters less to the meek' followers of " our dear curate '' than the fact that it is the stock creed of " the set " of minor bodies to whom the Curate,or Bishop,or Presbyter as the case may be, forms the gravitating centre. However sternly adverse facts may be staring Us in the face, we are, one and all, with few exceptions, in- tently bent on" making believe "that we really do be- lieve these Chaldren "Chestnuts." In reviewing the religious light literature of the day one is struck by the ' frothy superficiality, its immaterial matter—neither it nor its readers ever by any char .:T. vatting down to the bed rock of fact. Sentimental sup. y'.,ia.kities are the stock-in-trade of this class of " Christian literature." What effect such pietistic patter and feeble inanities must have upon the minds of its readers may be judged from the fact that the mental, like the muscular powers are increased by exercise and atrophied by disuse. The mind like the body requires for the fullest develepment of its powers and capabilities, a train of constantly but gradually increasing difficult tasks, The habit of doing only what is easy and pleasant tends to degradation. And to maintain thisstate of things we have among us a class of, presumably, educated persons pledged to teach and support a dogma the basis of which is a pagan fable. Is it then to be wondered at that mock rn life should be shot through and through as it is with shams and "make believe ?" It is thus that we see society stained by in- tellectual dishonesty. Hence we see the "follower of 1 Jesus" who peregrinated painfully afoot, doing his fol- lowing leisurely on wheels with a sixpenny cigar in his mouth.—Yours Truly,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>JOHN PEERS.</head>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>HOME RULE AND HENRY GRATTAN.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The subject of this letter has always loomed but very large in the history of England and mere particu- larly of his own loved country " Old Ireland " ; too recent events and the present juncture have combineclto make him more conseicuous than ever. It is meet and just that we should discuss the merits of great men whu ereceded us and who have fought against oppression for zonscience's sake. The question of Irish Parliamentary independence has, for the first time since the Union, zome not only within the range of practical politics, but is the greatest problem in the body politic of this king- dom and empire on which, to use a popular phrase, the sun never sets, because it is a question that must be solved in a way satisfactory to the Irish people. It there- fore follows that one whose name is so inseparably con- nected with that question, as in the name of Grattan, should occupy a striking and prominent position before the public mind. It has therefore seemed to me that it would be interesting and also profitable at the present time to give a brief sketch of the life of this brilliant and remarkable Irishman. Like many °Vier Irish leaders, such as Lord Edward Fitz4erald, Father Mathew, the greatest temperance advocate and leader that Europe has produced, Daniel O'Connell, John Mitchell and Mr. Parnell, Grattan, came of a good family. His father was for many years recorder of and member for the city of Dublin, and on his mother's aide he was descended from an ancient family—the Marleys. His grandfather on that side was Chief Justice of Ireland, sr d one of his uncles was the Bishop of Waterford. Grattan was born on the 3rd July, 1746, al; Dublin, which has the honour of being also the birthplace of Burke and Sheridan. It was an age of great orators,and Grattan's birth placed hiin, in point of time, he about the centre of the group of Illustrieus men whose eloquence lit up these islands in the middle and latter part of the last century. Chatham was thirty-eight years and Burke sixteen years older than himself, the rest were his juniors, Erskine by two, Fox by three, Curran by four, Sheridan by five, the younger Pitt by thirteen, and Plunket by eighteen years. He was educated at two schools in Dublin and at Dublin College where he met several who afterwards took a prominent part in political life, including John Fitzgibbon, known afterwards as Lord Clare the renegade patriot and eloquent advocate of the Union, so destructive to Irish prosperity. In 1767. being then twenty-one years of age, Grattan went to London, and was entered in Michaelmas term as a student of the Middle Temple. During the three years he was reading at the Bar he spent a great deal of his leisure time in the House of Commons and at the bar of the House of Lords. The" Gilded Chamber" was then the scene of the final oratorical efforts of the Earl of Chatham. Age and disease were now beginning to tell upon the physical powers of the great orator, but his genius was still fresh and strong and in opposition to the coercive policy o' the Government in America, he continually delivered speeches of unrivalled eloquence. Many of these speeches Grattan was privileged to hear, and it was then he wrote his celebrated sketch ef the great states- man. This from its condensed and yet wonderfully rips- criptive and comprehensive character, its impressive diction and striking originality of thought and expression is an altogether unique piece of composition. It may be confidently asserted that there is no fragment of literature in th 3 English language which within the small compass deals so completely and powerfully with the subject. Thanking you in anticipation of yeur insertion of this letter in your next issue. —I am, sir, yours ms pectfully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>4,4•641 J. KILGALLON.</head>
        <p>4,4•641 J. KILGALLON.</p>
        <p>HALTON. PARISH COUNCIL MEETING. The Council met on Tuesday evening in the Girls' ;hoolroom. l'he Rev. G. D. Wray presided, and there were also present :—Messrs. Stokes, Looker, Birch, Hughes, feacock, Ackerley, and Woodier (clerk).—The Chairman explained that there was no necessity for the Council to appoint a trustee to the charities. St. Thomas' charity had been handed over to the Council, and the members thereof in future would be the trus- tees. This would, also affect several of the oth ,r chari- ties. The official trustees of the Charity Commissioners would pay the dividends to the Council.—Several mem- bers again referred to the dangerous and had condition of flanging Rock-road, and the Clerk was instructed to write to the District Council on the subj-ct. He was also directed to write to the agent of the Marquis of Cholmondeley with regard to the dangerous footpaths leading from Halton to Weston—lt was resolved to issue a precept on the overseers for £4O, to met the expenses of the Council.—The report of the Committee appointed to carefully consider the regulations of the cemetery was submitted. It showed that the regulations had been compared with those of Runcorn and Warring- ton. The only alteratiGn necessary to the existing rules was that a monument of ordinary size" should be con- sidered one over sft. Bin. and not exceeding Tft. It was also recommended that instead of the sexton having double fees for outsiders, he should have a fixed amount. It was ultimately agreed that the sexton's salary he ad- vanced from £5 to £7 per year. It was also decided to purchase a dozen suitable trees to be planted in the , cemetery.—This concluded the business.</p>
        <p>. zichooll</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>WESTON POINT.</head>
        <p>WESTON POINT.</p>
        <p>fDANACIE9 mu AN ASSAIMT.—Before the Registrar (Mr. Johffe) at Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday, Samuel Clarke, potatoe merchant, Frodsham, sued Peter Coyle, Weaver Hotel, Weston Point, for £l6 is. 10c1. dimages fsr an assault at the Howling Green flot^l, Chester. Mr. Linaker, solicitor, appeared on behalf of the plaintiff, and stated that it had been agreed that he should tak, judgment for £l5 including costs. The Registrar concurred.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY, OCTOBER 12, 1895.</p>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEY GUILD.</p>
        <p>The opening meeting of this newly formed guild was held in the Lecture Hall of the Wesleyan Cbapel on Wednesday evening. The proceedings consisted of a" Fruit Banquet," and there was a large number of members present. During the evening games of various descriptions were indulged in, interspersed with songs contributed by Mr. F. P. Clare, Miss Eckersley and others. Fruit.biscuits, tea and coffee were handed round, and the meeting which was of a pleasant character, closed with a vote of thanks to the ladies, proposed by Mr. J. H. Profit and seconded by Mr. P. Parr. . - PECULIAR ACCIDENT TO A BOY. _ _</p>
        <p>On Wednesday morning a boy Wancf-Gregory, living with his parents in Sefton-et., Newton Corn- mon, was tha victim of a peculiar accident. A short time ago the lad broke one of his arms, which was duly set, but owing to want of proper attention it did not come together properly,and it had to be re- set twice over. It is not yet altogether better, and on Wednesday morning his mother was stooping in front of the kitchen fire making toast. After completing the operation she stood up, and not knowing the lad was behind her she stumbled over him, and finally fell on him, breaking one of his thighs. Dr. Hayward, of Haydock,was sent for,and in order to give proper attention to the case be had the unfortunate youngster removed to the Haydock Hospital.</p>
        <p>SOSB at TEMPERANCE.</p>
        <p>The usual fortnightly meeting in connection with the Samuel Morley Section of Cadets was held in the Co-operative Hall, EarleAtown. Sister Ethel Pearson presided. After the ~pening the election of officers was proceeded with and resulted as fol- lows :—W.A. Bro. A. Smith, V.A. Sister 1.40111,d Bar- rett, W. Bro. Alfred Cronin, G. Bro. Alfred Porer Secretary, Alfred Judson, T.W.A. Sister Ethel Pear- son, after which the installation of the officers wr proceeded with. At 8 o'clock the Adult Division ' met in the same room. Bro. R. Yorke, W.A.., pre- sided. After the opening Bro. C. Broster was elected as W.P. in place of Bro. John Pickett re- signed. Those members were proposed and initiated. The Grand Division digest was discussed and the representatives were instructed as to how to vote. After other business the meeting closed.</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN (By ,AN OCCASIONAL CONTRIBUTOR).</p>
        <p>The District Council still keep up their reputa- tion, and appear determined to carry on their busi- ness, what little they do, in an antagonistic spirit. This is the outcome of introducing party questions on the Board, instead of amicably working for the general good of the township. In a place like Earlestown it is sheer folly to attempt to conduct the township affairs on party lines. It was never intended by the ratepayers that such should be the case, but so it is, and so it will remain until Earles- town asserts itself. "^v be soon.</p>
        <p>The first difference ou Tiionday night arose as usual over the Minutes. It appears after they had been read for confirmation the Chairman wanted them to be adopted before discussion, but Council- lors Emmett and Gilmour objected, and to his credit be it said, Councillor Smith supported them, which was quite sufficient for the remainder to pllow suit. The Chairman to my mind is a trifle arbitrary in matters of this kind, and is averse to the Earlestowu representatives taking the lead.</p>
        <p>The question of the Town Hall floor was broached and discussed. One would wonder at this early date what could possibly be wrong with the Town Hall floor, and the statement that it is in any way defective may prove a source of alarm to nervous people. Mr. Emmett wanted to kno s if dry rot was in the floor, and the Surveyor said it was not dry rot, but that the defect arose from damp in the joists,and that such steps had been taken as would, he thought,prevent it spreading. Councillor Smith said it was evidently due to insufficient ventilation ot the floor. This is serious enough, and the ques- tion of liability for this defect has arisen, but I doubt very much whether anyone can now be made liable.</p>
        <p>-- Wonders will never cease. The Council have de- cided to reduce the price of gas by a penny per thousand feet. It is now something like two years since the price of the gas was increased, brought about by the advanced price iu coal at the time of the late collier's strike. Coal has been reduced for some length of time, and although on several occa- sions, a proportionate redue:tion in gas has been asked for, it has not come until the near approach of the next election to the Council. Selfish people would say it was an election dodge, but this is not my opinion.</p>
        <p>On the question of technical instruction being brought up, Mr. Ormand presented a very exhaus- tivs statement, which required some explanation. Nobody inside or outside the Council is so fully ac- quainted with matters relating to technical instruc- tion, and it appears not only amusing, but a little out of place when Councillor Thompson attempted to instruct Mr. Ormand on Abe subject, but Mr Ormaud was as usual equal to the occasion.</p>
        <p>The Council have awakened to the fact that the pit at the top of Market-street is dangerous to the community, and have decided to request the owner of the adjoining land to fence it in. The minutes state that Messrs. R. Evans and Co. should be written to, but Mr. Gilmour who was sponsor for the minutes, denied that he mentioned the name of any person. It turns out that. the land belongs to Lord Newton, and it is to his Lordship that the communication should be addressed. At any rate, no matter who is the owner, it is time the pit was fenced in, as without doubt it is a standing danger, especially to children.</p>
        <p>The Council have erected a new lamp at the top of Market-street, on the opposite corner to the pit above referred to. Just at this spot, the corner by the pit is decidedly a dangerous one,and Councillor Gilmour called attention to the fact that the lamp was erected in the wrong place, pointing out that in the Case of conveyances driving towards it from the direction of Newton and intending to turn down Market-street, the light will make the corner still more dangerous by reason of its shining full in the face of the drivers. Councillor Smith saw the wi.dom of this and agreed to the lamp being trans- ferred to the dangerous corner.</p>
        <p>Some amusement was caused by Councillor Gil- moor alluding to the Appliances Committee. In a ' true vein of sarcasm he Faid be wished to resign from the Committee, as he did not care to be saddled with the responsibility attached to its membership. Be had, he said, been on the Committee since February, and not a single meeting had been held, and as the duties were too onerous he wanted to be relieved. It turns out that this is a Committee ap- pointed to see to the fire appliances, hut they have evidently not yet found time to give any attention to such an important matter. Perhaps this little ventilation of the subject will do some good.</p>
        <p>I 41Ia BOWLING. I LEGA CHALLENGE CUP COMPETITION. The final match for the above waa played at Newton on Saturday, when North Ashton beat Newton Nursery by 47 points. North Ashton again became the cup holders. Scores : NEWTON NITESF:RT. NORTH ASHTON. I McCormick (rapt) l2 d Parkinson I Bennett T Croston H Holeroft l6 1 Birchall 'Thomas Gorse, jun 2l W Weisby. jun l2 7 Cameron 2l T Adamson 2O I, Murphy 0 Sturges' 2l John Lowe l7 W McCormick d Chapman l3 W Welsby. sen 2l E Wyatt lO 13 Haseldin 2l F81. gess J I Randall I Burgess T GUI W Kent l4 W Shaw, jun 2l I 259 206 ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>EARLESTO WN ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTO WN ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF SALMON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday morning, before Mr. T. E. Withington, an elderly woman named Eliza Clarke, was charged with hav- ing stolen one tin of salmon, value 6.1.,the property 'of Mr. John Howe, of Oolborne, on the previous Saturday.—Evidence having been tendered by the prosecutor, the accused was remanded to the New- ton Petty Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>THROWN FROM A CART AT EARLBSTOWN.</head>
        <p>THROWN FROM A CART AT EARLBSTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Monday afternoon Thomas Stokes, living in Viaduct-st , Earlestown, received severe injuries through being thrown from a cart in which he was riding. He was standing in the cart in Viaduct-st. near his home, when the horse which was attached to it suddenly started, and Stokes being,t iken un- awares was thrown out. He sustained an injury to one leg, and incised wounds to the head and nose, besides severe shock, and after beit.g carried into the house was attended by Dr. Valentine, who dressed his injuries._</p>
        <p>ST. JOHN $ CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. The special services in connection with the har- vest thanksgiving held in the above church were brought to a close on Sunday, when.at the morning and evening services, the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. J. Barnes, M.A. (Vicar of Failsworth,Man- chester), who delivered instructive discourses to large congregations. In the afternoon a harvest can.ata "Crowning of the wheat," was given with excellent taste by the choir, the accompaniments being played by the Earlestown Orchestral Society. The collections during the whole of the services amounted t 3 £2B ss. 3d., and were devoted to ti 9 Curates' Stipend Fund._</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</head>
        <p>GOOD TEMPLAR&amp;</p>
        <p>The weem'7 session of the Hope of Earlestown Lodge of Good Templars was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall. Marlestown, on Monday evening. Bro. J. Hatton, C.T.,Dresided, and there was a large attend- ance of members. Several candidates were pro- posed for membership. Bro. Barker read the digest of business for No.l Convention to be held at St. Helens on Saturday. Several items of business was transacted, the lodge being afterwards declared open,when the attendance was greatly augmented. Bros. Hatton and Barksr were respc.nsible for the programme, and songs were given by Bros. J.Taylor and 11. Bridge,Sisters Willis and Whitty, recitations by Bros. G E. Warburton, W. Wilson and Sister Hopkins, dialogue by Bros. W.Wilson and G.Clarke, the latter also giving a Lancashire reading ; and a short address was given by Bro. H. Dale. Sister Blair accompanied and played a selection on the piano.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>NEWTON BOWLING CLUB.</head>
        <p>NEWTON BOWLING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual closing handicap of the season, which was commenced on Saturday September 28th, was concluded on Saturday last. The number of entries was 72, and the prizes ranged in value from 178. 6d. to 2, 61. The supporters of the club contributed very liberally to the prize list, which enabled each competitor to receive a prize. Owing to the ex- treme inclemency of the weather, the bowling on Saturday was carried on under a disadvantage, and good play was not to be expected. Mr. H. Williams I and Mr. George Sturgess were left in the final, and they decided to divide the first and second prize At the conclusion of the bowling the players ad- jiurned to the Pied Bull Hotel, where about 60 partook of an excellent tel provided by Mr. Dor- man. Councillor J. Smith should have presided over the gathering, but owing to indisposition be was unable to he present, and wrote expressing his regret that he ceold not be with them. Mr.Bodmer occupied the cuair, and after a song by Mr. J. Seddon, he presented Mr. W. H. Hindley on behalf of the club, with a clock in recognition of his 24 years valuable services to the club in the capacity of serTetary, treasurer, and captain. Mr. Hinrlley suitably replied, and a number of toasts were after- wards duly honoured. Mr. Bodmer and Councillor Simpson afterwards presented the prizes :-1 and 2 prizes, Messrs. Sturgess and Williams ; 3, J. Mc Cormiek ; 4, W. Fairclough ;5, D. Rylance ; 6, J. Cook ;7, R Smith; 8,, Hindley ;9, B. Corlett ; 10, T. Fairclough ; 11, H. Davies; 12, .J. Kean, &amp;c. Songs were given during the evening by Messrs. J. , Liptrott. W. Dwizenn F. ISrierley, W. Lane, &amp;c., Messrs. Waywell and J:Crouchley accompanying on I the planet. NEWTON VOLIINTERIt SHOOTING CLUB.</p>
        <p>The distribution of prizes in connection with the shooting club of the Newton detachment of the 6th V.8.K.L.R., took place on Saturday evening at the Hand and Banner hotel, Newton. A smoking con- cert was also held, and there was a very large at- tendance of volunteers, Col. Sergeant Houghton presided. Sergeant Major Hancock, the new Ser- geant Instructor, from the 2nd Wiltshire regiment was present. Sergeant Rimmer, secretary, read a statement of the ffnances, showing the turn over to have been £l5 94. The chairman afterwards pro- wards proceeded to distribute the prizes to the successful markapien, and addressed a few appro- priate remarks to each of the recipients, and allud- ing the success which had attended the club's first year of existence. The following is a list of the prize winners :—Class A, medals, Corperal J. Pullen,' Sergeant Rim mer, Private A. Walker. B. Series: 1, Private Persival ;2, Private F. Turner; 3, Private J. Pullen. A. Series : First day prizes, Corporal Pullen, Sergeant Wright, Sergeant Rimmer, Serge- ant McCormick, 154. each ; second day prizes, Cor- poral Bserdman, Sergeant Rimmer,Private Walker, Private Wallace, 104. each ; third day prizes, Pri- vate Gibson, Private N. Percival, Private W. Ellam, Sergeant Instructor Bird, 74. 61. each ; fourth day, Corporal Kent, Sergeant Roe, Sergeant Baker, Cor- poral Jones. 54. each. B. Series: First day prizes, Private J. Pullan. Private P. Percival, Private F. Turner, Private G. Gerrard. 104. each ; second day prize. Sergeant Appleton. Private Benison, Private Spencer. Private E. Williams, 75.61. each; third day Prizes, Private F. Wagg, Private J. Davies, Private J. Moran, Private F Pgan. 54. each; fourth day, Private A Mcflov. Private J. Kenyon, Private C. Naylor, Private D. Mcflardy,4s each. In the course of the distribution the Chairman mentioned the name of Sergeant Instructor Bird who was one of the prize winners, and testified in felicitous terms as to Sergeant Bird's excellence chaeacter, and ex- pressed a wish that together with his wife and family he would have a successful voyage to their new home in India. At the suggestion of one ' of the members present the health of Sergeant Bird was drunk with musical honours. Other toasts were submitted and honeured including "Our Officers." "The shooting club," and the usual loyal and patrio- tic toasts. The Chairman then introduced the new Sergeant Instructor to the members. Sergeant Hancock in responding said he would try to follow in the footsteps of his predscessor, whom he knew to be an excellent man, and he thanked them for the kind receptiln accorded him, specially men- tioning the name of Sergeant Rimmer, who had given him every assistance on his arrival among them. Songs, recitations, &amp;c., were given by Mr. George Wright,Bugle sergeant Biker,Private Brad- dock, Corporal Kent, Private Randall, Corporal Ger- main, Sergeant Rimmer, Sergeant R. Wright, .and others.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, special services in connection with the anniversary of the above chapel were held in ' that place of worship, the collections being in aid of the debt. Sermons were preached to large eon- , gregations by the Rev. J. M. Fairbourne, M.A., ' formerly resident minister at Earlestown. On ' Monday evening, the rev, gentleman gave a lecture in the chapel to a fair audience on " Hugh ' Latimer ; his life and times." The Rev. James Allatt presided and briefly alluded to the subject of the lecture. The Rev. J. M. Fairbourne said that . from the li,t of English reformers he had selected the name of the great Hugh Latimer. Latimer was not so great in point of learning perhaps as some of his contemporaries. Tie was not a man who wore soft clothing, and who shone in king's palaces. Nevertheless, he was a great man. He was a true ' Englishman, a hold, fearless, putspoken man, ready 'to discharge what he called his duty, at any cost An inspiring opponent of despotism, be was a true Christian. a burning and shining light. He was emphatically it man for the times. Hugh Latimer was a native of Thurkastow, a little village in the . county of Leicester. The lecturer then detailed at some length the early life of Latimer, whose father , was a yeoman, and traced his career until he graduated as a B.D. He was an ardent papist, and very zoalous for the religion of his fathers. He was as bigoted a papist as any in alt England, and his attack on Stafford and his heretical teaching. , earned for him the position of cross-bearer in all public processions. But the time of his conversion was at hand, Thomas Bilnay being the instrument of the conversion. Latimer began te maintain from the Cambridge pulpit that the Bible ought to he read in the vulgar tongue. The lecturer then pur- sued Latimer s life onward dealing with his im- prisonment as a heretic, and finally with his execu- tion by burning at the stake, along with his colleague Ridley. 11,e lecture was most interest- ing and was attentively listened to.—At the close a vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer and chairman on the motion of Mr. Gooding,'seconded by Mr. J, Robert&amp;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>SWIMMING GALA, AT THE BATHS.</head>
        <p>SWIMMING GALA, AT THE BATHS.</p>
        <p>A swimming gala was held at the Warrington baths on Tuesday evening at which there was a large attendance, including members of ths Wigan Corporation. Cpuncillor R. Roberts, J.P., presided. The programme consisted of an open amateur handicap, flying squadron race between the church lads' brigade of Warrington and Wigan which was won by the latter an invitation race between J. F. Finegan (Sefton), P. D. Nevin (Liverpool), and T. Locker (Warrington). This race was won by Fine- gan, Locker coming in second. A close game was played between Warrington and St. Helens polo I tPatIV in which the latter again proved the victors. The first of the "Athletic News' cup competitions ' was also hit off, the finalists being Osborne and Mayilol,l, both of Manchester. This tie resulted in a win for Osborne by three goals to one. During an interval, the Chairman presented a silver medal to Mr. Joseph Cross for attempting to save the life of a suicide in the river in February, and whicn had been awarded to him by a paper called "Pluck." At the close the prizes were presented to the success- ful competitors by Hrs. R. Roberts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above Council was held at the Town Hall, Earlestown, on Monday afternoon, Mr. C. B. F. Barron, J.P., C.C., occupying the chair. There were also present Messrs. T. Ormand, R. Stone, J.Smith, P. MacMahon, J. Slee, W. Beatty, J. Thompson, jun., S. W. Emmett, W. Collingwood, J. Simpson, E.T.Evans, F. J. Wood; Rev. E. W. W. Kave, J. E. Woreley (law clerk), and R. Brierley (clerk and surveyor.) SHOULD TECO MINUTES HE READ OR NOT? When the minutes of the Highways Committee were reached, it was proposed as usual to take he minutes which had been printed an.] sent to the members, as read. Mr. Gilmour thereupon moved that they be read by the Clerk, and Mr. MacMahon seconded. They each contended that there was something wrung and per- sisted in their motion, despite the appeal of the Chair- man, and upon this, the Chairman said that the minutes had better be read, and thus, although the minutes of the committees were in print before the members, they were all read.</p>
        <p>HIGHWAYS, ETC., COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee which were published in our last issue were moved by Mr. Collingwood, seconded by Mr. Simpson.—Mr. Emmett asked what was the cause of the dry rot which had taken place in the Town Hall.—Mr. Thompson said that according to the standing orders notice should be given.— Mr. Smith differed from Mr. Thompson as he thought that any member of the Council could ask questions relative to the minutes before them.—Mr. Emmitt again repeated his question, and Mr. Brierley said that it was not dry rot, but was decay and appeared to be caused by damp and the boards had been creosoted and the damp scraped off. They had done all they could to prevent it spreading.—Mr. Smith asked who was responsible for the decay, the architect or the builder.—The Law Clerk said that he could not</p>
        <p>—.— tell.—Mr. Smith said that Mr. Brierley i.,formed the committee whea asked, that he thought that neither the builder nor the contractor were to blame, but appeared to be caused by some gallery or platform which had pre- vented the place being properly ventilated. He was of opinion that the question should certainly be settled as to who was responsible.—The Law Clerk said that he WAS informed that the builder had been paid, but that the architect had not yet been Sully paid. If when the ex- tent of the damage had been fully ascertained, Mr. Brierley would lay before these the facts as to the cause of the damage, he would advise the Council who would be liable.—Mr. Gilmour said that the pit in Market-street which was ordered to be boarded round, did not belong to Messrs. Richard Evans and Company, hut he understood it belonged to Lord Newton. It was therefore ridiculous to order Messrs. Evans to board it. The minutes were then passed. GAS COMMITTF.E.</p>
        <p>These minutes also appeared in our last issue, and their confirmation was proposed by Mr. Smith with the exception of the resolution relating to the tenders for tar which he would like to be referred back to the committee. With reference to the reduction in the price of gas, he hoped that the Council would confirm that. It was a step in the right direction, and the com- mittee thought that they could do it without interfering with the rates. He hoped that the working people would become larger consumers of gas, and cease to use the explosive oils and use the gas.—Mr.MacMahon seconded , the minutes.—Mr. Gilmour thought that the gas main I ought to be carried along the St. Helen's road. There was quite a colony in that direction and it would be a boon to the people. —Mr. Emmett said that the gas was very poor the previous night. They could scarcely see the minister in the pulpit, the light was so bad:—Mr. Evans moved that Commcn-road . . _ . . . .</p>
        <p>lie. added to the list of reads to be lighted with lamps.— Mr. Beatty seconded.—Mr. MacMahon thought that a lamp should be added in King-st.—Mr. Kaye supported the amendment. He said that there were 96 houses there</p>
        <p>which he w* sure would require gas.—Mr.Smith wished the amendment to be withdrawn in order to bring it before the committee. They had,he believed,gone even further than they ought. The committee were doing all they could.—Mr. Evans then withdrew,and the nsinutes of the committee were then passed. FINANCR COMMITTBE.</p>
        <p>The minutes of this committee were proposed by Mr. T. Ormand, and seconded by Mr. MacMahon, and passed.</p>
        <p>SCHOOL ATTENDANCE COMMITTEE. The minutes which appeared in our last issue were moved by Mr. Wood. They were of a controversial char- acter and had reference chiefly to the dispute between Father Lennon and the School Attendance Officer (Mr. Bill).—Mr.Gilmour:Why are Father Lennon's letters not published on the minutes? There were two letters read</p>
        <p>at one committee meeting mg on these minutes. The letter our Law Clerk sent is not here, and we only have the paltry dialogue of the Kenny's on the minutes. Why are the others lett cut 7—The Law Clerk : I presume that the letters were not on the minutes of the commit- tee, and therefore are not printed.—Mr. Gilmour : This dialogue of Kenny's at Haydock never was moved at the committee. I want to know why it is put in and the others left out. Mr. Smith : I think the answer to the question would be that it was put to Mr. MacMahon at the last meeting whether he would like to have the letters published. Mr. MacMahon distinctly said no.—Mr. MacMahon :Mr. MacMahon dis- tinctly asked for them to be published. I ask for them . . _ _ .</p>
        <p>to be put and read before the Board. There is a minute of our last monthly meeting to the effect that the letters which were sent back to the Committee through the in- strumentality of our chairman, who must have known what was in them, should be published on the minutes. I want to known why these letters are kept back. All the correspondence should have been before the Board. Father Lennon distinctly said that the statements of John Ball were false. The minutes say that John Ball's statements are correct: _I want the correspondence pub- . _ .</p>
        <p>- . lished. I also say that I was not there when Joha Ball was present at the Committee meeting. The chairman also was not there.—The Chairman: Speak for yourself. —Mr. MacMahon : I was not given an opportunity of being there. Mr. MacMahon also contended that the minutes were not correct with regard to the attendance of members at the Committee meeting. Mr. Borron, Mr. Ormand, Mr. Stone, Mr. Smith, and himself were all down as being present, and they were not therv.—Mr. Smith : I know I was present.—Mr. MacMahon again said that he was not, nor was the chairman.—The Chair- man : Speak for yourself.—Mr. MacMahon : But were you present.—The Chairman : I am not speaking now. (Lau7hter.)—Mr. Smith: I was there. I remember the discussion.—Mr. MacMahon : You were not.—Mr. Or- mand said that he was afraid they were confusing two dates of September 4, and September 16.—Mr. Gilmour said that be would be satisfied if the dialogue were left</p>
        <p>_ out. It would be unfair if that were left in and the letters of Father Lennon left out.—After a little more discussion, Mr. Smith moved that the whale sof the cor- respondence with regard to the dispute be supplied to the press.—Mr. Gilmour seconded.—This was ultimately agreed upon, and the minutes were then adopted. ANOTHER LETTER FROM FATHER LENNON.</p>
        <p>The following letter was read by the chairman : St. Mary's and St. John's School, Newton-le-Willows, Sep. 30th, 1895. To the members of the Newton District Council.— Gentlemen,—l am glad to he able to acknowledge the favour of a reply te my communications of May last and , Sept. 2nd. I regret, however, that I cannot congratulate your Board on either the accuracy or straightforwardness of Its contents. These seem to me only in keeping with your action in regard to both my letters,viz., disingenu- ousness and fear of publicity. (1). in your letter of the 7th, you state that the de- lay in replying to mine of May last "was unavoidable, owing to your earnest desire to carefully and thoroughly exhaust all the matter contained therein." A high authority, however, of the School Attendance Commit- tee informed me, a few weeks ago, that owing to the prolonged absence of the Chairman from home, my com- plaint as well as other school matters were left in abey- ance. As the two statements are not reconcilable, I shall be glad to know which is correct, both cannot be so True, three or four weeks ago, the Chairman of the At- tendance Committee, a gentleman of very, limited ex- perience in school matters, and still, less experience and knowledge of the characters and circumstances of the parents complained of, establishing a questionable pre- cedent, visited certain of the families given in my list of absentees—to verify, forsooth, or otherwise, charges of irregularity that had taken place six or seven months previously and now I am gravely informed, that after such investigation by the authority, I have named, ac- companied, of course,by an official whose sole endeavour in such inquiries, le to screen his own gross neglect of duty by attacking and distorting the complaint of managers and teachers, that "the Officer's report, was fully corroborated, and that he (the chairman) failed te find any inaccuracy whatsoever." Is this trifling with our intelligence, simplicity, or incapability (2). You will remember that I accused your officer of deliberate falsehood, intended to mislead your Commit- tee in regard to his statements, described by him so gra- phically and literally verbatim, about Mr. Henderson, your officer and myself. My charge of gross and inten- tional misstatement by Ball, is, I think more than corro- borated by the decision given you by the Education Department, and therefore, I should naturally have ex- pected that so serious a 'charge, deliberately made against an official would have claimed at your hands some investigation, st least. (3). The case of the Kenny's children. quoted in your letter, and the action of Ball, in morally, coercing the boy to repeat a falsehood of father, brother, toacher,and managers, is contemptible to a degree, and shows the opinion polar officer must entertain of the mental capa- city and legal acumen of the members of your Board. (4). I am at a loss to understand whether the con- clusion and appeal at the close of your letter is meant seriously, or otherwise. You speak of your "officer's " assistance to school managers in securing attention ("attendance," I presume is meant) of children at the various schools, of your deprecating any action which may tend to frustrate your efforts to promote the education of the children of the neighbourhood, and above all. "of the assurance that your Committee feel that I shall appreciate their desire and assist them in carrying out a work which as a public duty, they hive to perform." Gentlemen, I ask is this appeal a piece of deep, though refined, irony?</p>
        <p>or is it simply intended as a touch of delicate plea- santry ? For five and twenty years I have devoted the best part of my time,and no small portion of my income (I hope not without some success) to the support and spread of education in this seighbourhood, probab:y to the poorest and humblest class, and that without sym- pathy or assistance, nay, rather in spite of the opposi- tion of the Attendance Omamittee and their officer. The duty, however, has been reserved to the Newton District Cmncil of 1895 to implore me to assist,or at least not to frustrate them in the great work they have undertaken and which they have so closely at heart. This I will do, and as a first step to success I have instructed our teachers to held no further communication in any shape or form with your School Attendance c.,fficer, nor with your committee as long as it remains constituted as at present. This arrangement will relieve any overworked member of your Board of further trouble from "F. Lennon and his school," and will enable your Attendance Officer the better to work the other schools of the district, from the " Oddfellows " or any other "Arms," or from the arms of his own easy chair, where, without molestation or interferenc(t from managers,teachers, or CounciLhe may enjoy the emoluments of his past sinecure. The correspondence with your doard, I may remind you,was not of my seeking or originating,and must new close as far as lam concerned with this letter.—l am, gentlemen, yours truly, F. J. Lamson. Mr. Wood said that as there was a personal attack up- on himself he must offer a few words el explanation. It had been said that the School Attendance Officer had given incorrect reports, and he thought that it weuld only be right to try to discover whether that was true or not. fie had accordingly made inquiries himself into the cases, and he was satisfied that the School Attendance Officer had not been giving incorrect reports at all. The reports were , practically corroborated by the parents.—Mr. Thompson said that to save all further discussion he would move that Father Lennon's letter be acknowleged and that it be published along with the rest of the correspondence. • —Mr. MacMahon seconded, and the motion was carried , unanimously. ; This concluded the public business of importance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1895-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>"WANDERINGS AT WATERLOO."</head>
        <p>"WANDERINGS AT WATERLOO."</p>
        <p>LECTURE BY THE REV. ARTHUR MURSELL.</p>
        <p>The opening lecture in the series which have been preparPd by the committee of the Bold-st. Wesley Guild was held at the 13,1 d-st. Wesleyan schoolroom on Monday eveninv. There was a very fair attends nce,and the chair was cccupied by Mr. William Silcock,who was supported on the platform by the lecturer, the Rev. Arthur Mursell, whose subject was " Wanderings at Witerloo," the Rev. G. D. Mason and the Rev. W. G. Triggs.</p>
        <p>After a brief introduction by the Chairman, the lec- turer proceeded with his lecture. He said that the man's idess about himself were very much affected by the objects and circumstances which lie round about him. When he visited Paris in 1862, the year he went to Waterloo, he felt very small ind•,ed when traversing the various streets and boulevards, but at night when he thought of England's greatness and her position in the world, he invariably grew larger and larger, and he felt every inch a man. The rev. gentleman proceeded to give details of the sights he saw in Paris and also in Brussells. He then proceeded with his journey from Brussells to Waterloo. He left at about eleven o'cleck in the morning with an old foreign looking driver in the hex. When he (the speaker) asked him a question, the driver made such contortions in endeavouring to answer him that he relapsed into silence and passed he knew not what until he got on the field at Waterloo. Here he met an old sergeant who had been wounded at the battle, and who kept an hotel on the field and showed visitors the various sights. It was not the speaker's in- tention to give a detailed description of the fight, its strategical management or its political results. He intended to give a description of the inci- dents of his own personal visit to the spot a place where he had been two or three times since—and the thoughts it awoke, and the impressions produced at the time upen his mine. As he passed across the field he thought it was one of the greatest and most momentous dramas which had ever been enacted in the history of the world. They often spoke of decisive victories, but the term was often misapplied. A decided victory and a decisive victory were two very different things. Waterloo was a decisive battle. There were some vic- tories which simply affected the places where they were fought, but a decisive victory was one which turned the entire tide of history,which affected the social,and poli- tical destinies of many nations, and the battle of Waterloo was one of these. A writer had mentioned 15 battles which had they been decided differently would have affected the history of the world. They were Marathon, Syracuse, Arbela, Metaurus, Arlines, Chalon, Tours, Hastings, Orleans, Blenheim, defeat of Charles XII. by Peter the Great, Pultowa, Saratoga, Valmey, and Waterloo. Thus Waterloo was the last of those 15 battles. He (the speaker) as he walked on the field of Waterloo was rapidly growing into a full grown man. (Laughter.) He felt that that was British ground bought by the Mood of his own countrymen. The sergeant pointed out many points of interest on the field and took him to the hotel which contained a museum where could be seen the sword of General MacDonald, a pair of spurs belonging to Napo- leon, several of his kitchen utensils, a dragoon's &amp;oldie bag marked with blood, officers' accoutrements stripped from dead bodies on the field, the authentic autographs of Napoleon, Wellington, and Blucher, and copies of the " Times " containing an account of the battle. Re also purchased some bullets although he expected they had been imported from Birmingham. (Laughter.) Although he had as enlightened a horror of blood and war as the president of the Peace Society had, his bosom swelled as he thought of the glory which England won in Water- loo. The price of glory a; as a fearful price. But he rejoiced that their country's cause was a righteous one, and even the French might rejoice at that battle, for it was not fought against France but a blood thirsty tyrant. The battle was the means of ridding Europe of the ambition of the moat selfish tyrant that even mocked at human liberty. They fought not against France, but against the man who had deposed her law- ful monarch,duped her people, usurped her throne,whose very thought was self; who had waded through a de- luge of blood to the throne, and the stepping stones to whose imperial throne were the corpses of a million men; whose cruel sword was brandished in the teeth of truth, equity and law, and never once unsheathed in the cause of honour, or of right ; whose ears no widow's cry could pierce ; whose heart no suffering tale could trust, whose soul nor wrong or misery could arouse ; a man who had neither piety nor pity in his blood; who was ever seeking to quench his thirst for blood, whose wonderful genius bent all its power on the work of war; whose only science was that of strife, and whose only progress was the sweep of death (loud applause.) When they fought at Waterloo they fought for truth against falsehood, and for purity against vice. It was God's battle, for there was a God of battles even though he was the Prince of Peace, and that God stood by Wellington on the gory plains of Waterloo. The victory was over the most blood drunken despot that ever lived and it saved France from a tyrant's grip. Instead of the battle of Waterloo being , looked upon with shame, by France,it ought to be looked upon as a grand blessing to humanity, and the steeples of Paris had reason to unite with the bells of London in a merry peal on the anniversary cf the great event. (Loud applause.) Mr. Mursell then proceeded to des- cribe the various points of interest on the field, such as the mound with the Belgian lion with its tail between its legs, Hougoumont, La Haye Sainte, Sze. At the close a hearty vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer for his eloquent address, on the motion of the Rev. W. G. Triggs, seconded by the Rev. G. D. Mason, and the meeting was closed with the pronouncing of the benediction.</p>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday _morning, Elizabeth Makin, 60 years of age, wife of Isaac Makin, labourer, 37, Warburton-at.' expired at her residence under cir- cumstances of an unusual nature The night before deceased partook of a hearty supper consisting of potato pie, and about ten o'clock retired to rest. At 6.30 the next morning she awoke suffering from a severe pain in the stomach. Medical aid was at once summoned, but deceased expired before it ar- rived. The death was reported to the Coroner, but no inquest was considered necessary. Ifounittrotu LINP:ra, hemmed and marked Pre e of charge.— and W. Dutton. Sankey-,., Warrington.— Mat, BELICK-STERET MISSION BAND OP 110 PE SOCIETY. The first of the winter series of entertainments in connection with the above was given in the school- room on Tuesday evening before a large Eills. a appre- ciative audience. In a very interesting address the president, Mr. Samuel Gough, who presided, gave some earnest and sound advice to the Band of Hope children. The entertainment consisted of a lime- light exhibition illustrative of the life of St. Paul at Rome. The views and the connective readings en- lightened and gave valuable information to Bible I readers. A hearty vote of thanks was given to Mr. Wilkinson, who manipulated the lantern with his usual ability, brought an enjoyable meeting to a close. sxwuro mAcams. Haul £2 18s. 6d. Tee &amp;a £4 4a. with handsoms wjinut cover.—.l. and W. Durrox, IS and 18. Sankey-at.. Warrington—Advt.</p>
        <p>The annual return of the strength of the British army last year was issued from the War Office on Thursday. It shows that the average strength during the year was 219,121 of all ranks, a number higher than that of any preceding twenty years. The recruits enrolled during the year numbered 33,441, of whom 23.352 are stated to have been under twenty years of ag3. The militia num- bered 121,667, tho yeomanry cavalry 10,614, and the I volunteers 231,328. ' LAWN N 5 0 WHIZ&amp; - -GARLAND , Ironmonger, Warrington and liarlestown.</p>
